      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-10-17_WidnesExaminer_art0014" n="art0014">
        <head>IT IS 2 THE BEST. ALWAYS USE MC DOUG-MAUS PATENT SELF-RISING FLOUR</head>
        <p>IT IS 2 THE BEST. ALWAYS USE MC DOUG-MAUS PATENT SELF-RISING FLOUR</p>
        <p>PLEASANT PAIXGRA_PHS.</p>
        <p>A CORRESPONDENT asks—" Is a man what sells red cabbages a greengrocer?" A YOUNG girl's first love story always begins very sweetly and harmoniously with Chap. L "DID Mrs. Highlife's travelling gown fit well?" " Yes • but it cost so much she had to give up her trip and stay at home." .M.alutiAGE with a good woman is, a sage says, a harbour in the tempest of life; with a bad woman it is a tempest in a harbour. hull: "Why did you lose your temper so unfor- tunately at that game of cards?" It was the only thing left I had to lose." IF a man wants his wife to notice that a button is off his coat let him put a woman's hair where the</p>
        <p>button should be. - STRANGER: "What are the principal objects of interest in this town?" Citizen : " Savings</p>
        <p>bank deposits." : "Would you like to hear me sing 'ln the Sweet By-and-Bye,' Miss Mattie ?" She (sweetly); "Yes, Henry, but not before." "GIVE me the man who sings at his work," says some gushful writer. Do; give him to anybody who wants him. SHE "Yes, my little girl is a beautiful child. People say she took her beauty from me." He (gallantly) : "I can't say that I noticed your</p>
        <p>MAMMA: "Bessie, why don't you wash the dishes ? It is easier to do a thing than to sit and think about it." Bessie : " Well, mamma, you wash the dishes and I'll sit and think about it." GOVERNESS: "When I was your age I could answer any question in geography. Ethel: "But you forget that you had a different gover.</p>
        <p>flees." - CONJUROR "Now, my little man, are you quite sure there is nothing in your pocket?" Tommy: " Yes, sir—positive. The rabbit you put there bet ore the performance got away." MUTUALLY ENTERTAINED. Terwilliger: "I wonder why Jones and Smith are so fond of argu- ing with each other?" Peterson: "As near as I can find out each one thinks the other is a fool." THE STATUS. —Friend : "Isn't the baby afraid of the cat ?" Mamma: "Oh, no! The cat is atraid of the baby." MRS. NAGLEIGH : "1 suppose you are satisfied now that you made a mistake when you married me." Mr. Nagleigh : "I made the mistake all right, but I'm not satisfied." DIFFERENT.—" Before I was married the con- sciousness that she was thinking of me was very sweet." "And now?" "Now, she thinks for</p>
        <p>me." VISITOR (at goal, to prisoner) : "What brought you here?" Prisoner: 'Bad judgment." Visitor: "How was that?" Prisoner: "I was too fast for my employers and too slow for the officars." OCCUPATION FOR THEM.—" The millennium will be a hard time for the cynics, if there are any left." "Oh, I don't know. They can spend their time demonstrating that it can't last." THE ONLY WAY.—" Bridget, you have broken as much china this month as your wages amount to. Now, how can we prevent this occurring again ? " "I don't know, mum, unless you raise my wages." IRATE OLD GENTLEMAN: "Here, I say, your beast of a dog has bitten a piece out of my leg !" Dog Owner: "Oh, bother 1 And I wanted to bring him up a vegetarian I" CHILD, aged six, on seeing a fine engraving of the Assumption: "Mamma, I think little angels' parents must be very poor, for I notice they never have any clothes on.' NEPHEW (wbo takes his uncle from the country into a restaurant)—" Look, uncle, I press the button and order supper." Uncle—" Well, what then ?" Nephew—" Then you press that button, and pay." WHY rr WAS TOUGH.—" I say, mamma," said a yourgster who had been tussling with a very tough wing of "chicken," "I think that this fowl must have been hatched from a hard-boiled NEWPORT BELLE: "Oh, those naval officers are too sweet for anything. Did you ever lunch on a man-of-war?" Portsmouth Belle: "No; but I saw a young lieutenant to-day who looked good enough to eat." PHYSICIAN "The truth can no longer be hidden, madam. lam obliged to tell you that your little son is—er—weakminded. That is—well, it must be said—he is an idiot." Mrs. Highupp : "How fortunate it is that we are rich. No one will ever notice it." A BIG OPINION OF HIMSELF.—Atkins: "What do you think of Puffington ?" Grimshaw : "Oh, he isothe kind of man who thinks when he steps on one end of the country the other end bounds up into the air." SHE? "Oh, please, don't tease me to sing. I'm so hoarse to-night that I can scarcely make a sout.d." He : "yes, I know. That's why I think this would be a good time to have it over</p>
        <p>with." • "I THOUGHT, Miss S., that you hated that flirty minx; yet you went up and kissed her." "So I do hate her, and that is why I did it. Look at the big freckles on her chin where I kissed the powder off." MR. CRUSTY: "Confound it young man, it's after 12 o'clock. Have you no home to go to?" Mr. Pert: " No ; but we were just talking about making one. Funny coincidence that you should mention it." " WHAT you take your mother-in-law out shootin' ?" said a French sportsman to another sportsman. "Yes," was the reply, "with a five- frank gun that I presented to her; there's no knowing what may happen." RAISING THE RENT.—Landlord : Well, Awlford) glad to see the farm looking up so well • (jokingly: I shall have to raise your rent. Awlford (tenant). Glad to hear 'ee say so, sir, for 'tis more than Fee been able to do these three years. SEE: "You are very depressed. I didn't know you cared so much for your uncle." He: "I didn't, but I was the means of keeping him in an insane asylum the last year of his life, and now that he has left me all his money, I've got to prove that he was of sound mind." TEACHER: What is tsutology ?" Boy: "Repe- tition." Teacher: "Give me an example." Boy : "We are going to have sheep's-head for dinner, and my sister Elsie's young man is coming to dinner also." Teacher: "Go up top." " WAL, JIM, wot d'ye think abart the Czar o' Rooshia, and this 'ere universal disarmament?" Jim: "Well, it's summat like me and my ole woman, mate, when there's a bit of a shindy brewing. The one wot propoges peace is the one wot ain't got 'old o' the poker. "I An really delighted at the interest my boy Tommy is taking in his writing," said Mrs. Hickleby : "he spends two hours a day at it." " Really ?" How strange. How did you get him to do that ? " I told him to write me out a list of everything he wanted for his birthday, and he's till at it." VISITOR—"I am grieved to learn of your mis- tress's illness. Nothing serious—no great cause for alarm, I trust ? "TheNew French Maid—" No, monsieur, nozzing beeg, nozzing grande. Somez. lug—what you call letle, petite. What zay call ze leetle—small—smallpox." LUZ INSURANCE AGENT (filling Out application): "Your general health is good, is it not?" Appli- cant: "Never had a sick day in my life." Agent: "Urn I You do not contemplate entering upon any hazardous undertaking, I suppose?" Applicant: "Well, yes, lam afraid I do. lam going to gzt married next Wednerlay.o A BOY'S DAIRY. —A boy in town was given by his ma a very handsome diary. Here is a sample of one day's entry:—" Got up and washed me— bad breakfast—slid down hill—had dinner—slid down hill—had a fight with Willie Smith—we won't speak any more—wore a hole in the toe of my boot -steering my cart—ate supper—pa „spanked me about the boot—Went to bed—bully good day." JOHNNY and Tommy, who are the noisiest children in England, were playing. "Let's play on this doorstep," suggested Tommy. Jimmy said, "there isn't any fun in doing that. That's an empty house. Let's go down the street and play. I know where there's a newspaper man's house. He works all night and sleeps in the day- time." And the pair of cherubs started off to the spot where the maker of thoughts for thousands of ladies and gentlemen was trying to replenish hi 3 amntv brain-box by the balmy sleep nrocess. The Most NutritiouS.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-10-17_WidnesExaminer_art0015" n="art0015">
