        <p>EPPS'S</p>
        <p>Grateful—Comforting.</p>
        <p>clkictA</p>
        <p>Breakfast—Sup per</p>
        <p>ILLUSTRATED FASIIION S.</p>
        <p>The versatility of the feminine mind is almost unbounded, and though lately nothing but chiffons of the most diaphanous description have been discussed, the first chill of early winter consigns all these airy-fairy nothings to oblivion, and causes us to dwell with satis- faction on the possibilities of cloth costumes and fur. In regard to the latter, flat stole-like boas or (as they might more fitly be termed) pelerines with a cape deeply pointed at the back and stole ends extending as far as the knees are more fashionable than the rounded necklets of last winter. These are more in accordance with the early eighteenth century modes whose sloping shoulder lines and large hats of the picturesque order appear to exert a strong influence over and above all other styles. Furs are always amongst the earliest fashions to declare themselves : and it is always best to buy such at the beginning of the winter season</p>
        <p>9,5 tke best skins and largest selection is then to be obtained at a more moderate price than later on. The leaders of fashion have this year axpressed their approval of that most delightful, though somewhat expensive fnr—sealskin. This fur has somehow been put in the shade for some few seasons past by sable, caracule and one or two other varieties, but save and excepting sable there is no fur so becoming or so corn. fortable looking as sealskin. The cosy coat illustrated is of this fur made in the form of a Russian blouse with an embroidered belt. It is worn with a brown cloth skirt and a sealskin toque trimmed with a sable tail. Some of us have no doubt stored away an old sealskin coat which shows signs of wear and general usage ,which render it useless for present day wear, not to mention the fact that it is of a cut and design altogether out of date. There is however an opportunity now of bringing it—or the, best parts of it—into use. for bands of sealskin are considered very smart as a trimming for brown cloth costumes. It not a very difficult undertaking to unpicls the old-fashioned coat and ascertain how much of it can be used as a trimming. I knew of s girl who possessed a very good. but sadly fadee fawn coloured cloth coat and skirt, and an ole jacket of sealskin which had formed part of hei mother's trousseau. The brilliant inspiratior of having the former dyed a warm brown ane employing the best parts of the latter for s collar, revers and trimming in the form of bands laid flat and outlined with narrow gold and brown passementerie, put her in possession of a very fashionable and good look. ing costume whici, will wear all the winter and has set all her acquaintances puzzling as to how and why she can afford to dress so well. There is nothing like a little ingenuity for assisting</p>
        <p>ENOVATED TOILETTE. those of very limited dress allowance to keep pace with the modes without attempting to outrun them. I have given a sketch of the toilette, as it might prove of some assistance to my readers. A red costume after this style would also look very smart trimmed with black astrachan and some black braiding, worn with a black hat. I think it is always a good plan and, though it gives a little more trouble at the time, will be found to repay itself in the long run to unpick as much as possible of a dress before sending it to be dyed. If this is not done the change of colour shows at the seams after the dress has been worn once or twice for the machine stitching never takes the dye in the same way as the cloth. Th. seams should be unpicked carefully and aft morsels of thread removed. It is not very difficult to run up the seams together again on the machine with some new silk thread of axactly the same shade as the material has been dyed. At all events the hem at the edge of the skirt nimt be unpicked, as the skirt is sure to shrink slightly• in the dying process, and the necessary alterations will be very difficult to effect succe9sfully afterwards. The wrists of the sleeves should be treated in the same manner. This Toreador hat is quite the latest thing for early winter wear. It is somewhat larger than 'usual, and the upturned brim is trimmed with fringe, while two long silk scarves start from either side of the .brim, where they are fastened with ornamental pins and meet behind on the nape of the neck, where they are tied in a bow and ends. It is not given, of course, to every one to wear this style of hat with success, but there is a certain jauntiness about it which makes it very captivating, and in a more or less modified style is sure to have a certain following. The cache-peigne is certainly con- sidered very modish at present and is worn in a variety 4)f styles. Large fiat hats of the</p>
        <p>d - . ..;',",',e ,-4,‘,V - • v 4, ~ ------7-•-' , ito 7, ---.• ,i, 0 \ v , 1‘ ~ 1 7 \ , A COSY COAT.</p>
