      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-10-17_WidnesExaminer_art0016" n="art0016">
        <head>NEW TOREADOR HAT.</head>
        <p>A NEW TOREADOR HAT.</p>
        <p>picturesque order have a fall of black Chantilly lace at the back, sometimes so long as to come well down the back. A white Toreador hat looks well trimmed with some black chenille fringe and a cache-peigne of black velvet ribbon. the depending ends finished by some black beaded fringe. Many and very varied, however, are the shapes in millinery, so that theke should be no difficulty in every one finding soredthing to suit their taste: and as long as tho headgear worn is becoming to the wearer: the requirements of fashion will be satisfied, as originality is car- : the &amp;ewe of success at present, when not rried t• satrem es.</p>
        <p>COQII arm.</p>
        <p>I.i</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-10-17_WidnesExaminer_art0017" n="art0017">
        <head>WOMEN'S CHAT.</head>
        <p>WOMEN'S CHAT.</p>
        <p>Lady 'Warwick, and her half sister the Duchess of Sutherland, are perhaps better known to the public for the active interest they take in aocial questions. than are their husbands. Moreover both the Duchess and the Countess work with energy, and with an all absorb- ing idea to do good. The same coed intentisns might be attributed to all Society women who associate them- selves with works of charity or for the putlie good, but I not all put the same heart into the work. In all rack;, the class among whom a woman daily moves becomes a very dull ore, and there are many in the highest circles who mainly seek their own diversion in associating themselves with social matters, or charitable objects. Ent it is cot in that spirt that the Duchess of Sutherland and the Countess or Warwick take up such a prominent place ic social activities, and for which they are so widely known to the general public The Earl of Warwick, who presided at the distribu- tion of the Dairy Show prizes, is not, as is generally supposed, a descendant of the celebrated King-maker, but of William Greville, a wool reerchant. Nowadays there are any number of peers who owe their titles to their success in the cernmercial world, but many of the oldest peerages are held by desceneants of successful men of business. The founder of the family of which the Duke of Leede is the head, was a silk weaver named Edward Osborne, and the descendents of Hugh Smith- eon, an apothecary of London, hold the title of N erth- umberland. The slow combustion stove, where there is net a free escape for its poisonous vapours,causee many deaths in France, and the tragic ending of M Zela has drawn attention to its dangers. In this country moat of our houses are fitted with open grates and chimneys, but many people use gas stew s in their bedrooms, and the danger from them is just as great as from the slow combustion stove unlass there is fres ventilation. Ae this, however, allows the heat as well as the fumes to escape,the aperture is more frequently than not reduced to the smallest dimensions, and sometimes stopped up altogether. Doctors all agree that gas fires are injuri- ous in bedrooms, and if fires are a necessity it is best to put up with the dirt and inconvenience of the real fire in the open grate which provides both heat and ventilation. The (open bedroom window is one of the essentials to natural comeliness. The muddy unwholesome complex- ion is invariably the result of impure air, rich ciet, the want of cleanliness, and of exercise in the fresh air. These facts are much better appreciated by women than they were in years gone by, and they .contain the secret of preserving a clear complexion and youthful appear- ance. Rut though we know all this to be true. we have not all of us, the energy, or the" will power " to carry out all it means. There is a tendency to eat too much, whereas if we are te keep the clear skin and bright eyes of e outh, it is necessary to practice abstinence and to avoid absolutely all stimulants. Then we do not suffi- eiently reccgnise the necessity for taking a sharp walk every day, no matter what the weather may be. Yet it is enly by the strict observance of hygienic rules that we ear retain perfect health,which will do more for the complexion than all the cosmetics in the wend. I Bonnets have made their appearance again, real bonnets of the old fashioned kind, and the elderly woman who does not wish to appear a quarter of a cen- tury or so younger than she really is, will rejoice at the revival. Dainty little eomblnations these bonnets are, of silk and feathers, velvet and fur; and the strings are of velvet ribbon, from an inch to two inches in width. In the way if gowns and mantles else, the elderly woman is being catered for in a manner that must surprise and delight her, after the years of neglect</p>
        <p>that have fallen to her lot. The number of velvet evening frecks and tea gowns appears to increase every week, and the most delightful ball dresses are being built of a new fabric appropriately named velours mousseline. This is a delicious fabric, almost gauzy in its lightness. Then there is the new bind cf velvet, known as velours covcite, which possesses a singularly seft and silky surface, and looks exception- ally:well in the rich emerald green shades so fashionable at the moment. For showing off lace or guipure to its utmost advantage, no fabric, or colour, could be better chosen. For very smart occasions only are very decolletee bleusee donned. For theatre, restaurant, and dinner wear, necks are being wholly or partially covered with some transparent fabric. Sleeves to the elbow, or long trasteparent ones, appear in such bodciee, which,'by the way, are short at the back, long and pointed in front, and slightly pouched. The prencuneed pouched effect is no longer sought after by leading dressmakers. Dainty indeed are the little tea jackets,which as usual at this season of the year are finding a forem @et place in the dressmakers' and drapers' show rooms. Such a earment is a real comfort, and well within the range of the majority of women. A short remnant, a length of lace, end a little ribbon, can, in the hands of the merest amateur be speedily transformed into a tea coat. The implest style, and perhaps the most becoming, is a yoke, from which the material, either gathered or Pleated. falls to just below the waist. The sleeves should be of the wide bell variety. These little coats offer a wide scope to the artistic designer. the yoke may be of any fabric, but lace for choice, and the lining so arranged as to be removable, so making possible any number of change. Dr Parker of the City Temple has given an amusing Recount of bow he bought in Edinburgh a toy peram- bulator for some little friend, and was in consequence compelled by the railway company to pay for the carriage of "a baseinette " a larger sum than the artiele coat. A similar experience hss befallen a passenger wile travelled frem Paddington to Malvern, and wanted to carry a small band sewing machine, for which a charge of as 6d was levied. It certainly does appear a little hard that a seatntress for example, should be compelled to pay heavily for the carriage of a sewing maehine which weighs much less than the maxmiurn allowance of personal luggage. A phcrneraphee might just as well be charged for his camera, or a workman for his bag of tools. The only remedy seems to he to pnt the sewing rnaehine in a box, so that the railway company may not be able to tell what the article is.</p>
        <p>MADGE.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-10-17_WidnesExaminer_art0018" n="art0018">
        <head>EXAMINER FRIDAY OCTOBER 17 1902</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER FRIDAY OCTOBER 17 1902</p>
        <p>d, op OA Ireqs wqm „ •spurq awl uj Vagosna aacclod 4papo aqs „ pawed aeola Sato ‘aarpori 'IV,, %Soren paqoujd emus alp pus Slaputo 3° WU aveJ 'loadxo oq Tenn&amp; Sup&amp;ouN `Soio!nTa OUIVQ ato &amp;oUwg •tuoplas net wto ‘2IIMUIS qou paw&amp; Sam uaget ‘lsvaj we—sdn lag cope keet pau!upaqap o pug saSa aoljj pe&amp;aaqs Tope 11131110 A 2uptoopamolaojulooU wee&amp; ams •saatorj sip tope alods imp&amp; saopoaqi aaws BUM qi •a[ooq alma JOAall mamma alp `pauaddsq saulo -MOS so ‘uaqat aaq jo gal oqpoqJammu ~.uclaafn. °ID WV' "3 pus 'Liam 3jorq aureo Salo ualpe pves uoappqo ato.sqt aoj ere&amp; qj sdoload ao 'Hods qou pinoo pop polonps alp nano Roulet '9053 aaq Sjuoaq alp wee&amp; qj sclecoad •auo Saona aaq paeoj ',Cato qnq `OllOl5 Sjuo poq aqs uaapipio 31o1s alalp eas ()I 011150 011 M 8.1910 VJ 9q JOJ UOAR•V StAk low; qnq 'saowadsuj quaumaaeop ato pwe an9u2l9suow ora paqaodaa alp avak S DDIIO pwe ‘saspaalca 0n012119a "Rio pavan') oqm llnsar 2unoS ato wipe Swags .xajuno puo Soto jj quoo2a9s awl al „ tlyuaoul poop„ putom. aus •11001 01 0310119 .13A9 Siparq atunduaas J9SLaoA -woe&amp; aoluo oso Tyne&amp; SUM aaato seaulsnq qoqe&amp; pagonpuoo pus Teaodulaq OuTtll Ito ul aollosunoo s‘aujqdraas aalsjs suet aLIS .osllol.l ato Ilf unu qsapjo aqq son&amp; vaopony aaqsjs</p>
        <p>"TETI 6.40 A slog lunaans 0111 pus,, 'pies ags „‘ao4l 04 3459ds os 4ursi Jan nai„ I don't quite remember about it," said Sister Seraphine. "You told me, but there is so much to think of for the children." " I know, Mother," answered Sister Theodora. "Such things are not for you. Why should it come in your time? The Holy Mother knows best. But what will become us? The wicked Bill forbids anyone after this year to sell land to religious houses or to grant them a lease, or even to renew one. What are we to do? Our lease expires in two years." "We must buy the hospital before the end of the year," said Sister Seraphine. "Was not that what Father Andrew said ? I think I remember that." "But the Baron has refused to sell." "Then we must pray his heart may be softened, and ask him again. He will not refuse now." "Ah Mother, you do not know that man's heart. Perhaps it is even impious to ask the Holy Mother to deal with a thing so foul." " Peace, Sister," said the Mother in solemn reproach. "It is a poor human heart you speak of! Somewhere in it, doubt not, there is one pure spot. We are too sinful, perhaps, to see it, but the Holy Mother knows where it is and she can find it and make the hardest soft and the foulest clean." " Ah, Mother," answered Sister Theodora, gazing on the angel face with loving sadness. "You are too good for these evil days. But I was wrong to doubt. Truly I think your thoughts begin to purify the worst you think of. And yet—remember what he is—think what his life has been. There is not a —" "Hush ! Sister," said the Mother, "speak not of such things. it is enough that we know he is wicked. God knows the number of his sins. Our part is to pray for them. "Then shall we ask again ?"</p>
        <p>"It can do no harm."'- " Well, we must hope for the best." "And pray for the best, too, Sister. Do not forget that. And now I must go to the wards." And. so in perfect faith the Mother rose, and taking in her thin hands the flowers that adorned hr room she passed into the long wards, where the white beds were ranged on either hand, and the little faces that peeped from the smooth coverlets watched eagerly for her coming. With each she left a flower. Some laughed merrily at what she said, and some smiled wearily, wondering how far it was to where the other angels lived, and one moaning in delirium felt her sweet lips and was at peace dreaming the Mother of God had kissed away his pain. In the Mother's room sat Sister Theodora with pinched lips; and her pen with querulous sound made formal phrases for the Baron's eyes. Sister Seraphine signed the letter when she came back, and presently Sergeant Poiton was stalking with it along the causeway towards the Chateau Blamberg as though he bore a challenge. '</p>
        <p>Ave Maria! Soften Thou his heart! In the ugly square chapel the little Sisters of Pain with bowed heads knelt around the Mother. Ave Maria! They prayed their little prayers, for the love of Sister Seraphine and the children that suffered. The gaudy blue Madonna held her little one and smiled down prettily on the grey figures that knelt with heads bowed over the faldstools, little more than shadows in the twilight. Ave Maria! Father Andrew flitted out and it was very quiet as the sisters knelt still with bowed heads. The shadows stole up from the floor till the praying nuns were but undefined patches where the wide coifs absorbed the last rays of the daylight. All was cold and grey, except where the shadow was splashed with gold as the lamp flickered in the draught before the smiling Virgin. Ave Maria! Make clean the foul! Still they prayed and the night wind stirring the reeds in the marsh soothed the day to silence. Surely they will hear—it is so still, and the yellow-haired lady laughs so boisterously. Heaven send they do not. Through the open window the night wind blows gently in, and it comes but just now from the flarinalinner-table, where the Baron has read the letter to his guests. The ladies with flushed cheeks scream in delight. It is long since they had a joke so fine, and M. de la Course and the Comte de Selle have to shout their brutalities to get a hearing. It is a splendid idea of the Baron's. The white arms gleam and the jewels glitter, as they hold out the sparkling glasses, crying on the Baron with peals of laughter to clinck with them. There is no one like the Baron! Frocks and wine and laughter; a little love, now and then when we are in.the mood, and a gamble when we are not. Who knows how to live like the Baron? Drink Baron, drink and laugh while the candles flare and youth is not quite burnt out. But will no one take his bet? No one? Will nobody back the Abbess? The night wind in mercy left it all behind and carried into the silent chapel nothing but the whispered dreams of the reeds. In peace and darkness the sisters prayed on and the Madonna smiled prettily in the glow of the flickering lamp. Ave Maria. Soften thou his heart!</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-10-17_WidnesExaminer_art0019" n="art0019">
        <head>[ALL RIGHTS RESERVED.]</head>
        <p>[ALL RIGHTS RESERVED.]</p>
        <p>SISTER SEE APHINTE,</p>
        <p>JULIAN CORBETT,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-10-17_WidnesExaminer_art0020" n="art0020">
        <head>:Author of " A Bu4ne.ss in Great Waters," &amp;c.)</head>
        <p>:Author of " A Bu4ne.ss in Great Waters," &amp;c.)</p>
        <p>Sergeant Poiton was proud of his post of porter to the Little Sisters of Pain. He considered it an honourable retreat for an old soldier, and was fond of quoting the Mother Superior in support of his pretension. Once as Mistress of the Novices she had had to take a party of young nuns to a distance by rail, and she had chosen for them a carriage in which were already seated some infantry of the line. They were smoking short pipes with their rifles between their knees, and were making a noise. The young nuns were frightened, but their Mistress assured them there were no better travel- ling companions for nuns than soldiers. "That is what she said," Sergeant Poiton would conclude. "The Mother knows." The sergeant had an unbounded faith in the Mother, and so, indeed, had everyone at Ghoulines. Even the radical sheriff, if the subject arose, would say any afternoon in the skittle-alley, "It is a good woman," and the station-toaster would spit, seeking a new phrase for answer, but it was always the same, "Ah, sheriff, the little ones can tell us something about that." Then the sheriff would nod his red face, satisfied with the support, and the rest took ' up their mugs of brown beer to cover their want of words, having nothing in their minds but a vague pride in the breadth of mind their leaders dis- played ; and especially if the clerical sergeant happened to be there to hear. If he came it was . always with some new story of the saintliness of the Sisters or the good work of their hospital. He would lay it down that there was nothing like it in Europe, and no one disputed the dictum, for the tarpt %vats held, in spite of his opinions, to have ripe. experience of men and things. He had seen service and could talk of campaigns that were already in the school histories. His opinions were tolerated as antiquities. "It is a fine fellow," the sheriff would say when the Sergeant went out and his tread, uncertain on the ill-laid stones of the causeway, was heard dying away towards the canal bridge. "Better a reactionary like the Sergeant," the station-master would answer, "than a radical like the Baron. The Baron always said "Good morning, station- master," when he took his ticket through the little window, and the station-master was proud of the acquaintance. But he was careful to conceal this by never mentioning the notorious romme. without hinting he knew more of his infamous life than an official should speak. "Yes," said the sheriff, profoundly, as one in the secret, "there are bad radicals, just as there are good clericals. lam sorry for Sergeant Poiton. I am sorry for the Sister Seraphine. But they will have to go." " The.Bill.will pass!" asked the station-master, as one proud to have access to authoritative infor- mation at first hand.</p>
        <p>...--.. "Undoubtedly," said the Sheriff. "The hospital will be dissolved. The children will lose good friends." And the rest drank their brown beer with a sense of elevation, wishing for nothing but that a stranger were present to hear what kind of conversation they of Ghoulines enjoyed at the skittle-alley of an afternoon.</p>
        <p>The Sergeant lived in the white lodge at the gate, where the high walls that enclosed the hospital came almost up to the lines of trees by the causeway. Inside were the bare buildings where the Little Sisters of Pain nursed the children, and the garden which the Sergeant tended, chiefly, it must be said, by the hands of Madame Poiton, his deputy. On the other side of the road was the canal, where between the pollard willows the Sergeant operated almost daily against the perch. If he chanced to catch one he offered it to Sister Seraphine. This was his religion ; the rest of Christianity was vague background. Sister Sekaphine had once reproved him for his hatred of radicals. Radicals were enemies of the holy Church, she said, and we should not hate our enemies. She' had even said something about loving them. " Ah, mother," was his reply, "that is right for you, but for me—it is different. Not to hate enemies—perkaps but to love them The good God would not aak it of a grenadier." To-day he was far from loving them. Last night was the great debate in chamber upon the Religious Houses Land Bill, and this morning of all mornings his Reactionnaire was late. He had stepped across the sand-track where the peasantry drove their cows, and the Baron and the yellow-haired lady cantered their English horses, and he was standing on the causeway watching. He could see all the way down between the two rows of dusty poplars as far as the quay, where the little canal widened out beside the red-roofed houses to make room for the lazy barges to go to sleep. But not a sign of the paper boy could he see—nothing but the long white glare of the causeway that hurt his eyes and the whirlwinds that the growing heat of the sun brought dancing off the cool sand track under the trees. At last the little blue blouse appeared out of the trim houses. It stopped awhile to stare idly at the slumbering barges, and then continued its way along the causeway, and the clatter .of sabots grew quick and noisy in the summer stillness when he saw the Sergeant was watching. "You are late," said the Sergeant, as the boy drew his hands from his pockets respectfully to doll his cap and hand the paper. "Yes, Sergeant Poiton. They had so much tc say down there about the Assembly doings, I could not get it before." "What did they say?" "If you please, Sergeant Poiton," said the lad, with a trace of mischief—for he was quite modern, having been cultivated in the State school. " If you please, I did not hear much. But some one said he hoped Sergeant Poiton would enjoy his Reactionitaire this morning, and I hope so too, Sergeant. A very good day, sir." And the boys clatttered back in the sun between' the dusty trees, and the. Sergeant went in for hisli</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-10-17_WidnesExaminer_art0021" n="art0021">
        <head>111.</head>
        <p>111.</p>
        <p>" Madame Poiton!" criedlthe Sergeant, presently, as he refolded the paper with precision. "Have the kindness to do yourself the trouble to take the paper to the Mother." He prided himself that his manners, even to his wife were still untainted by the radical spirit, and Madame Poiton stuck the spade into the fragrant brown earth where she was digging with a pleasant sense of importance. "Is there news in it for her?" she inquired, coming to him with curiosity in every wrinkle of her puckered face. "She will see, Madame Poiton," said the Ser- geant, and Madame Poiton smoothed her apron, content to be unsatisfied in the pride she felt at serving under a commander of such dignified reserve no less than in the pleasure of the command he had given. It was for her little talks with Sister Seraphine that Madame Poiton lived. Nothing else mattered. -The Mother's dark eyes that shone so soft and serene under the stiff white head-dress were more to her than the summer sky. No song of the birds was like the Mother's voice, and when she smiled on the labour-crumpled old woman it was the Holy Mother herself. So with no more words, Madame Poiton stumped off between the rose trees on her errand with reverent excitement, as one that goes to mass. "Good morning, Madame Poiton," said Sister Seraphine, when the old woman reached the pretty room where the Mother sat. "Do you want soma thing of me ?" No, Mother," she answered. "It is only th 4 the Sergeant has sent you the paper." "Is there news in it?" "He said you would see. He is a trusty man, my husband, and does not chatter of hospital affairs. But I think there is news, and bad news, too. Betook down the ald sabre when I came away and began to clean it. That is what he does when he is troubled. It makes things brighter, he says." "Well, by and bye, I will read it," said Sister Seraphine, "but tell me first, Madame Poiton, how your rheumatism is." The tale flowed free; for Madame Poiton in her heart believed no one ever had such rheumatism as hers, and the unfeigned interest of Sister Seraphine encouraged the conviction. The ramifications of the subject were surprising, and its illustrations exhaustive ;but the Mother listened on, sympathising, admiring, and suggesting, as though there were nothing in the paper of so much importance as the complaints of the garrulous old porters wife. It was not till Madame Poiton's resources were exhausted that Sister Seraphine gave a thought be the paper, and asked her to find Sister Theodora.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-10-17_WidnesExaminer_art0022" n="art0022">
        <head>VI.</head>
        <p>VI.</p>
        <p>Sister Seraphine was sitting in her room to rest. The sun was not yet hot enough to be shut out, but already she had done some two hours' work amongst the little white beds. The sunshine streamed in upon her where she sat with a faint scent of roses and a pleasant hum of insects that soothed her to dreams. As her eyes closed wearily there came to her as it were a vision of her full life , with its peaceful adoration and its labours of love —a life of love that knew no sorrow of its own that grieved only in the grief of others. She seemed to be resting in patient hope upon a pleasant place that lay. before the gates of Heaven, and a smile played upon her lips between sleep and waking to think she had found out the secret of sorrow. It was as a man with smile for pure happiness where pain long endured at last leaves him in his sleep. But a cloud passed over her, and there was a voice of strife. She started up. The sunshine had left her and streamed upon the floor. There was a clatter of hoofs on the causeway and she knew a horseman had pulled up sharply at the gate. She looked out over the roses where Madame Poiton, bent and formless, was digging. The Sergeant was opening the wicket stiffly, and she saw him take a note from a groom. Then the cloud took shape and she turned fiom the window as the Sergeant called to his wife. The clatter of the horseman died away down the causeway and all was still again except for the sound of Madame Poiton's sabots scraping on her spade. It was Sister Theodora who brought in the note. It was a pretty pink little thing pungently scented and glorious with the Blamberg arms, and Sister Seraphine could hardly help blushing as she took it in her hand. To her it seemed the embodiment of everything she had renounced. To open it and breathe its heightened fragrance was like breaking her vows. Sister Theodora watched with strained anxiety and saw the sudden flush that made the mother doubly beautiful in her eyes. For a moment Sister Seraphine hesitated, and then as tbe warm glow died from her face she gave it to her counsellor with a look so full of hopeless resigna- tion that Sister Theodora btirned with indignation as she read: "Baron de Blomberg" so it ran, "presents his most respectful and devoted compliments to %biter Seraphine and begs to inform her, that he is pre- pared to sell the Ghoulines hospitalonone condition, namely, that the transaction shall be arranged between her and himself at a private interview in the Convent." "It is an infamous jest ! " burst out Sister Theodora. "Could he not refuse without an insult."</p>
        <p>" I don't think it is a lest," said the other. "He is in earnest." " What ? " cried the Counsellor. " Does he think he would be suffered to pollute the place with his</p>
        <p>presence ?" "The good God," answered Sister Seraphine gently, "permits many things for our chastening that seem strange to us. I was too happy. I knew it. I was proud of your love for me and all that you and the rest did for my sake. Now He will chasten my spirit by setting me a hard thing to do for you." "But Mother ! We could not let you. It cannot be. Monsegneur would never permit it." "Would it were so, but I feel it will come. The good God has sent it to punish me." "No, no, Mother. It is your good heart that makes you see good in everything. Heaven has nothing to punish you for. It is the devil who has put it into the Baron's head to torment you. Let me fetch Father Andrew. lam sure he will say the same. Father Andrew knows the devil so welL" . _</p>
        <p>"Well, let him come,". said Sister Seraphine. "But I am sure he will say it is from the good God." VII.</p>
        <p>It was a very grateful relief to Sister Theodora'a indignation to pour into the Jesuit's ear a voluble statement of the case. He had expressed no opinion when they reached Sister Seraphine's room, but although he did not permit it to agitate him, his concern was evident. He was very polite and grave and calm, and asked to see the letter. He read it slowly through and then with a con- summate air of reserve force punctiliously refolded it in its covor. Sister Theodora gathered hope from his self-,.._:onfident manner. Aha,' she whispered to her superior, "He knows the devil so well !"</p>
        <p>" I am-inclined to think," he said, suddenly, in an even voice, "the Mother is right." "You think " begun Sister Th4odora. "I said I am inclined to think," said the Jesuit. "But if she were," protested the Sister, "it would be impossible. No one ever heard of such a thing." "1 regard the case," said Father Andrew, "as quite without precedent. There would be difficul- ties, immense difficulties, but the Church is accus- tomed to difficulties, apparently insuperable. Even this may he—l mean, might be—surmounted; but at present it need not be gone into. It is, perhaps, hut the light thought of an idle moment." " Aha !" parenthesised Sister Theodora to her- self. "He knows the devil so well." "The Baron," continued the Jesuit, "has friends to amuse. This will seem strange to you. You naturally do not appreciate what a deal of amusing such people take. Those who live on amuse- ment are always on the point of starvation. But these are worldly matters with which you need not concern yourselves. Continue in cheerfulness your blessed labours and leave the rest to me. The Baron must be approached in a spirit of ehristian sympathy. Sympathy is the talisman of the church —sympathy with sin. She can feel for sin and. gives it every encouragement. In this spirit 1 will go to the Baron. I will go myself, and this after- noon; and I have no doubt that a little tact and diplomacy will do all that iarequired.</p>
        <p>( To be Continued.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-10-17_WidnesExaminer_art0023" n="art0023">
        <head>HOCKEY.</head>
        <p>HOCKEY.</p>
        <p>STOCKTON HE,ITIE V. FLIXTON.</p>
        <p>The Stockton Heath let XI. journeyed to Flixton on Saturday, where they beat the home t earn by ten goals to nil. Although the home teem made a good resistance far the firaliquarter of an hour they were subsequently outclassed. STOCZTON BEATE V. FLIXTON (SECOND ELEVENS).</p>
        <p>The first meeting notween Stockton Heath second eleven and Flixton monond eleven took place on Saturday on the ground of th, first named, the match ending in an easy victory for Stockton Heath, who won by 12 goals to nil.</p>
      </div>
