      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-10-17_WidnesExaminer_art0030" n="art0030">
        <head>Junior Conservative Club.</head>
        <p>Junior Conservative Club.</p>
        <p>A smoking concert in connection with the Junior Conservative Club was held on Saturday evening. Councillor John Roberts presided, and was sup- ported by Mr Roger Parr, the president of the association; Councillors Hulse and Smethurst and. Messrs Seger Owen, W L Williams, T H Ashcroft, J. T and A J Boardman, and Mr F W Deacon, of the National Council. During the evening 3ir Parr distributed the prizes gained by members of the bowling club during the past season. He remarked I that they had had considerable difficulty in getting the lawn into condition, but thanks to Sir Peter Walker, it was now second to none in the district. All that remained was to make good use of it. Thsy had only had one main this year, but he hoped they , would have more nest year, as he thought a game like bowls was a means of keeping the members together. The prizes were then distributed as follows :-1, H Taylor; 2, J T Boardman; 3, a Hayes; 4, IT Lockett; 5, W Daintith ; 6, B Black- ledge • 7, G Ingham; 8, T Hackett; 9, R Knight ; 10,11 Bishop; 11, H Wells; 12, J Bradshaw; 13, :J. Dagnall ; 14, R Dam!! ; 15, T Jenks; 16, 11J cock Consolation prizes :-1, E Clare 2, McCormick; 3, (1 J Boardman; 4, W Wirbur- ton. Mr Sear Owen proposed a vote oE thanks to Mr Parr. Mr Williams seconded, and the motion was heartily agreed to.— Mr F. W. Deacon gave an address on current politics and a vote of thanks was accorded to him.—During the evening songs or musical items were rendered by the following in a very creditable manner Messrs F Webster, C H Moult, Taylor, J Jolley, W C Brooks, W Broughton, T Reddish, and Bell. Votes of thanks were accorded to the Chairman and the artistes.</p>
