      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-10-17_WidnesExaminer_art0032" n="art0032">
        <head>WARRINGTON MALE VOICE</head>
        <p>THE WARRINGTON MALE VOICE</p>
        <p>CHOIR. Concert in the Parr Hall.</p>
        <p>If one may judge by the large audience which assembled in the Parr Hall on Saturday evening on the occasion of the concert given by the Warring- ton Male Voice Choir, the public of Warrington are not wanting in their support of local choirs. It is but a few days ago that the Warrington Apollo Prize Choir was similarly favoured with a large audience at their concert and neither of the two choirs can reasonably complain of lack of interest or support on the part of the public. It now re- mains for the choirs to justify this confidence in them by cultivating a more refined musical taste in Warrington. The programme which was submitted on Saturday was not of that popular variety which is usually submitted to Saturday evening audiences and although the applause was perhaps not so pronounced, the high-class music was none the less appreciated. Chief in- terest was centred in the performance of the choir. Since June last the conductorship has been under- taken by Mr W H Payten, Mlle- Bac. and he is to be congratulated upon the solid progress which the choir has already made under his direetiou. The choir consists of 44 members but not more than three dozen were able to be present on Saturday evening. Six part songs were given and a high standard of choral singing was uniformly main- tained. The voices are exclusively tenor and bars, and in this connection the introduction of a few good altos would, no doubt, materially improve the tone. Nevertheless the volume of sound was pleasingly melodious, and effectiveness was given to the choir's pPrformance by careful tone gradation and the observance of marks of expression. At times, some of the members seemed disposed tq pay too close attention to the music score and too little to the conductor, but this is a mere detail which can be easily remedied. The most beautiful piece of the evening was undoubted- ly the part song" In absence " (Dudlsy Buck), in the rendering of which the choir acquitted itself most admirably. The piece was sung with a neat- ness and piquancy of expression which left nothing to be desired, and the audience showed their appre- ciation by demanding an encore—the first and only one which the choir complied with. " Crossing the plains " was another part song in which the choir gave evidence of careful training. The opening and closing stanzas were sung to the accompaniment of the piano and it was interesting to note that the choir maintained the right pitch throughout the piece, as was demonstrated when the pianoforte accompaniment was resume.] towards the close. " The Crusaders" was perhaps the most adequately mg piece, bit coming as it did immediately after the interval, the effect was somewhat impaired by the commotion caused by many of the audience proceeding to their seats. Mr Arthur Griffiths very aweetly sang the solo part. "Martyrs of the Arena" and the Soldiers Chorus from "Faust" were declaimed with vigour and earnestness,the singing of the guar tette in the former, being the only point open to criticism. Altogether the performance of the choir</p>
        <p>A LADY writes:—</p>
        <p>"Humanly speaking, Benger's Food' entirely saved baby's life. I had tried four well-known foods, but he could digest nothing, until we began the Benger.' He is now rosy and and fattening rapidly."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-10-17_WidnesExaminer_art0033" n="art0033">
        <head>enger,s</head>
        <p>enger,s</p>
        <p>redounded to the credit of the members and the conductor, alike, and augurs well for its fatare success. The leading vocalists were Miss Lucy Davies (soprano), Miss Kathleen Merchant (con- tralto) and Mr Cuthbert Allan (baritone). Miss Davies's initial performance was the difficult song "Love's Bargain" (Wishaw),but she was more suc- cessful in her interpretation of "At Last" (Liddle). Beeponding to a recall, she was on more familiar ground in her singing of "Sleep baby sleep," a song which delighted the audience. Miss Kathleen Merchant sang with consummate taste and skill "The Enchantress"(Halton)and was deservedly en- eored. Her sweet contralto voice was also heard to advantage in "A Summer Night" (Goring Thomas.) Mr Cuthbert Allan was very popular with the audience and was twice encored. The fervour with which be sang "Bedouin love song" (Pinsuli) and such old English songs as "The golden vanity" and " Twankydillo " evoked an outburst of enthusiam which culminated in a recall on each oecasion. Mr J G Ter Poorten contributed several violoncello solos. " Nocturno " (Piatti) and " Harlequin " (Popper) received careful and delicate treatnaent. The exigencies of time caused him to omit 6' Spanish Dance" (Popper) in the second part of the programme but his interpretation of the graceful work "Adagio' (Goltermann) was given with more than usual taste and judgment. Mr Harris,the humorist,was iti- clisposed,but this did not prevent him from amusing the audience with his witticisms on old songs and new books. These were accompanied by pianoforte illustrations in which Mr Harris proved himself an adept. Mr Harris also proved himself an excellent story teller and some of his ben mots will not readily be forgotten. The dries of accompanists devolved upon Mr H Payton and Mr F Atkinson.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-10-17_WidnesExaminer_art0034" n="art0034">
        <head>MUNICIPAL ELECTIONS.</head>
        <p>THE MUNICIPAL ELECTIONS.</p>
        <p>President of the Trades Council Approached. At the annual meeting of the Trades and Labour Council on Tuesday evening, the President (Mr J Edwardson) said that during the past week he had been '6 pestered" by people asking if be would stand for Latchford ward against Mr Perob.rton. He wanted to obtain the opinion of the Council as to whether he should contest the ward or not. A deputation had also approached him to see if he would stand for Bewsey Ward against Mr Brophy. He told them be could not give any decision until he tested the feeling of that Council. I The SECRETARY said he did not think it would be in I order for the Council to discuss election matters, a Labour Representative Committee having been formed I to take the matter out of the hands of the Council. The only answer they could give would be that the President must please himself. The PRESIDENT said he would not stand except as a Trades Council candidate. The SECRETARY moved that the Council advise Mr Edwardson not to allow himself to be nominated on this occasion. Mr WILLOCE9 seconded, and the motion was unani- o usly carried.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-10-17_WidnesExaminer_art0035" n="art0035">
        <head>Warrington Boy's Singular Death.</head>
        <p>A Warrington Boy's Singular Death.</p>
        <p>Mr. S Brighouse, County Coroner, conducted an inquest at the Police Station on Monday, touching the death of John Peter Welsh,agedl2years,who re- sided at 7,Dale-street,Sankey Bridges—Bridget Mc- Coy,the deceased's mother stated that the boy came home at ten o'clock the previous night, after selling the evening papers. Ile partook of a hearty meal of chipped potatoes and retired to bed. The follow- ing morning he complained of pains in the head and was allowed to remain in bed. In the after- noon witness went out for a quarter of an hour and on returning found deceased had died—Dr. It Smith said he made a post-mortem examination of the body and found that the cause of death was asphyxia, due to the presence of a small piece of potato in the larynx—A verdict of " Death from misadventure " was returned.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-10-17_WidnesExaminer_art0036" n="art0036">
        <head>FACTS ABOUT THE ALPHABET.</head>
        <p>FACTS ABOUT THE ALPHABET.</p>
        <p>You know the English alphabet has 26 letters. Da you know the number in other alphabets? The Sandwich Islanders' alphabet has 12 letters, Burmese 18, Italian 23, Bengal 21, Spanish 27, Arabic 28, Russian 41, and Sal:merit 50. There is an A.B.C. of disease too. In various countries, climate and conditions attack various organs. In the British Isles the organs most affected are the liver and digeetive, system ; and the A.B.C. of health lies in keeping these healthy. Authorities agree that the use ef Chas. Fords' Bile Beans is the moat certain means of doing this. Mrs. W. Davidson, of Dunhead Farm, Monikie (Dundee), says :—" Indiges- tion and biliousness made my life one long misery for years. Night after night I had to sit up in bed racked with pain. My head often seemed as though it would split, and the pains across my back, shoulders, and stomach, were almost unbearable. I held cold damp cloths to my head to ease the pain, and I tried to bring up the bile from my stomtch by making myself sick, but it was all to no purpose. Sometimes I would be going about doing nay work, milking the cows and doing other farm work, when suddenly a mist would rise before my eyes and almost blind me. So acute were the pains in my head that my eyes would get quite blaek underneath. I was also subject to dizziness, and often when I crossed the farm- yard I would turn so giddy and faint with pain that I had to walk about almost doubled in two. No matter what I took I could get no relief. Chas. Forde's Bile Beans were recommended and I owe my life to them. A few doses gave ma relief and you may be sure after that I gave them a good trial. They have cured me entirely and ill health is now unknown to me*" Such is one ease out of thousands. Wheeever Bile Beans are used, bene- ficial results fellow. Indigestion, constipation, piles,, billiousness, ansemia, rheumatism, all female ailmentst dizziness and headache cannet resist their powerful ye painless action. All chemists stock them at one and three-half-penee or two and nine per box. The large box is really the most economical.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-10-17_WidnesExaminer_art0037" n="art0037">
        <head>Wycliffe Temperance Society.</head>
        <p>Wycliffe Temperance Society.</p>
        <p>The inaugural meeting of this society was held in the Wycliffe Memorial Buildings on Wednesday evening when an excellent musical programme was submitted. Mrs Henry Roberts occupied the chair. The opening item was a song rendered by Mr Spruce entitled "The skipper," Miss Carrie Hastings then sang " The children's Home " in a manner which augues well for her future success. A Marionette solo was next given by Mr W Ford in good taste and style. Miss Carter then recited "Only a slave" and later in the evening gave another recitation, Mrs Moores delivered an address on the temperance cause and spoke of the great need for temperance in Warrington. They needed to be in deadly earn- est. She pointed out,the necessity for training the young, to the temperance cause and said she con- sidered it a work for the Master. A glee party composed of a double quartette of the Warrington Male Voice Choir under the conductorship of Mr S Hassell sang a glee entitled "In absence " for which they were encored. Mr Hassell then gave a solo entitled" When other Lips" in which his sweet tenor voice was heard to advantage. At the interval a fruit banquet was held. Miss Carrie Hastings afterwards gave another solo, entitled " Caller Herrin," for which she was encored. The Oleo Party again pleased the audi- ence by another glee entitled " Crossing the Plains," Mr S Hassell sang a second solo entitled " Ailsa Mine," which was highly appreciated. Mr F Morrell, secretary of the society, announced that another meeting of a similar character would b. e held shortly. Mr Osborne then gave a bass solo in good tone and manner. Miss E Reberts very acceptably eang two songs, the first entitled " The Dawn," and the mond "Lullaby. The Rev J Yonge brought the meeting to a close by thanking rir 7 Atkinson who all those who had taken part in the program/vie, ninedn le so t hohandecociaarprisendieodu.t the arrange- y At DerhyahTre Sessions on Wedeesdp,y, the Rev Arthur Gledhill , curate of St Anne's C ' hureh Sheffield, was in i dicted for ndecently asseulting Clara Wilson, aged thirteen,. at Dronfield on May 31. The case for the preseeiatten was that accused, whilst on a visit to his father, sehoolmaster of Brimfield, met the girl near the school and took her photo M an impreper !position. Arena en eathhe denied the clearee,and the jury found prisoner net guilty, and considered there was no stain upon his character. At an inquest upon a labourer at Birkenhead it was stated that deceased wore a waistcoat nest to his skin and above that a flannel singlnt, two more waistcoats, a Cardigan jacket, another waistcoat, a muffler and a jacket. In addition he wore two pairs of trousers. At a meeting e the Wigton r.seard if Guardians Mr F Grainger suggested that a soldier—a harmless. lunatic m n Ireland whom they had been asked to reeve—be et to the War Office. " There appears t) be 31'0; of armless lunatics there," be said.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-10-17_WidnesExaminer_art0038" n="art0038">
        <head>arPreATOtio 401t1/4414V 4 hjw4:45,3-.0!</head>
        <p>arPreATOtio 401t1/4414V 4 hjw4:45,3-.0!</p>
        <p>THE NORTHERN UNION. HE NORTHERN RUGBY LEAGUE.</p>
        <p>First pivision.</p>
        <p>SALFORB v WARRIN4T67.</p>
        <p>In gloomy, cheerless weather this important fixture took place at the Willows, Weaste, before 5,009 people. Having beaten Batley away a week ago, Warrington were considered to have a chance of being probably the first club to besmirch Salford's slate this season. This view was, howevermet fully entertained when it became knowa that the Wirepullers were without Fish and Dickenson at three-quarter back, the former stated to be ill. while Dickenson was on the injured list. Dock substitutes were llockenhall and Ratcliffe, Edmunds took Hockenhall's place at half-baek. The home side turned out as advertised, the only change being Rignett In place of Dan Smith at full back, the last named player having been married in the week, and in consequence was on his honeymoon. J Swift kicked off for Warrington and Brown caught the ball and made his mark. Ttee succeeding punt did net afford Salford mush benefit, as the leather was immediately returned, Boardman was conspicuous. A. couple of scrimmages near the centre line followed, and then the visitors were allowed a free kiek, which was cleared by Hignett. Davies pluckily tackled Knight near the middle line after which Bell put in a fine run on behalf of Salford, who in consequence were placed on the attack. From a pack near the Warrington line. Lomas was given possession. and the Maryport man drawing Tom Williams to him, gave the last-named possession at the right moment, and he sped over the line at the corner amid a chorus of cheering. Lomas was entrusted with the place, hut from a difficult angle he failed, the ball falling short. From the drop out. Salford were soon pressing again, but Knight came to the rescue with a good run and punt, which were the means of carrying play to the centre. Salford up to now had had the best of matters. After some pressure by the Reds, the leather was returned, and Eignett made a rather poor at tempt to drop a goat. A free-kick in the vicinity of the Warrington quarter lag was conceded the home side, but Lomas from the place just failed to lend the goal poets. The visitors, at length, took a turn at attacking, and close to the Salford line Davies, who was clearing, had a kick charged down by Taylor. who scored a simple try at the corner. Rat- cliffe failed at the place From some *lever thretkparter work Bone was given a grand opportunity of addine to Salford's score, but the famous sprinter, with a clear opening before him, threw forward. Tom Williams and Lomas were many times conspicuous and made great endeavours to take the lead, the Warrington defence having a warm time of it. Scrimmaging near the Salford line looked ominous, but a. free kick brought the necessary relief. Some good work on the part of Knight, Isherwood, and Ratcliffe resulted in the last named almost getting over, but he was hell no just in the nick of time. At half time the score was :—Salford, one try (three points) ; Warrington, one tr r (three points). ten resuming, play was fast and fierce. Salford at one, reached the Warrington territory, and Walsh was cheered for a short run and kick. When matters seemed serious for the visitors Forster made his mark, and brought much-needed relief. A strong attack by Warrington nearly resulted in Ratcliffe and Harris working the ball over between them. Mid-field play fo'lowed, and then Walsh cleverly picked up and ran well int. the opposing half, but Knight picking nil eluded Lomas, and got in a nice kiek. Hignett shaped clumsily in clearing, and in fact let the visitors in, the Salford full-back at his next call, with plenty of time to get rid of the ball, making it dead. As it happened, however, there was little harm done, as the next minute the Reds were attacking with the utmost vigour, Knight bringing Bone down when at fall speed for the line. This occurred near the corner. Prong the break-up of the ensuing pack the ball was passed across to Bell. who made ter the line with a couple of Warrington men after him Harris stopped him and he passed to Lomas who do tged iii close to the posts. Frem an easy place the same player was unsuccessful in adding the goal points, the ball swerving and going on the wrong side of the postal Salford, encouraged by taking the lead. played a better game. Lomas was especially prominent. MO Tom Williams and Bone were given numerous opportunities, which several times were nearly improved upon. After thrice being tackled by Knight, the Warrington full batik, Bone at last eluded him and scored at the corner. Brown took the place, which again proved abortive. Warringtost attacked again and in the last few minutes Taylor kicked a goal from a murk. Teams : Salford—A Efignett, fullback; Ii Bone. T Williarns,Walsh. and T Bell. three-quarter backs; J Lomas aoll D Davies, half backs; P Tunney. J Rhapps, J. Williams, 1+ Beath, K Shaw, NV Brown, H Buckler. and El Shore, forwards. Warrington —Knight, full back; Hockenhall, Isherwood. Ratcliffe, and Harris, three-quarters backs; Burgess and Edmunds. half backs; Boardman, Edmondson. Forster, Taylor, J Swift. Risberts. Morrison. and W Swift, forWsrds . Referee, Mr J Bruekshaw (Stoekport). Tough judges Messrs T Smith (Rochdale Hornets) and J E iwards (St Helens),</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-10-17_WidnesExaminer_art0039" n="art0039">
        <head>ST. HRLIINS v RUNCORN.</head>
        <p>ST. HRLIINS v RUNCORN.</p>
        <p>At St Helens. The home team opened play, which settled in the Runcorn half ,but weak play by St Helens let Runcorn into their half. Mercer saving at a critical juncture. St Helens gained ground, but the visitors worked up to the home line, and Moran dashed over, but was rolled In touch. There was a fine round of passing between Warder. Butter- worth, and Jones, but the last named missed a good pass,and Houghton regained the lost ground with a splendid run and kick. Jotly was injured and had to retire. Half-time arrived with ne score.</p>
        <p>On resuming, Banks kicked across to Liversags, who missed the ball, and from a beautiful opening Huncern entered the home half. Jolly here resurnel play. Runcorn had a fine chance of going throlgh,but St Helens forced them back to the centre. St Helens were getting possession more frequently, but made poor use of their chancs. Hunaorn worked their way into the home half, but could not find au Opening, and the game ended in a pointless draw.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-10-17_WidnesExaminer_art0040" n="art0040">
        <head>WIDNES v HULL.</head>
        <p>WIDNES v HULL.</p>
        <p>At Widnes, before 2,000 spectators. Parkinson started for Hull, finding touch in the home 25." flown intercepted a Hull pass, but Taylor grassed him on the "25 "line. From a nice opening by Lally,Hughes get away to the line, where he gave to Taylor, who got over. Aspey failed at goal. Peacock just failed os the line a moment later after a very pretty passing bout by Lally, Bown, and Taylor. Play now became very exciting. and Widnes attaeked with vigour. Taylor wee tackled by Tilley and Hughes, under his own I posts, and a moment later flown was grounded in an equally dangerous position. The visitors new changed the venue, but the home teen got back, and Riley added a try for Widnes, and Mexon a try for Hull. Half-this score : Widnes 2 tries (6 points), Hull I try (3 points). Aspey re-started for Widnes, and Lally was conspicuous. Some exciting open play followed, and a clever interception by Jaques forced Tilley to find touch on the line. Widnes now assumed time aggressive, and the Hull line was besieged. flown am! Peacock wee stopped on the line, and then Bows' near get over the posts Rigby failed to improve. Hull press- ed strong by for a time,and then a nice opening was afforded by Webster, a pretty passing movement ending in Peacock I getting over. Aspey failed to improve. Result :—Widnes 4 ,ries (12 points) Hull I try (3 points). Teams : Widnes : Tilley, back ; Hughes, Hewn, j. Taylor, and Peacock, three-qoarter backs ; Lally and Douglas, half backs; Aspey, Power, Riley, Rigby, Curtis, Webster. Leyshon, and Lynch, forwards. Hull : Taylor, back : Jaques, Brown, Cook. and MUROII, three-quarter backs, Franks and Thomason, half backs ; Lewis, Wade, Parkinson, John, Harrison, Langhorn, Hamer and Fildes, forwards. Referee. Mr P F Farrar, Halifax. Touch judges, Messrs Lilo% Birkenhead, and J H Yates. Swinton.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-10-17_WidnesExaminer_art0041" n="art0041">
        <head>BRADFORD V. LEIGH.</head>
        <p>BRADFORD V. LEIGH.</p>
        <p>At Park Avenue,-;;-Ba-i;ria-y:be-ftTo:e 7.000 spectators. Bradford reorganized their back line, and played Bagers for the first time this season. Bradford lost the t-as s, and Broadley kicked off on a heavy ground. with practically no wind. The game at the outset was fast but even, each end being visited in turn, and the backs kicking well. There was little to choose until close upon half time. Foste. Mars- den, liners. Reseltfne. and F Murgatroyd passed and ran well. The latter punted, and Reseltine was up quick enough to score a good try, Bagers failing at goal. Changing ends Bradford rapidly asserted their superiority. A mistake by Davies let in Sinton, who scored in the corner. Mosby could not land the goal. After dribbling by Bagers land Robertson, the latter scored again. Marsden also added 1 a try. from which Mosby kicked a goal. Molyneaux dropped a goal for Leigh. Result:-13radford 1 goal, 4 tries (14 points), Leigh 1 goal (2 points).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-10-17_WidnesExaminer_art0042" n="art0042">
        <head>Fortified by</head>
        <p>Fortified by</p>
        <p>MAZAWATTEE COCOA</p>
        <p>you can face the prevailing cold weather with impunity. The heat-producing elements contained in this pure eocea are most pronounced. It also forms a most satisfy- ing beverage, and is rich in nourishing aid sustaining properties. MAZ AWATTEE COCOA is far superior to, and goes twice as far as, any ordinary cocoa, and is, therefore, only half the cost.</p>
