      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-10-17_WidnesExaminer_art0043" n="art0043">
        <p>rim EXAMINER, FRIDAY, OCTOBER l7, 1902.</p>
        <p>BRIGHOUSE RANGERS V BATLEY. The Batley fifteen paid a visit to Brichause, and a crowd of about 2.000 spectators assembled. Batley won a keen game by 1 goal and 1 try (5 points) to Brighouse 1 try (3 points).</p>
        <p>T-TvPDEREIFIELD V. HILIFAX. There were 8,000 spectators at Fartown to witness this match Huddersfield won the toss, and Jack Riley kicked off from the Fartown end. The Halifax forwards soon had the measure of their opponents, and carried several scrum- Mares, Moseley kicked to Little, and following up tackled the Halifax back. The visiting forwards, however came away well, and a dribble led by Nettleton saw Huddersfield penned on their line. A penalty kick to Halifax cave Little a chance, and he kicked a goal. On resuming W Dewhurst was hurt in stopping a rush, and had to retire, and Sutcliffe came eut in his place. A penalty kick to Halifax sent the ball to Kitchen, and scrummaging ensued on the home line. A strong rush by the Huddersfield forwards removed the danger, and play settled down in the home half. Half-time—Halifax 1 goal (2 points), Huddersfield nil. On crossing over the Halifax halves passed out, but Rlgg and Williams mulled. Mesely intercepted, and kicked up the field. Sutalffe and Deere followed no well, and the last-named had hard lines. The gams had now got very rough, and In the subsequent play Naylor.- (Huddersfield) and Bulmer (Halifax) were ordered off. Oven play followed, and then Sutcliffe tried flying kicks The result was that Rigg picked up and passed to Joe Riley, who bad no difficulty in scoring. Little failed at goal. Sutcliffe was hurt, and had to retire, The rest of the game was roues, and the result WAS—Halifax 1 goal and 1 try (5 points), Huddersfield nil. Teams Huddersfield.-0. Readyhongh, bscks; L I Deere, W Dew- hirst, Moseley and W F Kitchen, three-quarter backs; A Marsden and F Dewherst,balf backs ; Furness.E V Chambers. II Dawson, P Driver, It Knight, H Naylor, N Sutcliffe and</p>
        <p>Taylor. forwards. Halifax.—Little, tack ; Joe Riley, Williams, Rigg, and Hadwen, threequarter backs ; Holroyd and Nettleton, half backs; Jack Riley. Swinbank, H Wilsoa, Bluster, Horbury, Hammond, Summerskill, and Morton forwards. Referee, gr W Robinson (Manningham). Touch judges, Messrs G W Wilson (Keighley) and W W Taylor (Hunsiet). ' WIGAN V. OLDHAM. Oldham paid a visit to Central Park, Wigan. where about 9,000 spectators assembled. Wigan made the pace hot in the earlier stages of the game, and pressed for a considerable period, Vickers almost getting over from a pack close to the line, and Bothwell striking one of the Oldham uprights from a penalty kick. At the interval, however, neither side had scored. Result :—Wigan 1 goal (2 points), Oldham nil. HULL KINGSTON HOVERS V SWINTON. The visit of the Swinton team to Hull proved a capital Fair-day attracti^n, and quite 9,010 people witnessed the encounter. Early matters favoured the Rovers, who re- peatedly got possession in the scrimmages. Just en half- time Barnett scrambled over with a try for Swinton,Preston failing at goal. Halftime :—Swinton 1 try (3 points), Kings- ton Rovers, nil. Kemp re-started, but Swinton very soon swarmed into the home quarters. and Wallwork snapping up a foalish pass by Gillings beneath the borne posts. scored an easy try. Preston Again failed at goal. The home forwards led by Sharks and Kemp played up strongly after this, and Starks scored a try, the same player failing at goal. The game continued to be keenly contested, end Morgan getting away.grandly passed to Evans, who scored the visitors' third try, the goal kick again being unsuccessful. Towards the end the pace slackened, and Valentine picked up and ran in another try, the aoal kick being a failure. Resnit :—Fwinton 4 tries (12 points). Kingston Rovers 1 try (3 points). BRIGHOUSE RANGERS V. SALFORD. This match was played on Monday at Brighouse, in wretched Weather, before a poor attendance of opeetators,and resulted in Salford being defeated for the first time this season. The visitors won the toss, and Kilburn started the game for the home team up the slope. A good loose rush by the home forwat^ds caused Salford to defend their line, but eventually the pressure was relieved by Davies, and Bone further improved the visitors' position by a strong run to the home quarter, where he was well tackled by Baker. Not to be denied, the Brighouse forwards again broke away, and from a mark Saville placed a goal. At half-time Brighouse lei by two points to nothing. In the second half Salford were almost continualty on the attack, but they were unable to break through the home defence. Saville kicked another penalty goal for the hone team, who won by two goals (four points) to Salford. nothing.</p>
        <p>HUNOLET v BROUGHTON RANGERS, into a goal. Ralf-time arrived Saints leading two goals to one. The second half was all in favour of the home team,bad A crowd of 7,000 or 8,000 people gathered at Parkside to shooting by the home forwards missing many chances, the Watch the meeting of amulet and Broughton. Hamlet i 3 four new players from South Wales—O'Brien. Martin, d visitors goal-keeper saved well from Oxley, full time arrived anwith the home winning by two goals to one. Owen (three-quarter backs), and Burnett (n half batik), and Albert Goldthorpe and Robinson also turned out. The Rangers' side was fully representative. All that was seored up to half-time was a penalty goal, which was to the credit of the Rangers, In the second portion Wilson made two fine runs in the space ,Df five minutes. At the first attempt he was tackled on the line,but when he get the ball a second time he ran throuzh the opposition, and scored a pretty try. W James failed at goal. Wicldesen was the next to score. He did so after a fine bout of passing among the Rangers' backs,rnnninc down the touch line aide and crossing the line in the corner. The position was a difficult one, but W James kicked a fine goal. Widdeson again scored before the close. The result therefore was : Broughton Rangers 2 goals and 3 tries (13 points), liunalet</p>
        <p>SECOND DIVISION. DARESBURY AND MOORE V. WIDNES ST. MARY'S. Played at Moore en Saturday. A keen game was antici- pated as neither of the clubs up to the present had lost a match. Widnes had their full team on, while the home team were without Risley their centre. An exciting game resulted in a draw, neither team having scored. CAMDEN WESLEY GUILD V. WILDFRSPOoL CENTRAL. Played at Runcorn. Final, Camden Wesley Guild, three goals ; Wilderspool Central, one WARRINGTON ALBIONS V. CABLE Co. RESERVE. Played on the ground of the last named club on Saturday. Morris started for the Cable Co, and at once made tracks for the visitors' goal, but Corradine clearel. The game had been in progress about five minutes when Webb dribbled down the field and scored a goal for the visitors. After this reverse the homesters livened up, but could not get through. From a forward rush Hanapson scored the second goal. Near half- time Payne scored from near half-way. Half-time : War- rington Athlone, three goals; Cable Co, nil. Hampson restarted for the Albions. and this half saw the visitors surrounding the homesters goal, when Webb scored two goals, Johnson (1.1. Rampeon (1,. Corradine (I),and Pugh (1). Cable Co could cot get a footing in the Athlone' quarters, and were outplayed all through the garne.:Result : Warrington Albions, nine goals ; Cable Co nil. LANOASHIRII LIIAGUR. RIRLESTOWN v. SOUTHPORT CENTRAL. Rarlestewn journeyed be Southport on Saturday to play the second league match of the season, and returned home defeated by three goals to nil. Such a disgraceful show did the Rarlestown forwards give at St. Helens in the Cup-tie that Leach. Settle, and Turton were dropped, Slater, Clare, and Ditchfield filling the vacant positions. The changes did not benefit the team much, for the attack was every bit as bad as before. Rarlestown started and immediately carried pray into the central goal, a fine cross shot from Barlow causing Garvey to fall full length to effect a clearance. The ensuing corner was easily disposed of, and pretty play by Atherton and Shadbolt culminated in the latter centreing with unerring accuracy, Lang meeting the ball and placing past Hobin without difficulty. Although the Sandgroundera had most of the play to the interval, they failed to pierce the visitors defence again, and the whistle blew with Barlestow. in a minority of a goal.—On resuming, the Central quickly assumed the upper hand, and goals were scored by Atherton and Hulligan in quick succession. Rarlestown tried hard te reduce the homesters lead, but although they had plenty 01 openings they were defeated by 3 goals to nil.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-10-17_WidnesExaminer_art0044" n="art0044">
        <head>SECOND DIVISION. RESULTS TO SATURDAY.</head>
        <p>SECOND DIVISION. RESULTS TO SATURDAY.</p>
        <p>voinls.</p>
        <p>Phi. Won Lost Drn. For Agat. P. Leeds Lancaster Kegley lielbeck 6 . 4 . 1 1 53 14 ... 9 Barrow Manningham York Rochdale Hornets Castleford Birkenhead South oblelds ...... Milieus Wakefield Trinity Normauton Bromley Morecambe Dewsbury Stockport RESULTS OF SATURDAY'S MATCHES. G. T. P. G. T. P. Leeds Birkenhead ............ 0 0 0 Bramley Keighley Rochdale Hor 1 1 5 Holbeek Barrow .. ......... ..... 3 521 Wakefield Trio 1 1 5 South .Shields Morecambe Lancaster : 1 3 11 Normanton Manningham ............ 2 1 7 Stoekport</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-10-17_WidnesExaminer_art0045" n="art0045">
        <head>LANCASHIRE COMBINATION.</head>
        <p>LANCASHIRE COMBINATION.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON " A " DEFRATED BY SALFORD" A:" Salford " A' visited the Wilderspeol enclosure on Satnr- 4ay and defeated the Warringtonians to the extent of nine points to nil. Warrington were poorly represented, whilst the visitors played a strong team. Fairhuat kicked off against the wind, and Salford made a raid on the Warrington goal-line early on, but the ball was sent back to centre. Play continued to be very even. Eventually, Warrington attacked for a long period. and Allen almost got through. At length, as the result of good passing by the Salford backs, Smith scored, no goal being kicked. Just before the interval another try was registered for Salford. Half-time :—Salford " A," 2 tries ; Warrington A,' nil:—During the second half Salford had the best of matters, another try being scored near the corner flag. Final :—Salford '• A," 9 points ; War. ngton " A" nil. RUNCORN " A" V ST. HELENS " A." Played at Runeorn before a fair crowd of spectators. Run- corn won the toss and the visitors started. After several serums Runcorn took up the aggressive. Foster securbd,and threw the ball over to Price, who transferred to Dutton, the latter scoring a fine try. Jones kicked an easy goal Barton showed fine defensive tactics. Halt-time: Runcorn, one god t one try; St Helens, nil. On resuming; play was I ocated in centre for a time Poole charged Barton's kick down and dribbling over the line, added a try. Jones again doing the needfull. Price followed a kiek and the ball was grounded over the line, but the referee disallowed the score. This WU followed by a determined attempt by Jones, who went in touch at the corner. Price slipped at the corner,but recovering, passed to Foster, wha ran in easily. Jones just failed at goal. From passing Bacon secured, and ran in a neat try. No goal resulted. Final : Runcorn, two goals three tries (13 points) ; St Helens, one try (three points),</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-10-17_WidnesExaminer_art0046" n="art0046">
        <head>ASSOCIATION FOOTBALL. `.7-1</head>
        <p>ASSOCIATION FOOTBALL. `.7-1</p>
        <p>SANKEY GREEI A.F.O. V. WILDER9POOI ALBUM. Played at Wllderspool and resulted in a win for the Al blocs by eight goals to nil. WARRINGTON AND DISTRICT LIIAGUR. FIRST DIVISION. LATCHFORD V CABLE COMPANY ATHLETIC. A close game marked the meeting of these teams at Latch- ford on Zonclay, victory resting with the home team by a goal to nil.</p>
        <p>BURTONWOOD V TRELWALL.</p>
        <p>Burtonwood were the visitors to Thelwall on Saturday, and a highly interesting game resulted in favour of the visitors by two go sis to nil. ST. MARYS V STOCKTON REATH. Ihe meeting of these teams on the Saints' gronud resulted in a capital exposition, victory resting with the h)me team by three goals to nil. WARRINGTON " GUARDIAN" v LATCHFORD BRITANNIA. This game was played on the ground of the last named club, resulting in a somewhat hollow victory for Latchford Britannia by eight goals to one. OUGHTRINGTON PARK V WARRINGTON TRINITY. Played on the ground of the former. During the first half A Lord, Leak and Siddeley, scored for the home team. Half- time : Oughtrhagton Park, three goals ; WarringtOn Trinity, nil In the second half the leaders again hal the best of it and adding enothor goal, won a highly interesting match by four goals to nil. ST. PETERS v NEWCZURCH. Played on the ground of the former. Joseph Oxley kicked off for the Saints who ran down to the visitors goal. Saints pressed for some time before J Phoenix opened the score for the Saints. Broomfield following with a second the visitors pressed and a penalty was awarded them which was turned</p>
