      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-10-17_WidnesExaminer_art0047" n="art0047">
        <head>WARRINGTON.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>Personal.</p>
        <p>The" London Gaist-t;'"--nn.nounces that Second Lieutenants E E Ridgway and P N Avery,of the let Volunteer Battalion the Prince of Wales's Volun- teers (South Lancashire Regiment) have been pro- moted to the rank of lieutenant. Among a number of supernumerary officers "absorbed into the estab- lishment of their respective unite" is Captain B Fairclough, of the same battalion. On Sunday Lady Greenall gave birth to a son at the Hyde Park Hotel, London. Miss R M Dakin, of Grappenhall, a student of the Manchester School of Art, has won the King's prize tor modelling. •</p>
        <p>Juvenile Rechabitism. The members of the Hope of Sankey tent of the Independent Order of Rechabites (Warrington Juvenile District No 4), were entertained to tea on Monday evening in Sankey Bridges schoolroom. Bro IT Tindall (the superintendent of the lodge) was present and he was assisted by Bros R Gregg, J Woolley, J Lawton, R Pardoe, D Graham, and C S Fellows, After tea, Mr Hassell delighted the juveniles by some interesting gramophone selec- tions. Fruit was handed raund and altogether a most enjoyable evening was spent.</p>
        <p>Good Templary. The weekly session of the Temperance Mission Lodge of the 1.0.G.T was held on Wednesday in the Ebenezer Mission Room. Bro F Merlin, C.T, presided over a good attendance. Seven new mem- bers were initiated into the lodge and two were admitted on clearance card. Bro Roberts stated that the Midland Railway Company had established la temperance refreshment bar at Bradford and es- I pressed the hope that these would .be established all over the country. He moved that a vote of thanks be sent to the directors for their forethought. Sister Sheath seconded and the motion was carried. The C.T gave a report of how a Welsh policeman closed a drinking club and made some very perti- nent remarks on drinking clubs. The rest of th evening was spent in games, &amp;c, and refreshments were partaken of, and an enjoyable evening was spent.</p>
        <p>The Birmingham Visiting Justices met on WednesslaY to inquire into the affray which took place in the chapel at Winson Green aaol. Birmingham, during Divine ser- vice list Sunday. Two prisoners named Butterworth and Lightfoot were charged with assaulting warders, and after going into the cases, the Justices recommen- ded that they should be flogged. The sentences will have to be submitted to the Horns Secretary for confir- mation before they can be executed. WAS THE CHAIRMAN BIASSED ? —sllr George Wilder was, on Wednesday, charged at Worthing with driving a cnotor•car at exce9sive sped—The defendant objected to Colonel Wisdin (the chairman) adjudicating in the case on the around that he was biassed against motor-cars, but the chairman refused to retire. —Mr Wilder then refused to plead. The magistrates imposed a fine of £lO and costs. Notice of application to quash the proceedings was given. In opening a Primitive Methodist Bazaar at Accring- ton on Wednesday, the Mayne (Councillor Cunliffe) said he was glad there was no raffling. Ile was sorry, how- ever, to know that a palmist had been engaged. Palmistry was decidedly wreng. and was associated with fortune telling. It was not only foolish, but Dais- ahievous, and in some eases disastrous.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-10-17_WidnesExaminer_art0048" n="art0048">
        <head>ROYAL PROCESSIO.</head>
        <p>THE ROYAL PROCESSIO.</p>
        <p>Next Saturday the King and Queen drive I through London in State, as arranged last June: and the whole town will turn out gaily decked to welcome them back. At the urgent representation of the officials of the various Councils on the route, his Majesty has decided, after all, to turn his pro- cession into a strictly State one, to proceed at a walking pace, to extend the route and to have it guarded by troops. At first it was said that this postponed procession was only to be a drive at a trot over the June route with a pause for luncheon at the Guildhall; but this would not have been very popular with the masses who were done out of seeing his Majesty on the original date. So the King in council with his advisers at Balmoral decided that the procession through London should be made as imposing as possible and the route was extended slightly at the City end, while that for the drive to the Thanksgiving Service at St. Paul's on thq Sunday was made entirely different, proceeding hi the Embankment and returning by Holborn and Oxford Street. The new State carriage, which gives such a much better view of the occupants, will be used, and many features from the summer programme will reproduced. Owing to this change of plans and the exti a work involved. his Majesty returned to town earlier than he had intended, leaving North Berwick on Saturday last for London, to make all the final arrangements. The King looks even better than he did before he went to Balmoral, and there is no doubt that seldom, if,ever, has he been stronger in all his life: and it will be a general cause of delight to his loyal subjects to see how well be is looking. In response to the King's gracious decision to meet the wishes of his people, London has decided to dress itself in its best; and the whole town will be decorated as sumptously as it was to have been in June. A totally new scheme, howeder, -has been adopted; and the long Venetian poles, which have recently been so much in evidence, will be con- spicuous by their absence. The main feature of the new scheme will be strings of flags, hung across the road every few yards and festooned with flowers : so that their Majesties' triumphal drive will be under a sort of many coloured canopy. The King has a very busy time in front of him at the conclusion of a week's racing at Newmarket, as on Friday he is to hold an " Investiture " at Bucking- ham Palace and receive the City's address, to economise time on the Saturday: while on Sunday he has the great Thanksgiving Service at St. Paul's, and on Monday the review of the Guards newly home from South Africa. To draw up such a programme shows that his Majesty and his medical attendants are well satisfied with his state of health. The King and Queen may look forward to a right royal welcome from the crowds which will be gathered all along the route.</p>
        <p>• No sooner does Lord Kitchener seem to have arrived home amidst a flourish of trumpets from his South African successes than he is off again most quietly and unostentatiously to seek fresh fields and pastures new to him for military service in the Indian command. Great soldier that Lord Kitchener is, the one thing he lacks to make him an ideal Commander-in-Chief of the British Army is Indian 'experience: and throughout the South African campaign it was felt that the most fitting position that could be found for him was the supreme military command of this great Empire within the Empire. It was obvious, also, that this was in the eye of the military authorities in their retrospective appointment of Sir Power Palmer and the subsequant increase of his tenure by one year, which showed that the post was merely being kept warm for the victorious leader in South Africa, until such time as he could be spared from his duties on the veldt. Moreover, it is rumoured that our Indian Army and its whole system re-</p>
        <p>(1) COLONEL HAMILTON: (2) CoLONEL CONGREVE; v.c. : (3) MAJOR MARKER: (4) cArTAIN" MAXWELL, P.C.</p>
        <p>quires reorganisation : and Lord Kitchener is certainly the man for the post. Leaving this week. he will travel via France to Egypt, where he will inspect the great Assonan• dam and the Gordon Memorial College at Khartoum, after which he will proceed to India about the middle of Novem- ber to make preparations for the great Coronation durbar, for which he is entertaining a very distinguished party. During that time and at the subsequent mancenvres Lord Herbert and Lord Ingestre will act as extra A.D.C.'s to the Indian Commander-in-Chief: but this arrangement will only be temporary, and Lord Kitchener's regular staff will be practically the same as it was in South Africa. He expects unremitting work and energy from those around him: but in return is very faithful to those who serve him well and not a tuft-hunter. Colonel Hamilton will be Military Secretary; and the other leading members of his personal staff will consist of Colonel Congreve, V.C., of Colenso fame, Major Marker, R.A., and Captain Maxwell. V.C., 1.5. C., who gained both the bronze cross and the D. 5.0., to say nothing of promotion and mention in despatches, in South Africa, where he attracted Lord Kitchener 's notice, since when they have been inseparable. Captain Birdwood may also go out on his lordship's staff, as well as Colonel Bingham of the Life Guards, who served on General French's staff during the late war. His Majesty gave a farewell luncheon in honour of the departing general on Monday: and on Tuesday Lord Kitchener gave evidence before the War Commission, preparatory to his departure. The most enthusiastic good wishes of his grateful fellow- countrymen will accompany Lord Kitchener to his new post, which is site to prove a fresh source of honour and success to this indefatigable soldier.</p>
        <p>Last week Oxford was en fete, more decorously than usual, as the younger sons of Alma Mater were not in residence at the time. The cause of the festival was the tercentenary of the Bodleian Library, which is one of the great glories of the University, and a national treasure houss, It was founded by Sir Thomas Bodley near the end of Elizabeth's reign, and has since then expanded into one of the grandest collections of books in the world under the tutelage of the Uriversity and specially appointed officers. This tercentenary was marked by a concourse of scholars not only from other parts of Great Britain, but from America, Franca, filarganz, /War* 4talY, and ON</p>
