        <p>Mai Mar</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-10-17_WidnesExaminer_art0049" n="art0049">
        <p>EVENTS OF THE WEEK.</p>
        <p>ILLUSTRATED.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-10-17_WidnesExaminer_art0050" n="art0050">
        <head>NS THROUGH LONDO'</head>
        <p>NS THROUGH LONDO'</p>
        <p>other countries of the civilised world: and at s special Convocation in the Sheldonian Theatre many honorary degrees were given, including the D.C.L. to the Hon. Andrew White, of Yale, Lord Strathcona, Dr. Martens, of St. Petersburg, and Professor Vinogradoff : the D.Sc. to Professor Minot, of Harvard; and the Doctor of Letters to Conte Ugo Balsani, of Rome, Dr. Bury. of Dublin, Dr. Canfield, of Columbia, Mr. Clark, of Cam- bridge, Mr. Jenkinson, of Cambridge, Dr. Von Laubmann, of Munich. M. Omont. of Paris, Dr. Schipner. of Vienna, Dr. Vries, of Leyden. Mr. Warner, of the British Museum, and Professor</p>
        <p>THE TEIICENTENARY OF THE BODLEIAN</p>
        <p>LIBRARY AT OXFORD.</p>
        <p>West, of Princetown University. A big banquet was held in the grand old dining-hall of Christ Church, at the close of which all drank to the "Pious memory of Sir Thomas Bodley," eloquently proposed by Sir Richard Jebb, M.P., of Cambridge. Other interesting speeches were made: and the whole of the celebrations passed off most pleasantly and successfully, It may safely be said that. seldom has there been a more representative gathering of learned professors: and unquestion- ably the occasion was a great one in the world of letters.</p>
        <p>Seldom has the report of a Commission upon a subject of national interest given less satis— faction on all sides than that recently published as the result of the secret sittings of the Re- mount Inquiry Commission, and signed by General Sir Randolph Biddulph, Major - General Sir Frederick Maurice, K.C.B. commanding the Woolwich District, Major-General T. A. Cooke (late 17th Lancers), Major-General Sir G. de C. Morton, K.C.1.E., C. 8., commanding the Dublin District, and Major - General Arthur Pao-et, C.V.O. (late Scots Guards), who did such good service at the front. It will be remembered that, when early in the year in re- sponse to the unanimous de- mand of public opinion the Government ap- pointed a Com- mission to inquire into the question of the general mismanage m e nt of remounts dur- ing the war and the compulsory retirement of General Tr n - man, great indig- PRESIDENT OF TIM . REMOUNT INQUIRY COMIIISION, WIIICII nation was ex- 11AS JUST MESE:II'IID ITS pressed at its REPORT, secret sittings.</p>
        <p>GENERAL S EIDIMILPH, G.C.13., G.C.M.G.</p>
        <p>and the cart taken that none of its transactions should leak our,-as is now the case with the Commission of Inquiry into the War, the general prophecy being that everything would be glossed over with generalities and the main object burked. Whether General Truman himself was to blame for incom- petence or merely an official scaugoat, was a matter of minor importance: and in the report the nation is simply gently told that every- thing was a little wrong, and nothing very much so, while the main object is successfully hurked in platitudes, and no real result is achieved beyond clearing General Truman of direct responsibility, he being pronounced, in short, rather conscientious than competent. The Commission's report has been received with strong criticism on every side , and the Commissioners may be congratulated upon adding another evidence of work without result to be filed amongst the red-tape dockets in Pall Mall. This report raises fears for the result of the Commission on the War now sitting in camera.</p>
        <p>It is not often that a short sentence is remitted' in England for private reasons: but, whether sympathising with the iconoclastic attitude of the Kensitites or not, all will be unanimous in admitting their pleasure at the release of young Mr. Kensit from Liverpool gaol, where he was serving a sentence of three months. The sad circumstances surrounding his father's death are too well known to need repetition : and it was- eminently one of those occasions for the exercise of the royal prero- gative of mercy. Mr. Kensit's father, it will be remembered, petitioned his Majesty direct from his death- bed in the Liver- pool Infirmary for his son's re- lease: but prob- ably the King would have seen no reason to interfere with the course of the law but for the tragic end of his illness. Mr, 3. A. Kensit</p>
        <p>_ . upon release tele- Vino lIAS. 311 ST BEEN, RELEASED graphed his FROM LIVERPOOL GAOL BY ms , thanks direct to MAJESTY'S CLEAILICCY. his Majesty and . the Home Secre-</p>
        <p>MR. J. A. K.</p>
        <p>tary, but, at the ;11.ne time; announced his intention of prosecuting uis crusade against Ritualism with undiminished vigour, in accordance with his father's last behest of no compromise." Though his sentence has been remitted, the question of its legality will be raised in Parliament as a separate issue. The inquest on the late Mr. Kensit's remains was held this week: and his funeral at Hampstead last Saturday excited much interest and sympathy.</p>
