      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-10-17_WidnesExaminer_art0061" n="art0061">
        <p>Established 183 C.</p>
        <p>e drop pin I ephaaae. mum ; about 6 ton,. t ACCORDING to thf</p>
        <p>_ The Cal Ito Arms. tonal newspapers, the Government intends t° use its utmost energy to pass the Education Bill: as far as possible, in its present form. There WI be some slight modifications to make it less no" palatable to the Birmingham gorge, but these be more in semblance than in fact. Mr. BALFOO stands by the principle that the denominational schools are to be maintained under denominational influence out of the rates. He will have to face 0 serious revolt in his own party, aud much resent' ment among other Unionist members, who may forced to vote under the clerical banner. Prob' ably Mr. BALFOUR will be able to hold together * sufficient number of supporters to get gi bill through. After that, however, will come the„ deluge: The disorganisation of the party Onlev hasten a General Election, and few old Parli°' mentary hands able to judge think that can be delayed more than a year or so. The Patil of the Government is beset with difficulties. In the first place, they will, throughout tha session, be i* danger of an adverse vote through the reluctance of. their own side to be associated with tiP unpopular measure; secondly, they will We to meet the refusal of local authorities t° put the Act into force if they get it throng% and the resistance of ratepayers where authoritio/ do endeavour to make it operate. There will /01 only one way of settling the strife, and that will h,ei by an appeal to the electorate. The matter vo°: then, in most constituencies, become a politico.' issue, as there are only two parties in the coutatri between whom the people can choose. The quee,: tion the Liberal party has now to consider whether its armour will be ready for the contet It is pointed out by those who are makiii.it the appeal for £250,000 for a Liberal W*. Fund that one hundred and twenty-three seatai*6 England, Scotland, and Wales were unconteste by Liberals at the last General Election, and le many others the work of registration has beefj sadly neglected from lack of means. That, 1104' ever, does not reveal the whole of the weekneo The professional Conservative workers from the districts free of conflict are drafted into the otbe constituencies, and all the arts of canvassing I exhausted. Then, again, it is frequently foe° necessary to pass over a highly desiral#ll Liberal candidate because he cannot a/ad to give money as well as knowledge an labour to the conflict. A democratic pal must always have financial troubles, but it sheIJI at least be assured of such support as is availabl° on democratic lines. The Trade Unions are geeer ally equal to an occasion, and are now raisifig money to put a hundred candidates in the fiehi; The Salvation Army can raise £50,000 in a OIL° denial week ; and the Methodists can refer pride to their " Million " fund. This appeal the rank-and-file of the Liberal party for a quarteo of a million at:a time when the party has the beg i possible promise of achievement should meet t, a prompt and hearty response. Mr. Hnano; GLADSTONE deserves this encouragement in arduous task which he is fulfilling as chief Libor* Whip. Lord ROSERERY'S speech at Glasgow hi*: quickened memories of the great statesman wli° 5 long directed the destinies of the Liberal partY• We may hope that the multitudes who honol°_ the father will be prepared to help the son in tb° practical work which he is doing to re-assert tbe strength of Liberal principles in the country.</p>
        <p>• _ .</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-10-17_WidnesExaminer_art0062" n="art0062">
        <p>LORD ROBREERY'S letter Lord Rosebery to Mr. BLACK must be and the Liberal Party. regarded as still further</p>
        <p>hardening the separation frO-na Sir HENRY CAMP - BELL-BANNERMAN which he announced in the spring. He practically declares that he cannot co-operate with the Liberal Party and its leaders unless the party adopts without qualification or reserve the advice that he gave to it last winter. At Chesterfield Lord ROSEBERY did two things— he said that he wanted a peace by agreement in South Africa and he advised the Liberal party to clean its slate of its domestic programme. What he said about peace in South Africa was important and welcome, because Lord ROSEBERY had hitherto been known as a supporter of the war, and on that occasion he ex- pressed himself in agreement with the wishes of the advocates of peace. What he said about domes- tic politics received less attention, though obviously, if it meant anything, it meant a great change of front in the Liberal party. With the suggestion of such a change of front a great many Liberals were in strong disagreement, and amongst others the leaders of the party. Lord ROSEBERY then' arranged a series of meetings at which he de- veloped his ideas,and explained that not only was he in favour of giving up Home Rule but that he had never been at heart with Mr. GLADSTONE'S policy of Home Rule. Sir HENRY CAMPBELL- BANNERMAN could not ignore all theseldeclarations unless he chose to imply that Lord ROSEBERY was a perfectly random and irresponsible speaker. On the other hand, he could not endorse them unless be was prepared;to abandon• the general policy for which he had made himself responsible to the Liberal party when he was elected and re-elected Leader of the Opposition. In a very moder- ate and courteous speech at Leicester Sir HENRY CAMPBELL-BANNERMAN re-affirmed his loyalty to the accepted policy of the Liberal party and asked whether Lord ROSEBERY would co-operate with the party. Lord RDSEBERY replied in a letter to the "Times," in which he used the ominous term "definite separation" to describe his relations with the Liberal Leader. The next event was the forma- tion of the Liberal League, and at a dinner of that League in July, Lord ROSEBERY explained that the League was to continue until the Liberal party had accepted its policy. Thus for the second time a group of Liberals put a pistol at the head of the Liberal party and demanded its capitulation. Lord ROSEBERY next refused two invitations to address meetings in E linburgh. One came from the Scottish Liberal Association, the other from the official body representing the four Liberal Associations of Ecltnburgh. He, how- ever, arranged to hold a meeting in that town, but it was to be a meeting held under the auspices of the Liberal League. Mr. Thomas SHAW, Solicitor-General for Scotland in the last Liberal Government, refused to attend that meeting ; and another Scotch member, Mr BLACK, on being invited,wrote to Lord ROSEBERY himself to ask him whether he still maintained his attitude of definite separation, pointing out that no self-respecting Liberal member could join in what would be a slight on a statesman whom the Liberal party in the House of Commons hal promised to follow. To this Lord Rossi3sav re- plies that until Sir Henry CAMPBELL-BANNERMAN accepts Lord Rosseeßy's policy the definite separa- tion must continue. About the answer to Lord ROSEBERY from the Liberal party there can, we think, be little doubt. Lord ROSEBERY is an engaging personality and an attractive orator, but the Liberal party has too much self-respect to throw over its leaders,its principles,and its policies, in reply to a random invitation from a politician whose most conspicuous qualities are not tenacity of purpose, perseverance in adversity, or invari- ability of mind.</p>
        <p>It is very easy for Ministers to go about the country defending the Education Bill and the Corn Tax at packed meetings of the Primrose League, but when their supporters have to face the electors at the ballot-box it is somehow a very different tune which we hear. Mr. Leckie is standing as the Conservative candidate. for Devonport, and he opens his campaign by announcing (1) that he would vote against the Corn Tax, and (2) that he is anxious to remedy the " reasonable grievances" which Nonconformists find in the Educationßill. A party which can only hope to carry a by-election by putting up a candidate who attacks its two chief undertakings has come within measurable distance of the end.</p>
        <p>The British public has still no clear conception of the enormous devastation wrought by our arms in the Transvaal and the Free State, and even the " Times " persists in declaring that no more than 600 farms were burnt—a figure which may have been accurate when it was published, i.e., at a date before the systematic" sweeping" movements had begun. The truth is, as a British officer who had travelled over the greater part of both Republics shews in the "Spectator," that about 80 per cent of all the Boer buildings were destroyed. And now we have the " Times " reporting that the Dutch Reformed Church is claiming compensation for the churches which were burnt by the score during the war. Can a greater scandal be imagined? A clause in the Hague Convention specially prohibits such devastation as this. But the " Times " opposes any thought of compensa- tion. Instead, it suggests that we might pay the Boer pastors a subsidy or salary which would enable us to supervise their opinions, and depose any ,bold spirit who might happen to offend us. Imperialism and conquest have made us surpris- ingly apt pupils in the arts of tyranny.</p>
        <p>So the famous inquiry—i—ntO-the conduct of the Mr war, which Balfour promised so often and with ususul d.eo chaahfieloeu-reinshfrtrumpets, is after all to be the iloeorr white-washing party. It is dto sit with closed s withoul, reporters, and the evidence will never be published. When it has finished its labours we may expect some such re- port as the committee of major-generals, who have been studying the behaviour of the remount d partment, have just issued. They find that despite the fact that the horses purchased abroad cot about £3O, and were worth at most £l2, and that they were of such miserable quality that in many regiments their average life was five weeks, this department Was based on " a system of tried and proved efficiency." It was under a general" of no exceptional ability," who was, in fact, so stupid that his whitewashers complain that he "had difficulty in seizing the drift of their questions" to him during the inquiry; but he did his best, and was honest, and therefore they had nothing but praise for him. As thEl QUartermaster-General put it, " I found him there," and it is no one's business to ask how he came there. The report explains, however, what it means by the " effici- ency " of the remount department. It was efficient in time of peace. It bought its 2,500 horses in the TJnited Kingdom every year in a steady jog-trot way, and it had never once asked itself what it would do if war should break out, or dreamt of stvdying the foreign markets to which it would have to go. Naturally, it failed when the stress came. The fact seems to be that our Army is nothing more than a peace force at the best. Its swords are quite sharp enough—for parade.</p>
        <p>--- The death of Mrs Beamont at the advanced age of 89 years removes a well-known and highly-respected Warrington lady, who will be</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-10-17_WidnesExaminer_art0063" n="art0063">
        <p>missed in Church and philanthropic effort. The name of Beamont will always be associated with the history of local government in the town for it was largely owing to the endeavours of Mr. Beamont that Warrington was incorporated a Borough in 1847. The town showed its appreciation of Mr. Beamont' s work by placing him in the honoured position of being its I first Mayor. Over 50 years have tmssed since then, but the Mayoral chair has never had an occupant who filled the position with reater credit to him self and the town generally. His portrait fittingly adorns the Council Chamber and it is a noteworthy coincidence that the year which witnesses the passing itf his devoted wife should also be marked by the additio of a beau- tiful work of art to the Corporation art gallery, which Mr. Baamont's legacy largely helped) to provide.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-10-17_WidnesExaminer_art0064" n="art0064">
