      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-10-17_WidnesExaminer_art0065" n="art0065">
        <head>RUNCORN AND WIDNES.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN AND WIDNES.</p>
        <p>RUNCORN TECHNICAL INSTITUTE. In connection with the lechnical Institute the annual distribution of prizes will be held on Wed- nesday evening. The successful students of the Institute day school and evening classes will be presented with their awards by Sir Edward Russell, of Liverpool. 'An interesting evening may be ex- pected and all are cotdially invited. For further details see advt. DISAPPEARANCE OF A. RUNCORN MAN. The police have been notified of the disappear- ance, under singular circumstances, of James Snowball, stonemason, of 11, Bold-street, Runcorn. He had been working for some time for Messrs Spencer. Chapman, of Silvertown, London, and had been lodging at 6, Wythes-road. Recently he came home to Runcorn ill, and after remaining there a little while, returned on Friday week to London. He went to his work on the following day, and nothing has since been heard of him either at his lodgings or at home. It is stated that he was a little strange in his manner before leaving home to return to London. MERSEY MISSION -...40C1AL. On Monday in connection with the Mersey Mission a social gathering and fruit banquet were held which was fairly attended. Mr C Tonk's band played for dancing during the evening. Songs were given by Miss Garvie and Mr Ball. Mr W By- field gave several items on the gramaphonre Musical chairs and parlour games and ping poag &amp;c., were also indulged in. Fruit, tea and refreshments were at intervals handed round. The vegetables and fruit were sold, and everyone seemed to enjoy the evening's entertainment. Mr J Griffiths who before leaving England was the secretary of the Sunday school, and member of the choir, and is now en- deavouring to build a church at Mount Parry, Queensland and the proceeds of the evening £2.5s will be sent to him as a small donation towards his noble work. RUNCORN MAN MISSING. Mrs. Snowball, of Runcorn, applied on Monday to the magistrate at West Ham to assist her in tracing the whereabouts of her husband, Samuel, a foreman stonemason. He came to London to take a contract at Silvertown on October 3. He worked all that day, and part of the next, since when nothing had been seen or heard of him. The miss- ing man is described as being sft. 7in. high, of dark complexion, brown eyes, dark moustache and tuft, on chin turning grey. On the left hand he has two overgrown fingers. He was wearing a grey suit., brown boots, and a bowler hat BRANCH OF THE WOMEN'S TEMPERANCE ASSOCIATION. The first meeting of the above Association was held on Monday evening in the Primitive Methodist school room Green-road. Mrs T Simkin presided over two hundred members and friends. The meet- ing was opened by the singing of a White ribbon hymn, prayer being offered by Mrs Wright. A good programme was subsequently submitted. Mrs Ainsworth of Southport and Mrs Wright of Welling- ton, New Zealand, gave very interesting and profitable addresses. The ladies contributing to the musical part of the programme were Mrs Timperley and the Misses Waterworth, Timperley, Heath, Wright, Loyd and Warburton. Miss Freda Lloyd acted as pianist. By special request the dialogue entitled" Emiline Holder's at home" was repeated. The singing of the Temperance Doxology brought the meeting to a close. RUNCORN SONS OF TEMPERANCE. An interesting meeting in connection with the Sons of Temperance Order was held in the Green- way-road school on Saturday evening. The Presi- dent, Mr Thomas Clarke, eccupied the chair. After the ordinary business had been transacted Mr George Powell, one of the auditors, submitted the quarterly report. Among other things he said that the members now numbered 310, an increase of three in the quarter. Toe sum of £4O 4s 4d had been paid to 22 sick members. They now had 145 cadets, an increase of 14 on the quarter. Tee sum of £6 12s had been paid for sickness. After the report a temperance testimony meeting was held A number of short addresses were given. The question put was " Why am I a teetotaler and how long ?" The following officer and members took part :—Messrs James Johnson (the president), Thomas Clarke, Daniel Bisbrown, Thomas 11 Whyte, Edward Key, jun., Joseph Walker, J H Green, and Thomas J Holt, Four of the speakers were life abstainers. The experiences were very interesting and showed in some cases the great importance of parental influence. CYCLISTS AT VARIANCE. On Tuewlay, before His Honour Judge Bowen Rowlands, R.C., at the Runcorn County Court Benjamin Looker, of Warrington, sued Reuben Burman, of Runcorn, for the sum of £5 in respect of damage done to his cycle and for personal injuries.—Plaintiff's case was to the effect that a few Sundays ago he was riding up the hill at Frodsham in company with two other cyclists on the correct side of a road twenty yards wide. Half way up the hill they met defendant also riding with a company of cyclists. Defendant was then on the right side of the road. When, however, defendant got Within a few yards of the plaintiff he swerved right across the road colliding violently with the plaintiff, buckling up his maehine,and causing injuries which laid him up for four days. As ant go when the accident P lai no ct icffu rwr eodu dl de ine ontdlaent t d aegf er eneda- to pay for the damage though he ,would not admit be was in the wrong. The case fr the defendant was that he was on his right side and that he crossed in order to avoid a collision with some of defendant's companions who were on their wrong side. Free wheels were hard to manage and he had bad two accidents previously. He only promised to pay the plaintiff because it was the Sabbath and he did not want to have a bother.—His Honour said the fact that defendant bad told one of his companions of his bad luck with a free wheel was a practical admission of his being in the wrong on the present occasion. He thought the excuse respecting the Sabbath day altogether insufficient. —JUdgMent for the plaintiff for .22 13s 6d with costs. PUBLIC BENEFIT UNDER raciiCLOCßlzoa BEST BOOTS IN LUNA 51111{11.—Advt</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-10-17_WidnesExaminer_art0066" n="art0066">
        <head>EXAMINER FRIDA Y OCTOBER 17, 1902</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER FRIDA Y OCTOBER 17, 1902</p>
        <p>AN ALLEGED WANDERING LUNATIC. On Thursday morning an alleged wandering lunatic named William Porter, of Preston was bound over by Mr F J Norman on the condition that his brother took charge of him. SCALDING FATALITY AT RUNCORN. The three-year-old-daughter of Samuel Dutton,of 66, Cawdor-street, Runcorn died at half-past. six on Monday from the result of a scalding accident. It , seems that on Sunday evening Mr Dutton was lying on a sofa in the kitchen. His son Arthur brought a washing bowl and filled it with trot water from the kettle. Leaving the bowl in front of the fire he went out for some cold water. Dur- ing his absence the child, Betsy, came into the room and complained of toothache. She leaned over the table with her face in her hands for a moment or two and then walked backwards to- wards the fire and fell into the boiling water. The lower part of her body was very badly scalded. Dr. McLennan was called in,but despite his attention the poor child succumbed to her injuries on Monday morning. On Tuesday at the inquest a verdict of "Death from misadventure" was returned</p>
        <p>FLT-WOMAN'S SAD DEATH. An inquiry into circumstances attending the death, which took place on Thursday morning, of Margaret Marshall, 51, wife of Joseph l‘larshall, 6 Portland-street, Runcorn, was conducted by Mr Dobson, district coroner, at the Police Station on Friday afternoon. Mr. &amp;van, H.M. Inspector of Factories, accompanied the Coroner. Mr F T, Crosby was foreman of the jury.—rae husband of deceased said he was captain of the boat Mary belonging to Mr. Abel. About three weeks ago he was taking his boat through some locks at Liver- pool. Deceased was on the boat with him, and be had no one else to help him. Deceased was slacken- ing the rope on the winch when her clothes became entangled and she was crushed against the winch. She made no complaint. She afterwards went to a doctor in Liverpool, but he said nothing to her. He brought her home about a week later. She was very ill when she came off the boat. Dr Robinson was called in and attended her until her death.—By a Juror : She said she was sick before she went to see the doctor in Liverpool. She said nothing when she came back. She did not complain until she got back to Runcorn.—Charlotte Row- botham, daughter of deceased, said she came home on Saturday week. She complained of being sick, but did not say she was injured in the side. he afterwards said she had been crushed against the winch, but that it did not hurt her. She spat blood on the day she came home.—Dr Robinson said he was called in on Saturday week. Deceased com- plained of pain in the lower part of the left side of the chest, and she accounted for it by the injury described. She said the Liverpool doctor had told her to see a Runcorn doctor. Otherwise it would have been a case for the Infirmary. Her tempera- ture was sub-normal and she coughed up clots of blood. She gradually got worse, dying before he arrived on Thursday morning. He had been seeing her daily. He attributed death to an internal in- jury. The jury found that the woman died from the effects of an internal injury accidentally re- ceived.</p>
        <p>religious sect but was the outcome of the wishes of a thoroughly united town. Sergeants Attwood and Jeseock responded. They had only done their duty and were pleased to know those at home' had been so well looked after in their absence. Dr McDougall toasted the chairman, Mr Taylor, who briefly replied. During the evening a number of musical selections were given by local arOstes,Meesrs H Branch,o Pownall, R Morris and H Grounds taking part. Mr T Morris, and Mrs Chamberlaine were responsible , for catering, which was done in first-class style. ' Mr W Jenes, secretary, carried out all the general arrangements in the most business-like manner, his efforts contributing in no small degree to the success of he dinner. The following is a list of those present :—Militiamen, volunteers, and yeomen, G Attwood, John Antrobus, R Bond, R Bankes, Bradbury, W Cowley, W Clarke, M Coffey, W IT Charlesworth, W Dale, T Dunbavin E Douglas, John Ellison, T Green, W Griffiths, John Howas, Henry Hill,J Johnson, F J Jeaeock,A Kennedy, W Looker, P Lovell, H Limb, T Mellor J Massey, James Moore, S Mack, P Mort, T Newport, W Parkinson,James Robinson, John Robinson, E Richardson, J Royle, T Starkey, W Stubbs,W Spencer,F J Speakman, J Stanton, J Wilson, P R West, C Ward and 9 Whitby. Together with the following :—The Revs F J James,A Wells, J B Foster. W A Eyre, W Gay, J Osborne and F S Morris, Drs McDougall and McLennan, Captain Timmins, Captain Ashton, Lieutenant Miller, and Messrs P J Norman. A R Norman, R H Posnett, J Littler, G Bellamy, J H Weston, G R Frith,A Lightburn, J F Frith, E Ma7shall, J Pritchard, R Morris, D Little- more representing the Postmaster, W Boanett, F Wiswall, G Mackie. G H Harrison, M Faster, W Miles, H Wright, J Horsefield, E Ecnison, C R H Harrison, G Hewitt, J H Salkeld. G H Kirkham, T Dern, J Wilson.T Jones, who had the management of the dinner, and W Jones, the secretary.</p>
        <p>NOTES BY THE WAY AT EARLES TOWN. --- LBY A CORRESPONDENT). FOOTBALL. We are being sadly hampered just now for water. The authorities seem to think they can do pretty much as they like, for just wlien you most need s good supply of water, that is just the time when they take good care that you don't get it. The other day we were without, and when it kindly flowed through the pipes, it was of such a forbid- ding colour that people were unable to drink it. How we shall go on when the wells are completed, it will be bard to say, but we surely will not be worse off than we are at present. On Wednesday people had to go begging for water, wherever they could get it, all manner of utensils being brought into use, and yet we have to pay our water rents ancomplainingly.</p>
        <p>No excuse can be given for the ignominious display of Earlestown at Southport on Saturday. Indeed the game went so far in favour of their opponents that it would not have been surprising had they scored three or four additional goals. The forwards were never really dangerous and the chief fault of this,lay in the fact that the ball was rarely if ever passed out to the wings. Clare, tbie new outsidelleft, was little more than a passenger, his partner (Slater) failing to give him any of the passes usually expected from an inside man. Stan- ton on the other wing was neglected just as much, in fact, both of the outside wing men had to drop back with the half-backs if they wished to get a kick of the ball. The play was in the centre almost the whole of the game,at least, as far as the visitors were concerned. Hobbs in goal was not as smart as he usually is,tbe third gual being the result of a glar- ing blunder on his part. He caught the leather from a shot by Kelly and instead of getting rid of it at once he started bouncing it on the ground until he was charged off by Hulligan, who quickly tipped the ball into the net. Jones was the best of the backs, Leigh kicking far below his wonted strength. Harrison was the only one of the half backs to shine, in fact, this player was about the best. of the visitors team. The least said about the forwards the better, although Clare had no possible chance of showing his ability. Slater was hard- working but to very little purpose, whilst Ditch field was worse than useless. Serious alterations will have to be made if the team is to win matches.</p>
        <p>Newton came a cropper at Oswestry on Saturday in the combination. They have not done at all well lately, and on Saturday they had to retire beaten by 4 goals to nil. Turbo, a new centre for- ward, played a good game, but the Salopians were too clever for the Newtonians.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-10-17_WidnesExaminer_art0067" n="art0067">
        <head>REV. J. FLANNAGANI.VISITS RUNCORN.</head>
        <p>THE REV. J. FLANNAGANI.VISITS RUNCORN.</p>
        <p>In connection with the Primitive Methodist Church, Greenway-road, the Rev J Flannagtm, of London, who is well known to Runoorn congregations visited the town on Wednesday last. In the afternoon a service was held in the chapel, which was largely attended, the preacher basieg an excellent discourse on the text " Be ye therefore followers of God as dear children." In the evening the Greenway-road Chapel was filled to its utmost capacity, admission too, being by ticket, which in itself is an eloquent witness of Mr Flannegara's popu- larity.—Mr S Taylor, J.P., occupied the chair, and he was accompanied by the Revs A G Reed, of Warrington WA E 3 re, Mr Wilson, and others. The Rev W A Eyre said that they were always pleased to see Mr Taylor in the chair. The first time he ever saw Mr Taylor his impression was that he was a thorough English gentleman and he had never since had reason to change nis opinion. There were many loyal hearts towards Mr Taylor, the first citizen in the town, in the Primitive Methodist connection. He was especially pleased to see their friends from Warrington, Widnes, St Helens, Northwich, flelsby and elsewhere. (Applause.) The Chairman, in introducing the lecturer, said he did not wonder at the large audience when he remembered that Mr Flannagan's name was a household word in Run- corn. He was always pleased to help the Primitive Connection, and he was sure that they would hear something which was bound to bear good fruit. (Applause.) He bad great pleasure in asking Mr Fianna- gin to deliver his lecture on " Soul Saving as illustrated by my life." I The Lecturer, who was received with applause,thank- ed the chairman for his kind words. el Pc —8 was sorry he was not as well physically as he might be, but was comforted with the thought that Christ's strength lay in his human weakneee. He rejoiced in his work of saving souls and believed that when he saved a sinnncr he did more good to the world than if he were to win a Waterloo. A man or woman won to Christ was a higher achievement than winning a battle. The angels never sang after dread- ful human slaughter, but there was no doubt but their joy was intensified when any send was led to the Master. Some ministers allowed their soul-saving work to be confined to their public duties. But he found that outside the church and her services there were oppor- tunities in which they could kindly present their claims and win souls. When he commenced his ministry he was one of the church's most lawless workers. He knew nothing about church polity. He only knew God had saved him and that the world was perishing. He often got knocks for that was sometimes God's way of dealing with men. His way to win a man to a spiritual sense, to take the devilish lunacy out of him,was sometimes to break his bones so that no would cry out " Thou haat broken my bones." It might be figurative language but that aptly represented the beating of a man into goodness Thoroughness was his motto. Each should do his best in anything he put his band to. Though he (the speaker) commenced life at the bottom of a coal mine it was acknowledged that when be left as chief of five hundred miners, he was one of the most expert men be-- I fore the face of the coal. The first soul he saved gave ' him a taste of the highest and holiest blessing that was possible for the heart of a G3d or a man to feel. His thoroughness in things spiritual entailed a charge of madness from some of the cool philesophical men. But the truth was as Victor Hugo said, that the only mad- men in the world were men of moderation, Men who were lunatics for the cause of Christ ; these were the men who made reforms,purified reformations, journeyed into unknown worlds and prepared the way for feet to tread for civilizations that were following on behind. The Vicar of a certain parish once brought a policeman with handcuffs to take him to gaol for disturbing his parish with sieging and preaching. Since then the Free Churchmen in that parish, owing to the vicar's action had become stronger and a religious revival was the result of his madness. In his ministry he had always tried to deal with individuals singly. The salva- tion of the crowd depended on the one. Men had to be loved with goodness. The evangelist had to find the point of contact and from that point lead the sinner to higher things. If he had no faith they should believe for him. Many a man would be saved not through his own faith but through the faith of some one else. He asked them to go forward against the forces of evil. The milk votes of thanks concluded the meeting. In the afternoon a tea meeting was held which was largely attended. The flowing lscliee presided at the tables, Mesdames Brockleloank, Hardly, Parkinson, Jackson, Eyre, Cottrell, I Stubbs and Miss Lunt.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-10-17_WidnesExaminer_art0068" n="art0068">
        <head>RUNCORN NOTES.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN NOTES.</p>
        <p>[BY A CORRESPONDENT.]</p>
        <p>The dinner to reservists on Tuesday was in every respect a successful function. The fact that it was a town's dinner to the returned warriors accentu- ated the significance of the gathering. There was a thoroughly honest desire to greet the men with every feeling of good will. It really seemed very curious that neither the Vicar nor any of the "Cloth " were at the gathering. Runcorn has it ;I that Canon Wood abstained attending, on grounds which for the honour of all concerned had better not be made public. The earlier portion of Father Chambers' speech on the point that the Church of England was not clerically represented and that he supposed he had been chosen to respand to the "Clergy " as a "go between" left a distinctly unfavourable impression—not respecting Father Chambers—on the minds of those present. How- ever all honour to those who represented Runcorn at the war. May the loyalists of the town alwa ye present as united a front to the foe.</p>
        <p>Reference to our columns will show that a largely signed petition against the Education Bill has been despatched by Mr Walter Millington, secretary to the Free Church Council, to the Right lion A J Balfour, First Lord of the Treasury. After the Prime Minister's announcement of no compromise it is to be feared that but little good will result from the memorial. .1 can only urge all Free Churchmen to maintain their uncompromising atti- tude towards the measure irrespective of conse- quences.</p>
        <p>THE RESIILT OF DRINK.</p>
        <p>On Sunday night a man named John Tynan of Clay-lane, Burtonwood, was brought to Earlestown Police station in a cart having been found in Burtonwood in an unconscious condition. On making an examination a severe wound was found on his head from which blood had flown freely, and it was supposed that being drunk he had fallen, his head coming in contact with the curbstone. Dr Patterson was sent for and after dressing the wound the man was sent home in a cab.</p>
        <p>CRA WFORD'S WEAR CRACKERS</p>
        <p>CRISP AND CREADI7</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-10-17_WidnesExaminer_art0069" n="art0069">
        <head>IHE RETURNED SOLDIERS. Town's Dinner at Runcorn.</head>
        <p>IHE RETURNED SOLDIERS. Town's Dinner at Runcorn.</p>
        <p>On Wednesday evening at the Runcorn Drill Hall the postponed dinner, given by the town to Mr Samuel Taylor, J.P., and toe Ruacorn detachment from the seat of war was held amid a scene of festivity seldom witnessed in Runcorn. This dinner it will be remembered was to have taken place at the time of the Coronation but owing to the King's illness an indefinite postpone- ment had to be made. The town's committee however, kept the matter well in hand, and as the majority,if not all the reservists, had arrived from the front, Wednes- day was deemed a propituous moment for celebrating , the event. The Drill Hall presents d a ,ivery pretty lappearance strings of streamers having been hung from the roof which gave a distinctly picturesque appearance to the assembly. At the head of the table sat Mr S Taylor surrounded by the returned warriors while in the body of the hall sat the elite of the neighbourhood. Following an excellent dinner the customary loyal toasts were proposed amid a scene of enthusiasm after which Mr Posnett proposed the toast of "The Clergy of all denominations." In the course of a spirited speech,fre- quently punctuated with applause, Mr Posnett said no men had rendered more magnificent service to the country than the Army Chaplains who bodly had gone to the front. Their duties led them into the firing line, in the hospital and every place where the officer in commaod asked them to go. He had special pleasure in coupling the name of Father Chambers with the toast. He had met Father Chambers on many public occasions, and he considered him a good sample of an English Chaplain. (Applause). They mad a most vivid reccelaction of his predecessor. Dr Gastaldi,—(applause) —who was one of the few men who had the affection o every man, woman, and child in the town. (Applause). Father Chambers, in responding, said he was agree- ably eurprised at having been askei to respond to the toast. They knew that in Rancoin the Catholics were in a decided minority, which made him feel vary small indeed. He considered it therefore, a great honour to ' be asked to respond. He was afraid he was but a poor after dinner speaker for such occasionsu were not fre- quent enough for him to keep in practice. (Laughter.) When became in the room he looked for the Vicar and his many assistants, but found them not. And as he had not had the opportunity of conferring with the other clergy he did not know what to say on their behalf. Personally he always looked upon the other ministers as working towards a common end with the highest pos- sible motives. (Applause). One thing they ought to be particularly grateful for was the spirit of toleration that existed in every part of the British Empire. (Applause.) Though the Church of England was the State Church they had toleration for all religious belies. That, he thought, was something to be really proud of. he knew something of the work of the Chaplains at the front,and on behalf of them and the local clergy he had pleasure in responding to the toast. Mr VViswall proposed the next toast, the army, navy and the reserve forces. He hail often wondered he said, in looking over history whether the British soldier would , ever be called upon to defend the honour of the British ' Empire as in the days of Waterloo. The present war had shown conclusively he thought, that they could do and had done similar good service as in the past. When war was declared they only bad a handful of men in South Africa but these were sufficient to hold the enemy in check until reinforcements arrived. In the dark days of Colenzo the spirit of the army never faltered and at length pluck and determination had wen the dey. A word of praise was due to the handy men who so materially helped towards a successful issue at Lady- smith and elsewhere. They had shown that whatever danger threatened they were always reaay to maintain the honour of their country. The Volunteer, Reserve and Yeomanry forces bad won, by their heroic conduct, the honour and admiration of the world. They were especially proud that night of their own towns- men who had been to the front. (Applause,) He was happy to think that there had been contributed something like £1,792 towards the wives and families or those who had been to the war. There waS still a sum lin hand and this would be carefully looked after for the reservists' benefit. (Applause). He coupled the name of Captain Ashton with the toast. Captain Ashton, replying, said that during the last few years Englishmen had thought more about the forces than they bad previously done. Some of the criticisms passed on the Goveramont had borne fruit. They (the volunteers) had now to expend more time on their duties to attain the standard of efficiency • more time to their home lrills ; and to go into camp for a longer period than before. Their duties thus were more, ardu- ous than previously. But the Governmenz in expecting them to become more efficient should have supplied ' them with the power to carry out their instructions. The range of the detachment he had the honour to com- mand was over twenty miles away—a case similar to many battalions. To travel there the men had to 1030 time and money, A fortnight had also to be spent in camp and it was rather too much to expect them to give up time for training without any recompense. The least the Govern- ment could do was to recoup them for the loss sus- tained. To maintain a force to be efficient in time of 1 danger the Government should help the Volunteerlmoye- meat to the best of their ability. (Applause.) Mr Taylor rose to propose the toast of the ~vening " Our returned soldiers.'' He said that in 1900 the Government appealed to the Volunteers and the Yeoinany for assistance. In Runcorn a hearty response was made. Forty volunteered for the front—(applause) —but six were all that could be accepted. In 1901 another appeal was made and though twenty volun- teered but four were accepted. In February of the present year in response to another appeal fifteen volun- teered but five Only were called Out. He (Mr Taylor) thought that fore town like Runcorn to offer seventy fine Volunteers was something to be proud of:(App'ause.) If they took the United Kingdom through they would find Runcorn was not one whit behind any town. So long as they had volunteers to defend their country they had nothing to fear. The volunteers had opened the eyes of the world. (Applause.) Any European power would now think twice before threatening to attack England. The war had proved that a Britisher never knew when he was beaten and never gave in. He wasriglad they were celebrating that night the return of their soldiers who had come back sound in mind and limb. It must have been gratifying for them to know that their wives and families had been well looked after. He was pleased they were enjoying themselves so much and considered this was the grandest of meetings they had ever had in Runcorn. It was a towns dinner, he was happy to think, given in their honour. It was no - the work of the magistrates, the Urban Council, or ant</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-10-17_WidnesExaminer_art0070" n="art0070">
        <head>LORD NEWTON AND THE GAMING LAWS.</head>
        <p>LORD NEWTON AND THE GAMING LAWS.</p>
        <p>A Frank Confession.</p>
        <p>Considerable interest was centred in a Case heard before Lord Newton and the Stockport County magis- trates on Tharsday morning relative to the gaming laws. James S Hammond, licensee of the Queen's Arms Hotel, Cheadle, was summoned for permitting gambling on licensed premises. It was stated that the police visited the house and found fifteen men playing skittles for a prize, consisting of a China tea service. All the men had paid an entrance fee of sixpence. The defendant told the police that business was very bad and unless he bad attractions of that kind the house would not be worth keeping. I Lord Newton (to Mr Brown, who prosecuted) : Supposing a number of persons meet together and play an ordinary game of pool at a public-house, is that illegal ? Mr Brown : What is pool ? (Laughter.) Lord Newton : Billiards. I am afraid your knowledge is rather limited. (Laughter). Mr Brown : It would too illegal if for money or money's worth. Lord Newton . Then lam afraid I have committed many unlawful actions. (Laughter.) Mr Brown : Ping-pong is illegal if played for money or money's %coati. (Laughter.) Defendant pleaded ignorance of the law. Lord Newton said a technical offence had been committed, bat it was of rather a trivial nature. He rather sympathised with the defend- ant in the ignorance he had expressed with regard to the gaming laws, for having sat on a Betting Commission it was still anything but clear to him what was legal and what was illegal, and it was a thing many people found difficult to deal with. The defendant would have to pay the costs.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-10-17_WidnesExaminer_art0071" n="art0071">
        <head>CO-OPERATIVE BOYCOTT Meeting of Runcorn Members. Guarantee of £3OO.</head>
        <p>THE CO-OPERATIVE BOYCOTT Meeting of Runcorn Members. Guarantee of £3OO.</p>
        <p>A meeting of the members of the Runcorn Co-opera- tive Industrial and Provident Society was held in the Co-operative Hall, on Monday evening, to consider an important 'proposal contained in a circular from the Co-operative Defence Committee. Mr T Priestley pre- sided and was accompanied on the platform by Messrs J Shaw (secretary), T Cosier, W Rattles D Dyer, Withington, W Millington, J Sdragg, J Rollinson, T Watson, J Newall, J H Houghton and W E Dudley. There was a very representative attendance of members. The Chairman read the circular from the Co-operative Defence Committee, which stated that the Committee had decided to make a general appeal to all co-operative societies in England and Wales to raise a special fund for the purpose of carrying on a cam- paign against the boycotts which shall be defen- sive, aggressive or protective as may be required by the various circumstances in different localities. The Chairman went on to say that the committee had considered this matter very carefully and recommended the society to guarantee an amcunt equal to aboat £3OO. That was not a very great guarantee tor a society like theirs, and he hoped it would meet with the members' hearty sympathy and prompt and unanimous support. Mr Willan proposed a resolution confirming the com- mittee's recommendation,and expressing the willingness of the society to guarantee a further sum if necessary. He personally, was very gratified with the amount recommended. He thought a good deal more would have been asked for. Mr Lamb seconded. Be thought it was high time they did something, and he hoped every member would agree to the resolution. Mr Cosier supported and said that Accrington Society, I a society a little bigger than their own, had guaranteed £5OO, with a promise that if anything more was needed it would be forthcoming. The secretary of the com- mittee and the secretary of the Co-operative Union, recommended about Is per member,and that in Runcorn's case came out to about £3OO. Mr Lloyd said that this boycott was directed against the freedom of the subject, and when the freedom of the subject was attacked it was high time they should be up and doing. Even £lOO,OOO was not a big matter so far as the Co-operative Union was concerned. The Chairman said they were not asked to pay the money now. For tbe present it was only a guarantee. Mr Dudley said the encouragement of the meeting would be an incentive to the committee to go forward. With twelve stalwart men in front, and 6,000 members behind they need fear no one. They bad put their hand to the plough and asked for £lOO,OOO. Let it be known that the amount had been given and that if further was ' needed that society was prepared to do its share by in- creasing its guarantee. The resolution was put to the meeting, and unani- mously carried. It was understood that in the event of a further guarantee being necessary another meeting would be summoned. THE FIGHT AT ST HELENS. The struggle between the St Helens traders' and the local co-operative society continues with unabated vigour, and the latter society have decided to try to secure representation on the Town Council. A meeting of the Private Traders' Defence Association was held on Wednesday night, the result being that a committee was formed to support Councillors Grundy and Foote, retiring members, who are being oppoEed by co-operative candidates.</p>
        <p>Lancashire and Cheshire Entomological Society. The opening meeting of the winter session was held in the Royal Institution, Colquitt-street, and was largely attended. In the unavoidable absence of the president (Mr S 3 Capper), Dr John Cotton, F.E.S. (of St Helens), occupied the chair. Messrs Oscar Whittaker (Bolton), James Roland Charnley, F.E.B. (Preston), 4. Gi Wallington (Warrington), J I Lea, and J Jervis Richardson (Liverpool), were elected members of the society. Several presenta- tions to the library were announced by the secre- tary. It VMS decided to hold the next meeting in Liverpool on November 10, and that a meeting be held in Warrington on DE-tember 8, provided the secretaries were able to make necessary arrange- ments for the same. A large and interesting dis- play of insects was shown by members.</p>
        <p>B 13ST BOOTS Fon ALL.—UNDRIt THE CLOCH.—Advt FOR GOOD TWINES—WILLiam BEswicg, 21, Buttermarket-street, Warrington.—Advt. WEDDING PRESENTS in largest variety.-3. S. GAR- LARD,lroamonger,sl,s6 &amp; 55,Br1dge-st,Warrington.—Aclet, tor Ja 4,11:1k I 1111:111-CLUS MINIMAL AIN 13,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-10-17_WidnesExaminer_art0072" n="art0072">
        <head>EARLESTOWN &amp; NEWTON</head>
        <p>EARLESTOWN &amp; NEWTON</p>
        <p>PRIMITIVE METHODIST CHAPEL, EARLESTOWN. On Sunday the annual special services in aid of the Trust funds were held in the above chapel. Ser- mons were preached morning and evening by the Rev T Kynaston of Ellesmere Port, and in the after- noon by the Rev Si Cook, resident minister. Special hymns were sung by the choir. RETURN OY A LOCAL RESERVIST. On Monday night Private H Bonney, of High- I street, Newton, attached to the 3NI King's Rsyal Rifles, returned home and was accorded a hearty welcome by a number of friends and acquaintances. He has been in South Africa practically since the beginning of the war. WARGRAVE CHURCX CHOIR. A social and dance in connection with the above choir took place on Friday evening in the Wargrave schoolroom. About 24 couples were present and dancing was spiritedly indulged in to music sup- plied by Mr Peplow's band, Mr It Carson officiated as M.C. EARLESTOWN THURSDAY FOOTBALL CLUB. A smoking concert in connection with the above club was held at the Railway Hotel,Earlestown, on Thursday evening last. Mr 11 J Longton presided, the vice-chairman being Mr 11 Ball. Songs were rendered during the evening by members of the Birkenhead Post Office football team, Messrs H Houghton, E Perry, S Henderson. 3 Gibbons, and W Parkinson. Messrs Appleton and Walsh gave one or two comic selections. Convivial speeches were delivered during the evening, reference being made to the charitable work performed by the club MEMORIAL COTTAGE HOSPITAL BAZAAR. A social gathering of the workers and others in connection with the late open air bazaar was held in the Town Hall, Earlestown, on Thursday even- ing. There were about 100 persons present. Danc- ing was indulged in with much spirit to music supplied by Mr J H Lewis and Mr J Muscrott,songs being rendered at intervals. Messrs L Cutts and H Duke officiated as M.C.'s POLICE CASES. At the Earlestown Police Station on Monday, before Mr W Collingwood, Mary Ellen ReynDlds, of St. Helens, was charged with being drunk and dis- orderly in Earlestown on Saturday night, and in default of paying a flne of 2561 and costs was sent to gaol for 7 days.—Thomas Bassett, an old offen- der, was charged with being drank and disorderly , in Viaduct-street,Earlestown,on Saturday night, and was sent to gaol for 14 days in default of paying a fine of lOs and costs. EARLESTOWN GOOD TEMPLA.RS. The national body of Good Templars.—The Grand Lodge of England,—having arranged for the week commencing October 13th to be called the" Child- ren's Week ' and special meetings to be held in the interests of the juvenile templars, a meeting was held in the Co-operative Hall, Earlestown on Mon- day, when a special service was arranged to be carried out. Bro George Clarke presided, and with Bro George E V arburton, E Newman, T Rose, and Sister Mrs J E Warburton, took part in the service Hymns were very heartily sang by the large muster of j uveniles and adults present. Several songs and ' recitation were also given and a most earnest appeal was made by Bro George E Warburton in the clos- ing address. The whole service was of a very impressive character and could not fail to do good. The Lodge was afterwards opened and arrange- ments were completed for a visit to the Wigan Lodge next week, and for the 30th anniversary ser- vices of the Lodge. Two candidates were readmitt- ed. A large number afterwards sat down to enjoy a potato pie supper that had been prepared by Sisters Mrs Hatton, Williams, and Rowley, and Bro Hatton. Votes of thanks closed a vary interesting evening's proceedings.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-10-17_WidnesExaminer_art0073" n="art0073">
        <head>ST: HELENS BUILDING ACCIDENT</head>
        <p>A ST: HELENS BUILDING ACCIDENT</p>
        <p>Finding of the Jury.</p>
        <p>Mr Brighouse, county coroner, held the inquest at Peaeley Cress, St Helens, on Thursday, on Farringtou Walter Dean, aged twenty-thr e,of 190, Westfield-street, who died in the St Helens Hospital on Tuesday noon after being injured by the falling of the wall of a two storey building near the old Phognia Vaults,Canal-streA, which was being demolished. Mr E W Swift appeared for Mr Fred Brown, builder. Henry Brown, foreman, said that at the time of the accident they were taking down a two-storey house and shop. They had taken off the roof, and cut the wall at the corners in order that a rope might be put round the chimney and the wall pulled into the building, so that it would smash up the joista and the whole of the inside. Witness was in the middle of toe etreet watchiag the will. A gust of wind Came, and witness saw the wall going, and shouted to deceased. Dean im- mediately jumped off the floor that he was on, into the shop below, with the intention of running out of the front door, but the wall caught him before he could get clear. At the time the wall began to move, deceased was pulling the rope up, with the ob- ject of putting it round the chimney. Dean was got out within five miautes,but over forty-five minutes elapsed before tne horse-ambulance came to convey him to the hospital. They telephoned for the ambulance,and then sent a man on a bicycle to hurry them up. The Coroner : Who horses the ambulance ? Mr Swift : The St Helens Carriage Company. Witness, in answer to Mr Swift, said that if Dean had acted on his warnieg at once, instead of stopping to warn Burns, he might possibly have got clear. They had cut the ends of the wall away, but had not sup- ported the wall it all. The Coroner said that under those conditions they should not have got him to goup a ladder leaning against such a wall to put a rope round the chimney. Juryman : It is a poor wall that will tumble over</p>
        <p>like that. John Burns, labourer, of 57, Water•street, said he had assisted in the demolition of property in Bridge-streetokc, for the past &amp;lc months. Be thought the wind and rain caused the wall to fall. Witness was knocked down by the fall, and his leg was hurt. It was otten difficult to pull a wall over. A day or two before thay had to get about ten men more than were on the j)b to , pull one of the Pbceiix walls over. 1 The jury returned a verdict of " Ace,idental death," and presented that the foreman omitted to take the necessary precautions so as to have the wall taken down in a workmanlike manner. Mr Swift said when the wall was a high °tie they put up stays, but not when it was a small one like the end of the shop in question.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-10-17_WidnesExaminer_art0074" n="art0074">
        <head>Warrington County Petty Sessions,</head>
        <p>Warrington County Petty Sessions,</p>
        <p>WEDNESDAY—Before Captain Withington On the chair) and _</p>
        <p>Messrs White and Comber. UNHAPPY MARRIED LlFE.—Richard Walters, master shingler. Liverpool-road, was summoned for persistent cruelty to his wife, Clara Etty Walters, whereby she was compelled to live apart from him.—Mr Steel reported for the complainant and Mr Browne defended- Mr Browne said his client appeared that morning by courtesy, because the sum- mons had not been properly served, He (Mr Browne) had been acting for both parties for three jiears, and in tiaa Interests of the man himself. and after consulting PC Dug- dale, who lived near the parties, he thought it was in defen- dant's interests that there should be a separation. They were• only waiting for the new Act to come Into force. so that de- fendant might take out a summons for separation. Although defendant denied legally that there had been any persisi eat cruelty, he (Mr Browne) should advise the man to consent to an order for separation and the pay ment of alimony. There had already been three deeds o separation, but the parties had gone together again and in his opinion they would do so again. The man was 47 years of age ; the woman was 23. They had been married only three years.—Mr Steel said the sumraens had been duly issued by a Magistrate of the Court, a: d it had been legally servedif in these circumstances the defendant was not bound to appear he did not know who was. Mr. Browne : The police knew nothing about the summons.—Mr Steel continu- ing said it was the siefendast who was the drunken person and not the complainant.inComplainant was a clean and tidy person.—Mrs Walters said her husband was a master ehingler and earned £4 or £5 a week,—Mr Browns: Why his wages last week but one only Came to RI 18s 3d. I have a works certificate for that amount.—The Bench granted a separation order, and ordered the defendant to pay lOs a week towards the maintenance of his wife. THEFT AT HATDoCE.—William Burrows was charged o remand with stealing, some lime between September Ist and October 11th, from the yard of Messrs Richard Evans and Co., Havdock, a quantity (of braes, lead, wire, and tools, valued 85. He pleaded guilty. The evidence showed that the prisoner rented a hut near the colliery of Messrs Richard Evans and Co. On information being given to the police. this was searched and the articles mentioned in the charge were found. The prisoner was fined 108 and co3ts, or four- eon days in default.</p>
