      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-10-17_WidnesExaminer_art0080" n="art0080">
        <head>BEFORE the MAGISTRATES</head>
        <p>BEFORE the MAGISTRATES</p>
        <p>A TRAM A SREN4ER'S V4OLISIS ACT.</p>
        <p>fnehlt Lewers el 333.1Cnntefor4-road was anmronnea under the Corporation livAlaws at the warrington County Boronch Pollee Court on Friday. for leaving a tram ear whilst in motioa—Mr Stanley Wilson prosecuted and stated that on Wedaesdav, the 'Pith September, the defendant was a Passenger by the 9.35 p.m. tram from Rviands-street to Latchford and when onposito Vi-teria Park he made an attempt to leave The car. The conductor told him be was going to step at the next stopping plane and wont so far as to seize hint by the collar After the tram car had gone a little further. the Vmductor turned round, wherenpon , the defendant jumpel of the ear and was thrown with veld violence to the ground and rendered unconsciens. That prosecution was not taken to mulish the defendant he had suffered enough already and had been under the doctor's core for three weeks. But the committee thought the ease ought to he brought before the Court, more ail a warning to the pohlle than as a punish- ment to the defendant. fle (Mr Wilson) therefore propmed to withdraw the case en the defendant paying the costs,which he was quite avreeahle to do.—This course was adopted. A SCIT7F:a OUTSIDE TIE POLICE ST/TION _ _ _</p>
        <p>Mr Steel., solicitor, had a complaint to lay before the Bench at the acuaty Bormigh Pollee Court On Friday arising oat of a ease which was down for hearing. A client of his named Mrs Wallis. of Bostock-street. who had issned a STIMMOns egainst her husband for pemistent eruelty was coming to the Court that morning for the pnrpose of airing evidence, in company with one of the children, when her husband and two women lay in waiting for her at the door of the Court bongo. An attempt was made to take the child from Mrs Wallis and in the scuffle which enaned she was assaulted by her husband. Re (Mr Steele) said that a charge of aSsault be preferred against Wallis. If witnesses eenld not be protected in coming to the Court it would be a very serious matter. He (Mr Steele) witnessed the aasan't, and he was sure the Bench would do all they possibly could to nrotect witnesses coaling to the Cnurt.—Mr Brcwre. solicitor, said the defendant came to him three years ago with reference to his wife. He had already drawn op two separation orders He had been told by P.C. Bligdale that Ms. Wallis was not a fit seaman to have the custody of the Child. It was the defendant's intention to take nut a sum- mons under thanes.. Act and obtain the custody ef the child. Mr Steele : That does not justify the assault this morning.— The Phairman (Alderman Roberts): If the fasts areas stated WA will deal with it, at once.—A summons charging Wallis with unlawfully heating his wife was then signed and the ease proceeded with.—after bearing the evidence. the Chair- man said the magistrates were quite satisfied that an assault bed been emamltted wed seeing that It was of a character calculated to interfere with the proper course of justice they could net allow it to pass without notice. There appeared to have been Enrie misunserstanding as to advice which had been given to the defendsat by a Con- stable outside the Borengh with reference to seizing the child and be Same extent his conduct might be excusable. The magistrates thought the case would be satisfactorily dealt with by a fine of 111 including costs.— The charge of persistent (smelts, was not proceeded with. as Mr Browne pointed nut that the Court had no jurisdiction as the patties were living outside the Borough 0 the time.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-10-17_WidnesExaminer_art0081" n="art0081">
        <head>MINOR OASES. --- Warrington Borough Court.</head>
        <p>MINOR OASES. --- Warrington Borough Court.</p>
        <p>SATURDAY. —"Warm Messrs T Birchall.iia .tha chair), J Tr</p>
        <p>Hayward. and IL T Fairslough. IN,WLENGE RawARDRIS —Jelin Williams a tramn last from Liverpool, was sentenced to seven days imprisonment for begging in Foundry-street on Friday night—as he was being removed to the cells prisoner made use of very disrespectful expressions towards the magistrates, wile ordered him to be brought back, and doubled the sentence THVOITIIRMAN AISToN/SHED.—WilliamlLawlees was charged for making use of obscene langnage in Buttertnarket-street, on Friday evening —P 0 Clough proved the ease.—Mr Birch's!), in Caine the defen-hmt 5s said the abominable language re.ee in F7arringtee somewhat astonished him. He was pessing a man in Sankey street the other day, and he swore feur times in a second or two. He rebuked him and asked hint why he did so, but the man merely looked sur- prised and slid nothing. MONDAY -Before Hr F Monks (In the chair), Cauncillor Smethurst and Messrs Bird:mil, Hayward, ffright and Thornton. AN HABITUAL DRUNICARD.—Jetue Wilfred Deleoze, 30, BuilPy-ttreet. was charged with haviag been drunk and dis- orderly in Butterrnarket-street and with refusing to quit the licensed premises of the Higher Angel Inn on Saturday afternoon .—P S Hollingwerth stated that be was called to the Higher Angel Inn about four o'cleck by the landlord. Prisoner was drunk and refused to leave the Premises. Wit- ness sent fir P 0 Gluey and prisoner was ejected. He fell down in the street and refusel to go away. On being taken Into custody he became very violent and made use of very fat* language.— Mr Perdue. the licensee, said prisoner cams into the house drunk. and Mrs Perdue refused to serve him. He would not ge obit and the sergeant was sent for.—The Chief Constable said that was prisoner's 28th appearance and he had been before the court four times this year. He had qualified for an Inebriates, home, but as the Ohairman had said before, he was an able bedied man and if he were sent to an inebriates' home he would have to be kept at, the expense of the tax payers. fan One oeeasion he signed the pledge and promised to keep it for twelvernout los He kept it fee about twelve minutes, as it Look him that time to get to the nearest public house. He was the meet persistent and inveterate drunkard in the town —The Bench fined the prisoner 5s and costs—The Chief Constable pointed out that there were two charges against the prissier.—Mr G P White (magistrates' clerk) said that strictly speaking prisoner committed only one offence.—The Chief Constable said he would not press the matter if the Clerk's ruling WAS against him. SMASHING A WIND(' W.—Geerge Carter, of no settled abode, was charged with having been drunk and disorderly in Butternsarket.sfreet, on Saturday evening, and further with committing wilful damage to four panes of glass, to the extent of 68,at the house of Imily Carter, his wife.—P.C. Woods gave evidence proving the drunkenness, and Mrs Carter said that her husband, who lived apart from her, wee constantly annoying her. On Saturday be threw two bricks through the window.—The prisoner was fined 5s and eoste ter the first offence and for breaking the windows was fined thia to include the amount of the How • P•UPIER Sgirsti Zl5 T•sx.43olls Platt, an Inmate of the Workhouse, was charged with refusing to per- form the task set him by the master on Saturday—John Constable, the porter, said prisoner Was set to piek 41155 of Planta. - He picked one ponndland AS he was going to dinner at noon, threw a pound ever the boundary wall on to the, railway embankment—Corroborative evidence having been wen, prisoner wee committed to gaol for 21 days. DlSoltrtifnLT CONIMICT.—Thetnaa Mitchell. 7, Litherlaners- yard, Cecelia Mitchell. his wife, and Mary Kenny, 2 Lither, Wars-yard, Were summoned for committing a breach Of the peas* eta October 4th, by behaving in a disorderly manner.— Sergeant lihnith stated that at half-past twelve on the Sun-</p>
        <p>day morning in queation he beard a disturbance in 'Caber- land's-yard, Me proceeded there in company with P.O. Cooper and found the defendants shouting. The male de - fondant, who pleaded not guilty, m+de use ef very bad language —Defendants were bound over to keep the peace Thomas Mitchell to pay 10a costs and the other defendants 71 each. No 800 tact/mt.—Margaret Kelly, 65, Foundry-street was summoned for keeping a deg without having a licence on the 6th of October.—P.O. Cooper stated that on the date named he saw a boy who had been bitten by a deg, and after haring him taken to a chemist's to get the wound dressed he aseertainea that the dog was kept by the defendant who bad no licence.—The defendant stated that the deg did not belong to her It came to the house and she allowed it to stop there.—She was fined Es 6d.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-10-17_WidnesExaminer_art0082" n="art0082">
        <head>Runcorn Petty Sessions. BIONDAT—Before MetifitS G L Davies (in the 'heir) and A R Norma.</head>
        <p>Runcorn Petty Sessions. BIONDAT—Before MetifitS G L Davies (in the 'heir) and A R Norma.</p>
        <p>TRAVELLING WITHOUT ALIGHT—George Duckett,Regest- street, was summoned for travelling without a light on the 2nd October in Monghland-lane. P.O. Wylie proved the case and the defendant was fined Is 6d and 88 6d costs. CONTRAVENTION OF THE POGO MUZZLING ACT —Thenaas Gregory was charged with allowing his deg to be at large without the owners name on the cellar.—Sergeant Cooper proved the case and the defendant was fined 2s 6d• Teinowfwei STONES.—A small boy aged 12 named Edgar Davies was summoned for throwing stones in Frederisk- street.—P 0 Bowyer proved the case and the defendant was fined Os 6d. DRUNK Ann DBORDERLY.—Thomas Mahon, Loch-street, was fined 10s including cos+.s on the evidenee of Sergeant Gregory for being drunk and disorderly on Saturday night. POOR RATE FOR eLIFTOI,—A poor rate order was signed by the magistrates far is 2d in the £ to realize £2OO 14s 7d.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-10-17_WidnesExaminer_art0083" n="art0083">
        <head>STOCKTON HEATH AND THE 1 RAMWAY SERVICE. Proposed Terms With the Corporation.</head>
        <p>STOCKTON HEATH AND THE 1 RAMWAY SERVICE. Proposed Terms With the Corporation.</p>
        <p>We understand that the lifficulties which existed between the Stockton heath Parish Council and the Warrington Corporation with regard to the pro. posed light railway scheme have been practically removed and that there is every probability of the Corporation taking up the Light Railway Order and continuing the Wilderepool tramway to the centre of Stockton Heath. A meeting of the Stockton Heath Parish Council has been summoned to consider certain proposals which have been made to facilitate the introduction of the Corpora tion tramway system into Stockton Heath. Additional importance bas been attached to an early decision by the receipt of an intimation from the Board of Trade that if the Corporation would</p>
        <p>not take up the order tbe Board would grant the 'original order to tbe promoters of the North wich Light Railway. In that proposed order, the com- pany intended to construct a single line without any passing places and without any widening of the road except near the Mulberry Trae. The Stockton Reath people would also be called upon to pay a fare to the Company for conveying them from Victoria Square to Staffard-road, where they would have to dismount in order to get on the Corporation cars and pay an additional fare to the Corporation.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-10-17_WidnesExaminer_art0084" n="art0084">
        <head>DEAFNESS CURED BY A NEW METHOD.</head>
        <p>DEAFNESS CURED BY A NEW METHOD.</p>
        <p>The Editor esf the " Journal for the Deaf" has taken an active interest in the treatment of ear diseases by external application and to make known the success achievel has devoted a special issue to a full description ef the new method. With the special number is puislished a Supplement containing. the details of upwards of 40 cases cured by the treatment during last month. Any person whr, is interested can obtain a free copy of the " Journal "and Supplement by addressing the Editor, 72, Regent's Park-road, London, N.W. Each Jenne( is acentnpanied by a Patient'a Report Form which enables applicants to receive full particulars for applying the treatment at home. Any advice by correapondencs is absolutely free.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-10-17_WidnesExaminer_art0085" n="art0085">
        <head>CHRISTIAN ENDEAVOURERS.</head>
        <p>CHRISTIAN ENDEAVOURERS.</p>
        <p>The Warrington and District Union.</p>
        <p>Opposition to Sunday Trams.</p>
        <p>A mein Er of tha Exesatiye 'Llemmittee of the War- rington and District Christian Ealeawour Unioa was held in the Itoarnanu-1 Sehools the other evenine and was well attended. The President (Rey Wilsos e Lvint‘n) occusied the chair. The Rev E T Reed (ex- , president of :the Union), who recently vacated his charle of Emmanuel Church,was presented with a hand- some kit ha, bearinz the initials " E T.R.," together with a irentleman's dreosinv-ease, subscribed for by the variods societies nonnected with the Union. GENER kL 8U31NE33.</p>
        <p>A circnlar from tho Lancashire an -1 Cheshire C.E. Foieration, detailing the proposed work of their Conn- -61, was read and commented upon. The Rev Wilson Eccles (president) and Miss Vutels Taylor (secretary) wore chosen as the Union's repre- sentatives on the Federation Council, and Mr Austin, et Earlestown, was also elected as a substitute. It was decided to hold the annual Convention in Warrinzton early next year.</p>
        <p>' OPPOSITION TO SUNDAY TRAMS. It was unanimously resolved thst the SECRETARY be requested to send a letter to the Town (Mork protesting against the Suaday train service ; and also that a latter be sent to the Free Church Ceuncil. urging them te take immediate aggressive stops in the matter. PRESENTATION TO THE REV 11. T. REED. The PRESIDENT in akieg the presentati•ra tie the Rev E T Reed, said they we're all extremely sorry tho necessity. had arisen for Mr Read to leave theca. Hie 13811:18 had always been mentioned in their meetings with the deepest roennot. He did not think any minister in Warrington had taken such an importamt part as Mr Reed had in the welfare of Christianity in the termand ha had undoubtedly given he hesrt to the work. The societies and Union owed a great deal to Mr Reed. He (Mr E Eccles) trusted Mr Reel wculd have goad health and prosperity its the Master's work. The Rev J S HIICUIES said it was a source of great grief to him to know that Mr Reed had given up his work in connection with the town. He thought ea his part, that no one had taken such a bold stand for righteousness as Mr Reed. speakici., with regard to him ad a Christian minister as well as a good citizen. They Imew very well Mr Reed had had to hear obloquy and petty perseoution, but be lad been a good soldier of JesslS Christ. They were all lorry he was leaving thsm As a union they wisked hien every aimless in his work, and trusted he would he as useful as a ruissioner, 5,9 iss had been useful in the consolidating and developing wet-kilns bad had in the twee. (Applause.) . . .</p>
        <p>The Rev E T REED, who well received with a hearty eutburet of applaute, said he felt almost overwhelmed by their kindness. While president of the Union it had always been a great del2ht to him tit preeitle over their Cafhorings, as the strictest harmony had always pre- vailed. Whoa he looked bulk ever the past years, he felt in his heart that it had been a privilege to have had a share in the work of the Uoion—it had always been a privileoe to do so, because they were always loyal. Thoug,h they came from different branches of the Church ef Jesus Christ they all felt and worked as one. He would like to take that opportunity of personally thanking Mr Huehee for his belpfuloese. When the painful tirtri cause for him to leave Warrin 'too, one of the first letters of sympathy he had received was from Mr Hughes. R-eantly he (Mr Reed) had been spending his time in Norfolk, travelling in a caravan, as a gipsy, and he looked back with pleasure upon the many oppor- tunities he had had of presenting the Gospel to hun- dreds of men, women, and children. He felt thankful to the f-leads who had barns him in prayer. No did net wish to take up more of their time that evening, except to say how much he appreeieted the beautiful present be had reosived. They would be of the greatest possible ureic° te him, aid he would ever cherish, esteem, and regard them, as the gifts were promoted by loving hearts. The nteeting elesed with the singing of" God be with you till we meet again," and the Benedietion.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-10-17_WidnesExaminer_art0086" n="art0086">
        <head>rriE EXAIVIINE_R FRIDAY OCTOBER 17 1902</head>
        <p>rriE EXAIVIINE_R FRIDAY OCTOBER 17 1902</p>
        <p>SEQUEL TO THE WELL-DIVING</p>
        <p>FATALITY AT WIN NICK.</p>
        <p>Damages Against a Contractor at Warrington.</p>
        <p>Before Judge Bowen Rowlands, K.C., at th Warrington Counq Court on Thursday, last week Margaret Graham, a widow residing in Lady-lane, Croft, brought an action against George Lawson- well sinker and contractor, to recover ..C3OO corn pensation for the loss of her son—Mr A Browne appeared for the applicant and Mr Stuart Brown, barrister, (instructed by Messrs Hill,Dickinson, and Co., Liverpool) for the respondent.</p>
        <p>In openiiag ti:e case Mr Browne said tho applicant' son, Alexander Graham. was engaged at the new water- works at Croft for the purpose of sinking wells and completing the works. He was employed by Mr George Lawson, the respondent, as a foreman, and there was r no one above him at the works except the engineer who merely attended on behalf of the Water Committee. I Graham was the sole support of bis raother and appli- sant therefore chimed the maximum sum £3OO in conse- quence of hie death whilst following his employment at respondent's works. Deceased was left for seven weeks in sole charge of the works—Mr Laerson'e son and respondent's manager occasionally coming over. When respondent's men had completed number one well which was 150 feet deep the Corporation began to put down the pumping plant. It was discovered that a portion of scaffolding used in its construction had been left in th well, and this prevented the plant being put down. A was necessary that a diver should go down in order to remove the scaffolding and deceased was informed by the borough water engineer that he must send a diver down at once as the matter was so urgent. It was a well-known fact that we were within two or three hours of being absolutely without water in Warrington: A diving dress was sent by Mr Lawson from somr other place but no diver came, and the matte was so urgent that deceased went down tbe well for the purpose of removing the obstruction Deceased went down the well on a previous occasion after receiving a telegram telling him not to do so. He telezraphei to the respondent telling him that he has been t the bottom of the well and asking it he might proceed with the work there. Respondent replied Be certain to incur no risk. May proceed if all safe." Oa the date of the fatality, June 26th, there was no one to advise Graham as to any risk as he was the head man under Mr Lawson. He went down in the diver's dress,Mr Barren, the resident water engineer, bolding the life- line. He died the following morning from asphyxia. Mrs Graham saw Mr Lawson and asked him about com- pensation and be replied that he would see what could be done. He added that deceased was insured. She subsequently consulted him (Mr Browne) and a letter was written to Mr Lawson asking for the name of the company with which deceased was insured. Mr Lawson replied that the company was the Law Accident Incur- ance Society, Glasgow, and that company was com- municated with. No reply whatever had been received. Mr Stuart Brown said deceased had been in the employ of respondent over three years and his earn- kegs amounted to £284. The claim was amended to that amount.</p>
        <p>Nield M Barron, the resident engineer •at the Croft waterworks, said deceased was a timekeeper in the employ of the respondents. Prior to the date of the accident he had bad sole'supervision of the works. There were 118 feet of water in the well at the time of the accident. It became necessary to send down a diver to remove the scaffolding and Mr Lawson provided a diving dress. It was Mr Law- son's place to prnvid3 a diver. Deceased went down the well and witness understood that he had been told by his employer to go down. Deceased had been down many times previously and had come up safely. Witness held the life line and deceased came to the sur- face after a laps, of 35 minutes. His helmet was taken off and he sat down and began to talk as usual about the work. Be told witness about the hardness of the material he was trying to remove and witness suggested that he should cease diving operations and send for a " grab " with which to remove the material. Deceased agreed with this and after some conversation rose to go to the office and wire for a grab. On stepping upon the girders he suddenly became ill. He was carried to the office and witness and others did all they could for him by rubbing and bathing him. A doctor was sent for and after being medically treated deceased was removed home. Witness saw him put to bed and when he left the house deceased was much better. Next morning, however, he died. Deceased had been down the well five times on the day in question. The first time he was down 15 minutes the second 20 minutes ; the third 35 minutes ; the fourth 45 minutes ; and the fifth, 35 minutes.</p>
        <p>Mr Stuart Brown said he should submit that the natural consequence of deceased iping in such a great d.pth of water for such a long time and then coming up rapidly was caisson disease. He died from that disease and not from a p,runal injury caused by aa accident within the meaning of the Act. Dr James Bennett said he was called to the deceased on the night of Jane 25th, and found him suffering from elision disease,which was due to an excessive solu- tion of atmospheric gases in the blood. He attended the deceased for two and a half hours that night, and Graham died the following morning. The cause of death I was asphyxia. The excessive solution of atmospheric gases in the blood was due to atmospheric pressure °tweed by being at the bottom of the well. No evidence was called for the defence, Mr Stuart Brown submitting several legal arguments. In the first place he pointed out that there was no evidence that any mechanical power was used, and therefore the works were not Engineering works. He also contended that the respondent was not the undertaker of the building and other work which was being done at the place in question. He did not undertake the construction, alteration or repairing of the whole of the work ; in fact he was not even working at the well at the time. The only thing being done was that Graham OM diving for the benefit of the Corporation. It was also sub- mitted that deceased did not die as the result of an accident or injury. When he came to the surface he appeared to be perfectly wall. Mr Browne contended that the place was a factory within the meaning of the Act, there being a mortar mill driven by steam. Mr ll,vivon was tie undertaker of a well in the factory. Moreover the deceased was doing work in or about a building which was more than thirty feet high and in the construction of which scaffold- in.v. had been employed. _ _</p>
        <p>His Honour held that the deceased died as the result of an accident arising whilst following his employment and that Mr Lawson was the undertaker of the work. He gave judgnaent for the full amount-228 —with wee. Stay of execution for fourteen days was granted in order that notice of appeal might bs given.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-10-17_WidnesExaminer_art0087" n="art0087">
        <head>Warrington P.S.A.</head>
        <p>Warrington P.S.A.</p>
        <p>There was a large attendance at the quarterly open meeting of the society which was held in the Co-operative Hall. There was a large attendance of members and friends. Councillor W Roberts presided, and distributed over 200 books to mem- bers, fir regular attendance. and congratulated the members on their removal to a larger roes. He urgdd them to more earnest work for the benefit ef their fellows. During the afternoon Mr Williams sang very sweetly two solos : "The Chorister " and "Tears," and Mr W Woods rendered two solos.</p>
        <p>A Chemist's Sad End.</p>
        <p>Last Friday afternoon Mr J C Bate, coroner, held an inquest at the Temperance Hall, Norley, an the body of Samuel Robinson, chemist, Norley-lane, aged 64 years. The evidence slowed that deceased left home on Saturday merning,but did not return. Inquiries failed to ascertain'Lis whereabouts until Wednesday raorning,when his dead body was found in a field, about a nano from where he resided. There were no marks of violence upon it. Dr Smith, Weayerham, who made a post-mortem examination, found the liver very in ue h enlarged and expressed the opinion that death was doe to heart failure, accelerated by drink. A verdict to this effect was recori•Ad.—The deceased for many years was exceedingly well known througheut Northwich and district, he very successfully carry- ing on the business of a chemist and druggist in High-street. He left the town from 15 to 20 years are, and since then has resided at Fredsham, ulti- mately taking up hie residence at Norley.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-10-17_WidnesExaminer_art0088" n="art0088">
        <head>HARVEST FESTIVALS.</head>
        <p>HARVEST FESTIVALS.</p>
        <p>WORKING MEN'S. MISSION, BANK QUAY.</p>
        <p>The harveat services in connection witia- the above place of worship were held het week, the report being accidentally omitted from our prvieus issue. The chapel was very tastefully decorated by Mrs Millington, Mrs Edwards, Miss Dobbins, Miss 8 Ball, superintended by Mrs Ashley and Miss Bunch. The preacher for the day was Mr G Huntera of Leigh, who took far his text in the morning " The grace of our Lord Jesus Christ be with you all." In the afternoon the choir ably rew- dered the sacred cantata "The Lord of the Harvest." The solos were taken by Miss Birch, Miss Oakley, Miss IFolen, Mr 'lanai!, and Mr Millington. The chairman for the afternoon meeting was Councillor W Roberts.— On Monday evening a fruit banquet was held in the echeolroom and was very well attended. Mr T Dale was chairman and a very enj Iyable evening was spent. Mr Hassan and Miss Foden sang a duet, Miss Peden a eels, Mr J Hannan gave a song, and Mr J Hampson reeited twice. Mr Gr' Dale contributed a humoroua reading. Refreshments were provided.</p>
        <p>SIRE r TON CHfIRCIEI. The harveetthanksgivine neroices were held on Sunday. The church had been beautifully decorated with fruit, flowerJ and plants by several parishioners end frienc:e. Mr Lyon lent many handsome plants. Special anthems were rendered by the choir and organiet. namely, "I will magnify thle "(N Churchill) and "I will lift up mine eyes unto the bill" (Clarke Waitefiald). The orPacher morning and evening was the Rev J P Racers, formerly vicar of Marton, and in the afternoon the Rev HoHen, vicar of Antrobus, occupied the Puloit• The offertories on behalf of the ()noir ansountei to £l.l 1o:4d. It Wag a source of pleasure to see the Vicar back in &amp;mesh for the morale!: service after his sharp and try- ing illopee, elrhmze be felt unequal to attending the afterepon and evening servic,s.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-10-17_WidnesExaminer_art0089" n="art0089">
        <head>TEN YEARS OF PAIN.</head>
        <p>TEN YEARS OF PAIN.</p>
        <p>lUric acid Pains, Rheumatism Sciatica, Colt, Back- ache, and Cramp cannot exist alongside Dedd's Kidney Pills. These painful truub!es are caused by the presence of uric acid in the blood, a poison which should be removed by the kidneys. As Dodd's Kidney Pills act directly on the kidneys and make them strong and well. they are always an infallible cure. Mr. Edward Baker, of 56, Borneo's-street, Ipewich, ' was a great sufferer through um acid poisoning. On September 22nd, 1902, he wrote saying that he had suffered for the past ten years. The presence of uric 'laid in the blood had caused him a grnat deal of pain, often making it impossible to attend to his business. He was troubled with frequent attacks of rheumatic pains and stiffness of the joints, also pains in the back and loins, and cramp in the muscles. "I was," be says, " under the treatment of a number of tha best doctors and physicians, both local and in London, and paid a great deel of money in trying to obtain a cure." Finally, Mr. Baker gave Deeld'a Kidney Pills a trial. The result in his own words is, " They keep me in good bealth. I always have a few by rue, and if I find any of my old symptoms returning I take one or two pills and the trouble disappears." Mr Baker at length tried the right cure by attending to his kidneys. There is no better kidney medicine sold than Dodd'a Kidney Pills, which soon drives any of the Rheumatic family of diesasen out of the system. Dodd's Kidney Pills are 2i 91 per box, 6 bases 13s 91; of all Chemists, or post free on receipt of price from The Dodd's Medicine Co., 23, Farringdon kvenue, Lea- den, E.G. Remember the name D-o-d-Vs.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-10-17_WidnesExaminer_art0090" n="art0090">
        <head>OUR LOCAL CHURCHES.</head>
        <p>OUR LOCAL CHURCHES.</p>
        <p>WYCLIFFE F.S.A. SOCIETY.</p>
        <p>Mr Joseph Owen presided at the sleeting of this society on Sunday afternoon. Mr Et uckbody, of Man- chester, delivered an address upon " Sowings, and Reap- ing." The nansieal portion of the service was rendered additionally attractive by the performance of a trio of instrumentalists consisting of Mr J U Ter Poorten of Liverpool (violoncello), Mr R Pearson (violin), and Mr WPI Payton, Mae. Sac. (organ.) Two trios were given with musicianly taste and skill and Mr J G Tee Poorten also csatributed a solo on the violoncello. The service Wa9 also taken part in by Mr S Jones, Mr Bannister and the Rev John Yonge. Tee president alluded to the starting of a Savings Club in connection with the society for the benefit of members and members' wives. Be com- mended it to the support of all present anl pointed out that the deposits would be paid out on the Monday before Walking Day so that the money could be applied for the holidays. It was decided te far ward a letter of condolence to the parents aril family of the late Mr R Littleford, who had been a member for corns years,and who passed away after a brief illness.—During the afternoon 59 joined the Savings Club and over £4 were deposited.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-10-17_WidnesExaminer_art0091" n="art0091">
        <head>CATHOLW SCHOOL SERMONS AT RUNCORN.</head>
        <p>CATHOLW SCHOOL SERMONS AT RUNCORN.</p>
        <p>The annual sermons in aid of the schools were preached in St. Edward's Catholic Church, Ruacorn, on Sunday. In the morning solemn high mass was sung by the R iv. Father Francis Jeffrey, Canon Regular of the Lateran. There was service and benediclon in the evening. The sermons were preached by the Rev Father Bertrand, C.P. The inusical portioa of the services was of an elaborate character. Gouned's " Mises de Sancta Cseailia " (Masse Solennalle) was sung in the morning, and this inquieite work remitted admirable treatment. The solo music was taken by Miss May Kennedy (soprano), Miss Morrie Cukor,' (contralto), Mr Charles Watson (tenor),and Messrs I it Leigh, Percy Geormand Reid (bus). The offertory piece in the morning was " Ave Maria," which was most artistically rendered by Mr Reid. In the evening the music was equally attrac- tive, " Laudate puni Dotniaum" was sung by Mr Watson, who with the chorus and orchestra showed an evident appreciation of this delightful com- position. " Nazareth " followed (Mr Percy George), and the result showed signs of careful training. The instru- mental accompaniments were interpreted by the mem- bers of ths Runcorn Orchestral Society in a style deserv- ing of the highest praise. Mr Sydney Beckett presided with his oustomary ability at the organ. Mr J W Dockery was responsible for the musical arrangemente, and this gentleman may be congratulated no the high standard attained.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-10-17_WidnesExaminer_art0092" n="art0092">
        <head>WARGRAVE CISURCEI, BARLESTOWN.</head>
        <p>WARGRAVE CISURCEI, BARLESTOWN.</p>
        <p>Special serviees in conneetion with the harvest thanks- giving were held in this cherish en Suaday. TO. decora- tions wore not by aby means elabsrate, but what there were looked very aeat. They wire principally ia the window recesses, and about the Csmosuniew rails, the flowers and cereals being very tastefully arranged. The services were conducted by the Rector, Ray J Carson, who preached appropriate sermons at the morning and evening services. There were large cosgregatieae not- withstanding the fact that there was so special preacher. The choir rendered special anthems and the services were bright and cheerful. In the afternoon as organ recital was given by Mr Thomas Msrtie, the organist, interspersed with selections by the choir. Toe items submitted included organ solo " Toccata," •' At Evening," "Cook,. and Nightingale Concerto," " La .Radinage, and " God preserve the Emperor." The venal name were the anthem " All the works of the Lord are good," by the choir ; selo " 0 son Divine," Miss Cor- lett ; and solo by Mr John Mslyneur. The services were sontinued on Tuesday evening, whoa an address was gives by the Rev J Ailme,St Coryenstucae Church, Liverpool and a collection was mada in aid of the Mersey Mission to deameo.</p>
