      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-10-17_WidnesExaminer_art0093" n="art0093">
        <head>SKETCHING OR SHOOTING.</head>
        <p>SKETCHING OR SHOOTING.</p>
        <p>Warrington Men Prosecuted at Over.</p>
        <p>Fine Inflicted.</p>
        <p>John Gallon, engineer, s,Green-etrost, Werrineton,and R Surnames, draueletsmam, 157,Liverpool-road, Warring- ton, were summoned at Over Petty Sessions on Monday, before Dr °ken (pre din, Lord DeLamers, and Mr P Jeckeon,at the inetsnce of Major Wilbrallain.flor deg trespass in pursuit of gain., at the Flashes, on Septem- ber Btb. Both pleaded not guilty. Mr A Fleteher (Northwich) prosecuted in behalf of Majer Wilbraham. He explained that for a long time there had been a continual subsidence of the land,and a coneiderable portion of Major Wilbraham's estate had become submerged ; end in that manner it had been connected with the navigable portion of the Weaver. For more than a year the defendants seemed to nave been in the habit of coming up the Weaver and on to the Fleshee, proceeding almost as far as they could :at. They had a steam launch, and Major Wilbraham was not disposed to interfere with persons having a little entertainment on his portion of the property if due res- pect was paid to his rights. It wag for an abuse of those rights that defendants' were before the court. Os the date named, Tom Bellamy, the keeper employed by Major Wilhraham, was near Mrs Tomlinson'e farm, Weaver Weld, when he heard a number of shots fired from the direction of the fleshes. He proeseeled towards the water and saw two shots fired from the launch on which were the defendants and ether,. He intercepted the boat down the river, and told the defendants that Lie was the keeper He asked what right they had te be up the Flasees shooting with a gun and a retriever doeeand their reply waste the effect that a pees they had from the Weaver Navigation was a justifiation for their sport. They had paid for the sport, and were 'testified in what they had done. They did not treat Bellamy with the good feel- ing expected from men in their positions. They then began to chaff hint,and use language which WAS unjusti- fiable. Subsequently they beoame frightened, and one of the number *eked Bellamy to accept a bribe. After they hod time to think the matter over, they wrote a ' letcer to Msjer Wroraharn. It was signed by Jno. Gallon,and wse well expreeeel. Tee terms were :" AC I have (with other) unwittingly treepaseed on your estate, I take the first opportunity to write to you sincerely apologizing The circumstances are as follows :—For the lest fair years I have formed one of a party on a steam launch, to have a short holiday on the Flashes above Wineford, hue never had the least idea at all that in doing so we were trespassing. of coarse to pass along the Weaver we required a pass, which, in the absence of other knowledge, we took as giving us power to go as far as we could. My purpose for going was simply sketching, as Igo in fee art as a hobby pretty strong. This year, however, when going down on Monday evening, we took in tow a boat oontaining seine ladies, and bebiod them a man hung on without leave. You can well understand we had never seen these People before in our lives. In passing a bend of the river a man, wire turned out to be the keeper Bellamy, asked if we had a pass. Of coarse we said " Yes." He followed us down to Winsford, and told us that we had been trespassing. That was indeed news in the face of the fact that scores of boats use the Flashes Unfortunately, a gun, belonging to one of the crew. was lying on the cabin top, and that, with our watch- dog, led him to think we had been after game. Such a thing never entered into the heads of any there; we bad never been off the boat all day. the gun was put out there while the cabin was cleaned down far tea. Per- sonally, I never shot at anything, except a target, in my life, and told B,llatny I would write and explain the whole matter. I make haste to do so, hoping that you will accept our sincere apology for our intrusion, and our assurance that game was the very last thing in our thoughts. If we had had any idea that a permit was required before going on the noshes% I would have been the first to have respectfully asked for the Caine, for I think some of the river views are lovely, and people have frequently thoight I had got some of my pictures from the Lakes instead of Cheshire. My position in this town Is such that any association with such an idea as Bellamy suggested would be altogether beside the mark. We were simply out for a holiday, not intending to injere anything in the world, or going anywhere with- out proper authority. Hoping you may see your way clear to accept my explanation kc." Tom Bellamy, the keeper, corroborated the advocate'e statement as to what transpired between himself and the defendants. He did not see what they fired at. They were on the sniping ground, and preserved game was all round. When he asked them for the name and address of toe boat, they said they hud got a pass from the Weaver Trustees, and having paid for their sport, did not recognize Major Wilbraham's right. Hot threat- ened to wire to North wich to have them stepped, and they then gave him the name of the owner. The gun on board was a double-barrelled breech-loading gun, and the dog a retriever. He told them he would report the matter, and they did not deny having used the gun for sporting purposes. Burthern (the owner of the launch), offered him a coin, asking him to make the report as light as possible. Witness deelined, saying that Major Wilbraham paid him for his work.—Cross- examined by Gallon : Ha did not know which man fired. He never heard Gallon say that he had never fired a gure—Re-exemined by Mr Fletcher : He drew attention to the gun and dog on bosrd, but not one of the party said they had not used the gun. Gallon iefornted the Beach that they were out fir a holiday, the party being composed of four men—all in fair positioes in Warrington—and they would net go anywhere against the law. At Acton Bridge a men named Slater, Connah'e Quay, joined these, and the gun belonged to him. They lived on board, and never went ashore frem the time they started to their return. SOMA party joinei them for a few minutee, but he never heard a shot fired all the day. At this juncture, he produced two scenes from the Flashes, one of which he said Ise painted on the Bth ult. They had been up the Flashes on several occasions, but had never before been stopped. He declared that sport never once entered in theirealou- Intiens.—By Lord Delatnere : He never heard a shot fired that elay.—By Mr Fletcher : Bellamy complained of the boats they were towing. He spoke of the man in the beat at the rear as being a known poacher. He did not see anyone offer tile keeper a sum of money. Slater iateaded to sport en the Mersey, but not in the Flashen. The deg was never in the water. They did not give a name to 'slimily in the test instance because they doubted his authority. . .</p>
        <p>Burthem also deala'red that he mover heard a elicit lirad that day. The deg belonged to him, and had never been broken.</p>
        <p>Thomas Reynolds Critehley, tailor's assistant, 32, Arpley-street, Warrington, said he Wee with the defend- ants ea the date named. When Bellamy accosted them witness was in the cabin straightening up, and he put the gun belonging to Slater on top of the cabin. When they got down the river, Bellamy remarked about them towing some suspicious characters about, and defend- ants thinkihg the remark applied to the ladies resented it. Bellamy then made himself clear by stating his reference was to the man in tho last beat. He never heard a word mentioned about the gun.—Cruse- examined : The gun was only out of the cabin once,and that was_ when witness handled it.—By the Court : Gallon was painting all the time. Slater and Burthein went into another boat, !Mt the gun and dog were left with witness. Mr Cooke (magistrates' clerk) said it looked as if Galeen attached some importance to the gun when he wrote Unfortunately, a gun, belonging te one of the mew,</p>
        <p>Gallon aaid that was his apology to Major Wilbraham.</p>
        <p>The magistrates retired to.cottilder their return Bellamy was questioned by Lord Detainers and the Chairman. Witn3se was positive be saw tws shots fired front the defendant's launch. Their denial was an untruth. The Chairman said that Major Wilbraham was very generous in regard to allowing the public privilezes, but did not allow his rights to b 3 infringed. They tolieved defendants were tresoassing, and.inaloos3d a fine is each ease of £2, which included the advocate's fee.</p>
        <p>Brick-street _Mission Band of Hope.</p>
        <p>Tke winter meetings were opened on Tuesday evening with a lime-light lantern entertainment given by Mr Henry Taylor, PC M. The views shown included a trip through the British Empire, a peep at Bournville,Cadbary's model oilier homes, the Warrington Volunteers in South Africa and soldiers' graves, concludinv with a number of beau- tiful statuary and comic slides which greatly pleas- ed the juvenile portion of the audience. Mr Scraggs presided, and a hearty vote of thanks was accorded to the lanternist.</p>
