      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-10-17_WidnesExaminer_art0095" n="art0095">
        <head>LATE MRS. BEAMONT.</head>
        <p>THE LATE MRS. BEAMONT.</p>
        <p>Pulpit References.</p>
        <p>en Sunday the preachers at several Warrington Churches made references to the late litre. Beamont.—it the Parish Chureb, in the morning the Rev. A. e• Cameron spoke in appreciative terms of her piety and generosity. At the evening service the preacher was 3he Rector (the Rev. Canon Willis), who selected for leis text the words :—" Remember them which have the rule over yen, who have spoken unto you the word of God ; whine faith below, considering the end *1 their conver- sation, Jeans Christ the same yesterday and to-day and for ever."—Nebrews xiii. 7-8. At the close of his ser- mon the Rector dwelt on four features in the life of the late Mrs. Beamoat :-1. her intelligence, education, and culture ; 2, her simplicity, dress, and habits ; 3, her generosity, great but discrirniesting ; 4, her piety, real, ibut undemonstrative. She was, he pointed ont, a devoted daughter of the Chureh of England, and her loss would be keenly felt throughout the length and breadth of the town. She carried on the good work which her husband laid down when he died, and such a woman was held in the highest tensible respect and reverence. The Rector concluded by expressing the hope thst all who could make it elavenient to attend her funeral en the following day at Padgate would do se. The coogregation stood while the organist (Mr Darby) played the Dead March in "Saul." At Holy Trinity Church no references were made to the deceased lady, owing to the absence of the Vicar (the Rev E C E Carleton) who was out of town, but solemn hymns were sung, and the Dead March in was played on the organ by Mr Randall. At St. Paul's Churehlthe Rev W Bracecsmo (vicar) took for his text in the evening the words "Favour is deceitful, and beauty is vain, but a woman that feareth the Lord, she shall be praised," Proverbs xxxi. 30. The preacheren the ceureceof his eermen,showed what a great power for good a noble and righteous woman possessed. Anticline to Mr e Reannent, he said they had a true speci- men of the text in her case. She had sot the ladies of the congregation a splendid example, and the men also. Not long ago she said to him when he called upon I her, "I don't know why God is sparise, my life. ; but I think it is because Ho wants ins to do something elle for Him." What Warrington would do without Mrs. Beat/sent God alone knew, and when he said War- riegton he meaat the churches in the town. Net' did he know what they would do at St Paul's because by her death they had lost £5O a year towards the curacy fund and since he had been appointed Vicar of the pariah he had lost about £7O so far as the same fund was eon- cerned. A gap had been created, and who woe to take her place ? It seemed to him that the life of a good woman like Mrs Beamont should leave behind it " foot- prints on the sand of time." There was no question as to where she had gone, She had gone home to hear the Well done, goo' and faithful servant, eater thou into the joy of thy Lord. " Mr E Wilkes, the organist, play- ed Claopin's " Funeral March " at the close of the see- "vioe, the congregation standicg. At St. Ann's Church, in the morning, the Vicar (the Rev James Davies) also alluded to Mrs Reament's death. She was, he said, a great benefactress, and in order that he might come to Warringtoe to take charge of the ehurch, she increased the endowment by investieg £3,000 in Difersey Deck Bond. She also gave them • large playground for the boys' and girls' schools and the adjoining plot of land on which to build the pro- posed infant school. The last-named ground opened into Winwiek-road. She had contributed largely to the vicarage fund, the cleaning and repair fund, and was a regular subscriber to the cur Icy fund. They all deeply deplored her less. As a mark of respect to her the organist, Mr E A Parlmore, played the Dead March in " Saul," the congregation standing. In the evening the Vicar referred to tire Raiment in similar terms,and two special hymns were sung namely, Let nints ow earth in concert sing, with those whose work is done," and " And now the labourer's task is o'er:, At Christ Church, Padget*, on Sunday, the preacher, mornieg and evening, was the Vicar,the Rev W Pollo3k- Hill, who alluded to Mrs Bearnont's great benevolence and piety. He reoesrked that both her late husband and berself had materially ceutributel to the welfare of the parish both in its religious an i educational life. The late Mr Beamont built not only Weolsten volaeel and schoolhouse, but also Orford school, and he hAri also been a great benefactor to Padgeste school. Mrs Bsamoret had been a very generous subscriber to the Wade or the new infant school now in course of erection. Mr Black- well at each service played on the organ tie Dead March in " Saul"</p>
        <p>Appropriate references were nude a'; Orford Church bv the Rev G Vale Owen, and Mr Can played the Dead March on tha organ at each service. --....—..</p>
        <p>TEE FUNERAL.</p>
        <p>Additional evidence of the dev respect in which the late Mrs Baer:one was held was aff )rded by the large and representative assembly at th funeral, which took place at Christ Cburen, Padga-.e, en Monday afternoon. The church was completely filled, among those present being :—Rev W Patiock-Hill (vicar ot Palizate), the Rev. G Vale Owen. of Orford, the Heater of Warriagten (the Rev Canon Willis), and ala•-Rome-E CE Carleton.liderley Stevenson, E J Willcoeks, R V Pigot, H A Lester, James Davies, H R Wild, E R Tarhuck (vicar of St George's, Altrinchane, formerly vicar of St Paul's, Warrington), William Bracectmp, H Gibson Smith, B Percival sal the Rector of levmaa (the Rev G B Taurston) ; Colonel Backtoo, Dr Watkins, aad Messrs John White, El B Melville, Houghton, Joseph Houghton, Thomas Hough- ton, James W Houghten. George Mather, Rigny, Frank Tinsley, E B Cadet" Edward Rose, John Heskath, and James Hatton. Holy Trinity Church was represented by Messrs John 11 Hatton and Thomas Welsby ; and St Paul's by Messrs R J Chorley and Jll Smethurst. Mr E g Kidd represented the past and present holders of the Beament scholarship at Cambridge University. Mr Leonard Davies was unable to be present owing te indisposition. The burial service was conducted by the Revs W Pollock-Hill and Vale 0 wen,and the choir was in attend- ance. Too coffin, on waich were many b3autiful wreathe, was carried to the chancel, preceded by the officiating clergy and thechnir,the Rev G Vale Owen reading the opening sentenc2s of the burial office. Psalm no. was song aed rho usual lesson was read, after which the hymn " There is A ble4eed ho no" was sung. The coffin was then borne to the grriveside,Me Blackwell, who presided at the organ playing the Dead March in Saul" as the mourners left the church. Rain fell very heavily while the las: rites were Ixling performed • At the graveside the hymn " On the resurrection morning" was sung. The grave is situat3d in the north-east corner of the churchyard a short distance from the principal entrance, and the body of Mrs Beamont lies near that of her late frail:land. The sides of the grave were covered with evergreens. The coffin which was of brown oak, with brass mountings, was supplied by Messrs Garaett and Sons.</p>
        <p>Many children from the schools which had either been established or largely maintained by the benevolence of the late Mr and Mrs Beament in Padgate, Orford and Warrington attanded the funeral. In addition almost every parish in Warrington was represented by clergy, church officers, or some members of the congregation. Ths funeral arrangements were satisfactorily carried out by Messrs J and W Dutton, of Sankey-street. The principal mourners were :—The Rev M and Mrs Btapylton Miss Smith (Orford Hall), Mr E E Peacock Mr Vice B Davies, Mr Ed ward G Davies, Mr John Bur- ease, Mr Charles White, Mr W G Killick (Aigburth),Mrs Nixon and the Misses Webb, as well as Mrs Re brick and the maid-servants from the Hall. Floral tributes had been forwarded by :—Tho Rev M and Mrs Stapylton, " Laura Nixon," Mrs Wood (Faradon), gardeners, coachman and servants at Orford Ralf. " Emmeline and Vera Davies," Mr and Mrs E G Davies, Mr Lenard G Davies, the Rev W and Mrs rollick-UilL pupils of the Clergy Daughters' School, " one of her old mervaots," Miss Kennedy, the Rev E G E and Mrs Carletou,the Miiges and Mr T Woods,Bewsey- street • mistresors of the Clergy Daughter's School. Mr and ire W G Killick, " M S Woodall," Mrs John Cros- field, Miss Craofield. Rev M and Mrs Steven- see, Mrs Joseph Harrison, Miss Atkinson, " B Woolreer," Rev G and Mrs Vale Owen, Mrs William C Jones, teaahers and scholar Smith-street school ; Mr and Mrs Jahn Burgess, "Philip and Dorothy Burgess," Colonel and Mrs Buckton,toachers and scholars of Orford sehoals, "J W and A Appleton," Miss Warbur- ton, Mile Pierpoint, Mr J Edlosten. THE BISHOP'S TRIBUTE.</p>
        <p>Speaking at a rnissiouary meeting held in the Parr Hall on Tuesday evening in connection with the Society for the Propagation of the Gospel in Foreign pests, the Bishop (Dr Chavasse) said they ought as a church to thank and praise God for a great saint of His, whom He had just called away from Warrington. Fno name of Mrs Bean:taut was known through the length and breadth of the town, Thera was not a church, he thought, which did not bear in some way or other some traces of</p>
        <p>her generous heart and wise head. He had net known her as they bad known her. He had only been in the diocese two and a half years, bat he could look back with thankfulness to God on two or three happy to her. On each occasion he came away feeling that he had been talking with a saint of God standing on the threshold of Paradise. Now she was gone. " Sae I'oo from her labours and her works do follow her." They most try to follow their blessed Master as she did. She had lived es as to be missed. Gad had spared her to a great old age, and had spared her all her faculties. It would be difficult for them to irne,gine a church in War- rington without her. Might God in His mercy raise ani others ta fill her place.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-10-17_WidnesExaminer_art0096" n="art0096">
        <head>YORKSHIRE POET.</head>
        <p>A YORKSHIRE POET.</p>
        <p>Sir Samuel Girth was a vet, not of the ascetic variety but a sturdy Yorkshiretnan. He died so 101 l ago as 1718, and is bett3r remembered for his shrewil and peculiar sayings than for his written works. Pr ing his last illness. observing three physicians consulting near his bid, he surprised them by saying : " Pe!! gentlemen, let m 3 dio a natural death, I 13,g of you. 1" was a good wish. hut hardly complimewary per,oas to whms it was addressed. A st.ll bettor WI is that we may live a natural life, and then the natural death will follow as a matter of elute, but be log delayed in its coming. Tbs achievements of mince at° great, but they are alt mother overshadowed by the wonders of Nature. Nature and Science work together in the production of Mother Seigel's Curative Wall, which is made from eighteen ingredients,produote of !lac soil, each selected and proved by the highest seientls° authority, and together forming an labsolutely infallthic cure for indigeetion and billiostenees. I always bad an exeeptionally good appetite write I Miss H Farmer, of 111, Wellhead-!use, Perry call: Birmingham, on the let of May. 1902, ".but four yer ago, for the first time, food disagreed with ate. Noteic° I ate would digest, and for three weeks I endured A lwretched existence. It began with a feeling el distreP! after eating—a fulness or tightness at the chest acci stomach as theugh I had eaten much more than really had. Then In an hour, or perhaps sooner, a dull gnawing pain settled in my chest, and between my shoulders. This generally lasten several hours, and tortured me so cruelly thet I frequently went without the next meal, being afraid to eat. Especially was this the case when. aiditienal to what I have already described, a severe pain attacked me OR the left side. Though my sleep 10 night was heavy, I felt drowsy all day, rising in the morning with At sense of fatigue and dreading the eaeri tirm of the day. Alen, for the first time in my life._ suffered from shettness of breath, and had frequently a° stop and rest when I walked or attempted to hurrt'' Such is a true description of my condition when attention was first directed to Mother Seigel's Curative Syrup. I took four bottles of it only, and acute as ICY case was that small'quantity was sufficient to cure Illert' I have since had occasienal attacks of indigestion, lalo of such a flight aharacter that one bottle has alseas. been sufficient to set me up again."</p>
        <p>LOCAL WEDDINGS. Pullin—Erskine. A pretty wedding took place at noon on Tuesday 1.1, St Thomas's Church, Stockton Heath, the contractioS parties being Mr Albert Edward Pullin (of Cheltenbal and of Lloyd's Bank, Gloucester), and Miss Marßsre Edith (Madge) Erskine, daughter of the late Mr Hell Erskine, of " Dorms brook," Belfast, and ° Mrs David Fanshaw, of Derby Villas, Stiff road, Wilderspool. A large congregation witnessed tbe ceremony,the wedding party numbering about 40. 111 bride who was given away by her stepfather, Mr Deci„f Fanshaw, looked exceedingly handsome in a dress white ivory satin with train,with old Limerick lace Yet' •rt Her ornament was a gold Indian filigree nocklet,the of the bridegroom, and she carried a beautiful Owe' bouquet,fashioned of white roses and lilies of the va12,1:0 The bridesmaids were Miss Beatrice Fanshavr, se% Pansy McCracken and Miss Evelyn Grimshaw,who attired in white silk dresses with black picture be"' They wore gold curb bangles,the gifts of the bridegroorn; and carried bouquets of yellow chrysanthemums, 816'0 the gift of the bridegroom. The officiating clerg9o4, was the Rev H V rignt, vicar of St Thomas', end g J B Lea, of Bristol, officiated as best man. Mr RO5O , 913 at the organ, and he played an appropriate selection 0`</p>
        <p>music. - A reception was after wards held at the house of the bride's parents. Several toasts were proposed end dulfd honoured. The Vicar, in his usually hapoV felicitous manner, submitted the health of the bride an' bridegroom. Mr Pullin briefly but suitably respendir!,,g; Other teaser were given, including the health of u"' ladies and of the host Ind hostess, Mr and Mrs Parishes"- e Mr W H Podrnore, notwithstanding the varying t Mono of the weather, suceeeded in obtaining eree"en photsgraphs at the wedding party, and the pictureS will no doubt serve as a pleasing souvenir of the in- teresting occasion. Later on in the afternoon, amid a heavy shower of rice and confetti, the nowly-martied couple left fnr Lnedon„ where the honeymoon will MOO was a br3w-</p>
        <p>Cost mteh</p>
