        <p>An Evening with Bold-Street Choir. An opportunity was afforded the congregation of spending an evening with Bold-street choir on Tuesday evening, when about twenty-four items consisting Lif songs, quartettes, and choruses were gone through,—The schoolroom was well filled, and the choir acquitted themselves very creditably in the part songs " Miller's Wooing " (Cowan), and "Sweet and Low" (Barnhy).—The varied and numerous songs were all rendered with taste. The selections being of a high order, numerous encores were demanded and acceded to. The humorous readings were, as usual, a great treat. Miss Bessie Dutton presided and expressed her pleasure at being present in a few choice remarks. The accompanists all played their parts skilfully, aid thb National Anthem brought the third annual 'chair concert— which has proved a musical and financial suecess— to a close.</p>
        <p>___ ROYAL I- GUINEA SERIES B S 50 PATTERNS. 0 T Catalogue to Retailers for Post Card. SOLE AGENCY' ARRANGED WHERE OPEN Om Elm C. MIDCLEY &amp; SON, 0 4- , BOOT MANUFACTURERS, B 0 Swinegate, LEEDS.</p>
        <p>An interesting ceremony took place at Deb close of an inquest held in the Coroner's Court at the Warrineton Police Station on Monday afternoor. Mr. S. Brighouse, the Coroner for South-West Lancashire, was presented with a large and handsomely framed photograph of the members of the first jury who served in the new Court, and his Clerk (Mr. Carr) received a &amp;mener framed copy of the same photograph. Mr. Brighouse, Mr. Carr, the Chief 4onstable (Mr. Luke Talbot) and the Coroner's officer (Detective Barlow) figured on the photo- graph as well as the members of the jury. The CHIEF CONSTABLE made the presentation, and remarked that alter the first important iuquest beld in the Court the Coroner was kind enough to entertain the members of the jury and himself (Mr Talbot) to dinner at the Patters Arms Hotel, and they spent an evening which would ever be memorable in their lives. On that occasion they expressed in full their high appreciation of Mr. Brighonee, both as a highly esteemed official and as an English gentleman, and no words of his (Mr. Talbot's) could add to these enconiums. He noticed that on the photograph Mr Brighouse was wearing his familiar and well-known emile,andlhe hoped that was an augury and an omen of good concerning the relations which would always exist between himself (Mr Brighouse) and the W arricgton juries who served under his able direction. When the members of the jury looked at that picture they would be able to see the por- trait of a man whom they admired, honoured, and res- pected, and their honour and respect for him were not empty and outward expressions. In presenting the smaller photograph to Mr Carr, the Chief Constable said that they who so admired the Coroner, knew how very indispensable to him was a good clerk. They knew that in Mr Carr,the Coroner for South West Lancaehire had, if not the ablest clerk in England,certainly one of them. Mr Carr was, in his principles,almost a replica of his chief. Mr. Talbot then read the following inscription which appeared on the photograph presented to the Coroner :—" Presented to S. Brighouse, Esq., County Coroner, by the first jury on the occasion of the opening of the Coroner's Court,Warrington,February 4th. 1902. " In accepting the gift, Mr BRIGHOUSE said he could only assure them that although he felt he had become pretty well " case-hardened," he was genuinely touched by that tribute of affection and esteem. Outside the meterial value of the photograph and the beautiful frame in which they had chosen to place it, there woald be an intrinsic value attached to it which no monetary value could equal. Be knew that when he took it back to Orrnekirk and slowed it to his wife and family they would be as pleased as he was at that recognition on the part of the jury of the services he bad rendered to South West Lancashire in the office he held. He had not lead the pleasure of meeting them unofficially since he left them at the Patten Arms Hotel, and that seemed a long period. Be had very pleasant recollections of that evening and he appreciated the tact that they had thought so much of it as to give him that vary hand- some photograph. He should always treasure it, not for its value, but because he felt that a body of jury- men in South-West Lancashire had recognised his ser- vices in a manner which appealed to him very much indeed. It might be that from time to time perhaps, he gamed callous and case-hardened when cases came before him, but believe him, it did not matter how long he held that office he should still be moved by the same emotions ao themselves, the same desire to do what was right between man and man, whether they were rich or poor. He should always be moved and actuated by the desire, so far as that court was concerned, to hold the scales of justice evenly between everybody, whether they were employess or employees, whether they were of the rich and cultured portion uf the community, or whether, owing to circumstances over which they had no control, they were lower down in the social scale. He hoped, with the assistance of the jurymen to conduct the Court in such a manner that no one could point a finger of scorn at it. While be thanked them as Warrington jurymen for the very many unselfish ser- vices they bad rendered,and for the very great care and attention they had given to the important cases which name under their consideration, he knew they would not think him out of place when he took that public opportunity of rendering the same meed of thanks to the other jurymen in South West Lancashire. When they considered the import- ant coal mining industry which surrounded some boroughs in the district and the very large shipping in- dustry at Beetle, they must be aware that there were cases requiring very great care and attention, and be said quite honestly and from the bottom of his heart, that the work he had to go through and the labour he had to perform, were brought down from a duty to a pleasure by the services which the jurymen cf South West Lancashire rendered, and by the kind and generous way in which they always met him in all the work he had to perform. He publicly tendered to them his very best thanks Der all they had done for him and for the community. Mr. CARR also returned thanks and echoed everything that Mr Brighouse had said. TRUSS.—New washable waterproof is the most suitable of tropical climates ; it is light, clean, cool, comfortable, and durable; can be worn whilst bathing. WHITE &amp; WRIGHT Surgical Instrument Makers. 93. Renshaw-street, opposite top of Bold-street Liverpool.—Advt. A CROWN OF HEALTH.—AiI sufferers from Dyspepsia,Liver Piles or Headaches will receive sufficient of an infallible remedy for three days' treatment free. Send stamped envelope.-492. STRETFORD-ROAD, Manchester.—Advt. The Health and Vigor of an Individual depends on the quantity and quality of the Blood. When the tissues have been at work there are thrown into the blood waste products and if