        <head>PLEASANT PARAGRAPHS.</head>
        <p>PLEASANT PARAGRAPHS.</p>
        <p>WHILE the fool is proving that he is right, the wise man is finding out where his'is wrong. No mouse is superstitions enough to believe that a black cat brings good luck. THE trouble with the average prodigal is that he won't stay at home after he returns. IF you wait to get things until you can afford them, you will never have them. PROBABLY all men are liars, but some reticence in regard to your views on the subject is often wise. You can't always judge a man by his clothes; but you can sometimes get some idea of him by his wife's clothes. THE early geographers used to think that the world was square. Nobody ever thought that the inhabitants were square. THE absorbing ambition of life is to have the crowd cry, " Here he comes 1" when one approaches. POVERTY may be no] crime, but a good many people regard it as it serious misdeamour, at least. A ROLLING stone finds at last a hollow that fits it. IT requires a good deal of effort to love a poor man and marry him; but it is easy enough to love a rich man. THE DIFFERENCE.—" They say a man is always nervous when he proposes." "And a girl is always nervous when he doesn't." MY QUEEN!" fondly exclaimed the infatuated youth. "My Jack," softly responded the blush- ing maiden. JACK: "Yes; ancestors certainly help to give a person soeial prestige." Tom "Especially when they are wealthy and one lives with them." Mrs. Houten " Have you anybody you can re- fer me to?" Menial: "Oh, yes; I have come pre- pared to exchange references with you." Mrs. B. : "The lady Dabbs is going to marry is highly intellectual. She speaks three languages." Mr. B. (condolingly) : "Poor Dabbs." PREPARED.—" But, really, are we prepared for war ? " "I am anyway. I can make a list in ten minutes of the fellows I would like to see go. WHAT is the pain of which everyone makes light? A window pane. WHY should a publican be a jovial sorb of fellow? Because he's seldom out of spirits. ' THE majority of love's bonds are formed from a chain •of circumstances. Ir isn't always the loser in a pugilistic contest that gets the swelled head. THE up•to.clate war correspondent never fails to make use of the word "imbroglio." THE board of trade member doesn't mind being called a bull or a bear, but call him a calf or a cub and he gets so mad. WHAT kind of a face should an auctioneer have? A for-bidding one. No matter how high a man may fly he's got tc Come down to earth occasionally for board and lodging. Fr is a wise child that knows enough to keep ite face straight when its fathrr is learning to ride a wheel. EVERY man intends to have his own way after marriage, but sometimes his wife relieves him of a false impression. THE people who get along best in the world are not the ones who always know what other people ought to do. WHEN you hear a man boasting of a proud an- cestry, you may conclude that it couldn't be that they were proud of him. THE man who monopolises the attentions of the prettiest girl at a party is both envied and hated by all the other men present. Miss PASSAY : "I dread to think of my fortiett birthday." Miss Pert. " Why ? Did something unpleasant happen then?" LOVE.JOY (as he is about to leave, after his rejec. tion) : "Come, Miss Spurner, help me on with my overcoat. Since you cannot be my wife you may at least be assister to me." STRANGE.—" Dear me!" cried Mrs. Trotter. "the paper speaks of a yacht that ran aground last week—just as though it could move out of the water.' " WHAT was the cause," asked the superinten• dent, "of the Egyptian plagues ?" "They didn't boil the water," ventured a little girl in the infant class. BRIEF PEACE OF MlND.—"Clara, I love to be with you." "Why, Edith ? " "When I'm with you I know you are not gossiping about me." YOUNG HUSBAND: "My dear Minnie, I _ say that tins pudding tastes very bad." Wife: "All imagination, dear, it says in the cookery. book that it tastes excellent !" ALL TAKE A HAND.—" It is estimated," said Brown, "that 7,000,000 packs of playing cards are made every year." "That," said Jones, "is a great deal. A RECENT advertisement was to the following effect: "The public is recommended to secure a copy of this highly interesting book, as from pre- sent appearances it is not at all likely that a second edition will be printed." " THis potato is only half done my dear," said he, crossly. "Then only eat half of it, my love," she replied affectionately. TEACHER, "Now, Patsy, would it be proper to say, 'You can't learn me nothing?" Patsy: " Yes'm." Teacher: "Why?" Patsy: "'Cause you can't." IMAGINATION. Benny Dict "Liner writes about the responsibtlities of great wealth, and he isn't worth a cent." Henn Peck "Yes : and about the joys of married life, and he is'nt married." THE LAST TO DISAPPEAR. —Friend: "Don't you believe in der survival of der fittest ? " Cloth- ing Merchant: "Yell, I know in my peesness der misfits last der longest !" ESTIMATED.—VOn Blumer "How many do you expect to dinner?" Mrs. Von Blumer : " Mr. Stuffer and four others." Von Blumer "That makes eight." SAME THING' ONLY DIFFERENT.—Dr. Emdee : "Women, you know, _4see.:very subject to nervous prostration. * Mr. ll,llhlrlow. : " Dont% men have is ? " Dr. Emdee : "No ; men him irritability." LIMITED K.NOWLEDGE.—Gaytoy : "That doctor doesn't know me vcry, well, it seems." Askins : "Don't, eh f" Gaykiiy " Nci ; he told me that smoking was the worst thing I could do."</p>
        <p>must</p>
        <p>"BUT we cannot live on papa," protested the savage's bride to be. "He is dreadfully poor." "We can wait until he is fatter 1" said the cannibal. TIIE EVIDENCE. —She (approvingly): " Yor won her hand, then?" He (rather glumly): "Humph; I presume so. I'm under her thumb." WEDIN a girl makes an assignment of her love her sweetheart is immediately appointed as receiver. "IT pains me to punish you, Paul. Remember that the wickedness of the son makes the grey hair of the parent I" " 800-hoo I What a bad boy you must have been Look at poor grandpa over there—boo-hoo I"</p>
        <p>IN TEIE ART GALLERY.—Mary Ann: "Oh, I say, what a platy oil paintin' I" Susan (cau- tiously): "Sh—h ? Don't show per ignorance that way. People can 'ear yer. Don't you see it's a pictur o' ships? All o' them like that's water colours?"</p>
        <p>ROBI X9-07;7. That's a fine dog you have, Dumley. Do you want to sell him?' Dumley: "ru sell him for £5.?" Robinson: 'ls he intelligent?" Dumley (with emphasis) : " Intelligent ? Why, that dog knows as much I do." Robinson: "You don't say so I Well, I'll give you half.s-crown for him, Darnley." "DON'T you feel gloomy," said the young mai, who has occasional intervals of soulfulness, "when the sky is overcast with grey, when the rhythmic rain sounds a dirge, and the landscape's beauties are hidden by the weeping mist?" " Yes." she answered, with sweet, interest. " It's dreadfully annoying. It does make one's hair come out of curl so." . .</p>
        <p>RANK IsJusncs.—ln an Irish police-court the Magistrate was about to pronounce sentence on an Irishman for the theft of a goose. "And It is upon the oath of thim two witnesses that yer honour's going to condemn me I " said the prisoner. "Certainly," said the Magistrate. " Oh, murther I to condmian me on the oaths of two spalpeens whs swear they saw me take the bhird, when I cam bring a hundred who will swear they didet 1344 sue do It2l</p>
