      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-10-17_WidnesExaminer_art0122" n="art0122">
        <head>NOTES ON UAMES AND ATHLETICS, RuglV-Fo;tball.</head>
        <p>NOTES ON UAMES AND ATHLETICS, RuglV-Fo;tball.</p>
        <p>[By Ovalist.]</p>
        <p>Warrington's second defeat has now to be chronicled, the team having succumbed on Saturday to the might of Salford and the force of circumstances. Had the selected team .turned out there would probably have been a different result, for the whole of the backs were down for duty. Dickenson was a doubtful starter all along so it was no surprise to learn that he was not playing, but it was a great surprise to find at the last minute that Fish was in the hands of the doctor with a bad throat. Two such important absentees made a big difference to the team, in fact all the difference between winning and losing. It was hard luck that Warrington, while doing so well, should be compelled to put a weak team in the field at Salford, where there is always a prospect of victory.</p>
        <p>Quite a large crowd of Warringtonians journeyed to Weaste, and many of them would not know till the teams turned out what a change had been necessary. Even the Salford folk were disappointed, for they were in hopes of seeing Fish and Dickenson perform. This expectation and the fact that Swinton and Broughton were both engaged on Yorkshire soil has a lot to do with the fact that the "Reds ". had th.. 3 best crowd of the season at the' Willows.</p>
        <p>The substitutes on the Warrington side were Edmunds and Ratcliffe, Hockenhall filling the centre position and Ratcliffe playing on the wing with Isherwood. It would have been better in my humble opinion if Harris had crossed over to the left wing or if Isherwood had played right centre. There would then have been one sei viceable wing pair for either attack or defence, and seeing the way the play went I think Harris might have scored a try or two had he been alongside Isher- wood. The other two threequarters would have only needed to look after Walsh and Bell ,which was quite an easy task,seeing that neither of these twq has any class about him. Salford played their best backs on one wing, with the result that they scored by persistent I pegging away,and Warrington might have followed the same plan with advantage. The result of the match might not have been effected, but there is every reason to imagine that Warrington's score would have been a bit larger and Salford's a bit less.</p>
        <p>The expected did happen in one respect, for as I prophesied, Warrington were badly beaten forward. Salford's heavy weights were too much for our men in the scrummages and the' Reds" got the ball threeltimes out of four right through the match. Thus they had all the opportunities and made very poor use of them. The Warrington eight worked hard but they were no match for their ppponents, and the fact should con- vince those in authority that though they have done well in recent games, the Warrington forwards are not by any means as strong as they should be. One or two bigger men are as ,much needed yet as ever they were.</p>
        <p>To return to the game however! Salford were soon aggressive, but their lack of polish behind the scrum- mage enabled Warrington to keep them from travelling vary far. Powerful kicking finally drove Warrington back—and here again they sadly missed Dickenson— until the "Reds" were near the line. Then Lomas got away with the ball, and gave Tom Williams a pass which appeared to me clearly forward, the popular centre dashing over in the corner. This early reverse damped Warrington's ardour considerably, and it appeared all over bar shouting, when Taylor charged down a careless punt by Davies and ran over the line unopposed. It was a soft sort of try but it brought the score level and heartened Warrington up considerably. They made things pretty lively, and with Fish on the left wing they must have had a couple of tries, Ratcliffe doing absolutely nothing with a couple of passes which left him a grand opening. At half-time the score was still a try each and there seemed a chance, of a draw if nothing better.</p>
        <p>Salford made great efforts on resumption and so did Warrington, with the result that it seemed anybody's game. Hockenhall went half-back and opened the game out better, Isherwood twice running very near to the Salford lino. Then the home lot retaliated, and though the passing was poor they got ahead. Harris failed to properly "down " Bell when he had him, and the latter gave to Lomas who scored the usual simple looking try to the great joy of the anxious Salfordians. After this Warrington did little but defend, Knight twice tackling the speedy Bone in fine style. The third time was too much for him, however, the wing man just scraping in at the corner and upsetting the touch- fla,..a. Before the close Warrington got down to the Salford end again and Taylor kicked a goal frogn a mark, which left the score 9 points to 5 points in favour of Salfore.</p>
        <p>It was Rot a bad game by any means and the losers, behind the pack, were as good as the winners. Salford are unquestionably weak at threequarter this season, T Williams being the best of a poor lot, and if Warring- ton had been at full strength they must have given the " Reds " a very warm time indeed. lam certain neither Bone nor Williams would have scored. Warring- ton passed quite as well as Salford at times, notwith- standing their disorganised back division, but the finishing touch was wanting. Harris got injured early on, and in addition had very little work given him on the attack, while Ratcliffe was very little use., Some excuse should be made for him seeing that this was his first turn-out, and that he was not quite recovered from an injury received in practice. Isherwood and Knight Were the best of the Warrington backs, the latter play- ing a remarkably sound and vigorous game Taylor, Boardman and Forster were the pick of the forwards. On the Salford side Lomas was hardly as abiquitous as usual, yet he played well and received but little sup- port from Davies. The halves were fairly well-matched though Edmunds kept doing the wrong thing until he went centre, where he was more useful. Williams and Bone did nearly all the home three-quarter work, while Hignett played a fair game at back. Rhapps, Tunney, Brown and Buckler were always prominent among a powerful set of forwards.</p>
        <p>The poor opinion I formed of Salford, as an unde- feated team, was confirmed on Monday, when they were defeated at Brighouse, by the hitherto unsuccess- ful and rather unlucky Rangers. Two goals to nil was all the scoring, but it shows the want of class in the Salford team, which was at full strength. The wet weather may have made them worse than usual, but to be nilled by Brighouse is a bitter pill, which the Royal Borough enthusiasts will not like swallowing. It is' quite on the cards that the Rangers of Broughton will have another pillifor them this Saturday !</p>
