      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-10-17_WidnesExaminer_art0123" n="art0123">
        <head>MANCHESTER DEATH TRAPS. --- EVictims of the Fatal Scaffold.</head>
        <p>MANCHESTER DEATH TRAPS. --- EVictims of the Fatal Scaffold.</p>
        <p>There have been two hundred scaffold accidents in Mancheettr within twelve months—in fact the city is becoming notorious among bricklayers owing to the frcquency of such occurrences—many of them fatal, and the men are asking auxicusly whether some means cannot be devised for reducing the risks which they have to face. One of the most intelligent of Manchester artisans recently discussed with a press representative the dangers of the scaffold, and his raaarks are worth reproducing. Mr William Rhoiles is now a bricklayer in the employ of the Lancashire and Yorkahire Railway Company,and he has had long experience of his trade. " When I was a younger man, long before I came I into toe employ of the L. and Y. Railway," he said to I the reriorter, who saw him at his residenco,62B,oldham- road, Newton Heath, Manchester, "I came down frcm a sea ff 01 d sudden-like, and it was a bad job for me. The scaffold broke. but, as a matter of fact, I suffered no I injury at the time beyond a dislccation of the shoulder. I But My nervous system and spite had sustained a I shock. Up to a year or two ago I still felt the effects ' of that fall, and I shonld be feeling them still if it , bad not teen for Dr Williams' pink pills fcr pate people. It seemed a lucky escape at the time, but I had to pay for the mishap all the same, for that fall shattered my nerves. I got as nervous as a kitten " (Mr. Males is a big, healthy- looking man, standing well over six feet), "and was so dreadfully nervous that I would not sleep in my room alone. I went to several doctors, who said that my illness was due to my age—and I am only sixty-seven now—but I did not get any better until I tegan to take Dr. Williams' pink pills for Dale people." "In three months," Mr. Rhodes continued, " the pills put me all right, and I grew strong enough to work, although the company wished to discharge me, owing to excessive neroruniess which I exhibited." "You are convinced tit at it wae Dr. Williams' pink pills that cured you P" "Aye, and I don't care wl:o known it," said Mr. Rhcdes enthusiastically.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-10-17_WidnesExaminer_art0124" n="art0124">
        <head>– EDUCATION. . -- A Runcorn Memorial Against the</head>
        <p>- EDUCATION. . -- A Runcorn Memorial Against the</p>
        <p>Bill.</p>
        <p>Tho following petition against the Bill was de3patched on Wednesday by Mr W Millington, secretary to the Runcorn Free Church Council,to the HOUS3 of Commons, Over 500 signatures were attached. To the Right Honourable A. J. Balfour, M.P., First Lord of the Treasury, 10, Downing-street, London S.W. Sir,—We, the undersigned, being thA householders of the Northwich Division of Cheshire (Runcorn District) resoectfullv submit to your consideration the fact that the Education Bill, which you have in chargl in Parlia- ment, contains proposals objeciliona and offensive to a very large portion of the public. We would also re- mind you that the country had never asked the Govern- ment to bring forward such a moasure. There is no evidence that pablic opinion approves (1) Of the pro- posed destruction of School Boards (2) Of the remova from the people of the power to elct the members_of tbe Education Authority, both men and women, by a direct vote, or (3) Of the levying of local rates far the support of denominational schools not under complete public control. There are many indications in by-elr.c Vous, public meetings, tha Press, and othersvl;-, that the country is opposed to the said Bill. We therefora ask that you will either withdraw the Bill, or dissolve Parliament and appeal to the country on the question. ' Last Thursday evening a resolution pretecing against the Education Bill was unanimously passed by a large audience in the Brunswick schoolroom. The Rev W Wells occupied the chair. The resolution was proposed by A R Norman, who in the course of an instructive address dealt with the Education Sill from his stand- point as a Cheshire County! Councillor. The County Council were by no means unanimously in favour of the Bill, and had suggested several practical amendments to the proper authorities. Mr Frank Littler was the seconder, and Mr R H Posnett and F J Norman, JP, supported.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-10-17_WidnesExaminer_art0125" n="art0125">
        <head>Trades and Labour Council and the Bill. •</head>
        <p>Trades and Labour Council and the Bill. •</p>
        <p>Mr. C. E. Parker's Suggestion.</p>
        <p>At the annual meeting of tbe Warrington Trades and Labour Council on Tuesday evening, the secretary read 1 a letter from Mr CE Parker,stating that be noticed that in several towns organizations which opposed the Education Bill were uniting for the purpose of demon- strating against it. Last week he attended a demonstra- tion in Southport organized by the following associa- tions :---Bouthport Liberal Association, Free Church Council, Trades and Labour Council, and Women's Liberal Association. The demonstration was most successful and he would propose that they organised a meeting en these lines in Warrington. The Liberals had engaged Parr Hall for October , 28th and had secured the services of Mr H Broadhurst, M.P. If the organizations mentioned were willing to act conjointly he thought the Liberals would give up the idea of a political meeting pure and simple. Of course it would be distinctly understood that they united only for that specific purpose. Neither the Free Church Council nor the Trades Council would desire to be associated with any 'political party except for a specific purpose and for objects that were common to both for the time being. The meeting would be described as a " demonstration against the Education Bill " and would be neither political nor labour. There was very much need of an association like the Trades Council speaking out on the question. The members of the Council were directly in touch with the parents whose children the Bill affected. and their view was being smothered by a cloud of re- ligions controversy. MrlFArtencee asked what the attitude of the Teachers' Union:on the question was. If tha Teachers' Union was in favour of, or against the Bill the members of the Trades Council as Trade Unionists ought to be guided by it rather than by men who looked at the Bill simply from a sectarian point of view. The SECRETARY said that ha all fairness the Council could not go beyond the decisions which they had already arrived at in regard to the Bill. They had de- clared their objections to it on several cccasious and he thought they could only carry out those declarations. They could not very well te.gin to change now because the Bill wasen the same position as when it wasintroduced Theenembers of the Teachers' Union did not object to the Bill as a Bill but they objected to certain parts of the Bill and they desired to have various amendments made. IHe believed most of the members of the Union had looked at the Bill from a personal point of view. They I had seen how voluntary schools had been crippled, and in consequence the teachers in these schools had not been able to obtain the same rata of wages as the teachers who taught in the Board Scheele. He thought they had looked at the Bill from a selfish point of view. They thought iti possible that if the voluntary scholls were going to bo assisted by the rates, there was a chance of the teachers' salaries being increased and that was a very good reason why they should support the 13i11. Mr Pouraoaa said the Council protested against the education authority being any other than a representa- tive and a popularly elected body. They objected to money ' being paid unless they had full control. He thought personally that that was the " tad-rock " that working people should go upon. They should not allow their money to be spent by anybody but themselves or their accredited represzntatives. If teey once allowed the least bit of edging off from that pi iociple they would get back to the position that they were in many years ago and have people taking the fruits of their taxes and rates and spending them at their own sweet will. If the Council could carry out the protest which they had already placed on record against the measure, or against certain portions of the measure, he thought they ought to do so. They did not, however, want to be dragged in with Churchmen or Dissenters in regard to the Bill. He was of the opinion that the Bill was an effort to bring about a re-action against popular education, and that there was somebody behind the Bill who was dead against the advancement of the working classes. He believed they were going to try and smash the only source from which they could hope—the children. If the Council could protest without allying themselves with any pelitical party or creed they ought to protest against the Bill. Mr PLINSTON said they ought to give Mr Parker credit for being sincere. He was a straightforward, honest gentlernan,and he (Mr Plinston) believed he said exactly what he meant. With regard to the Bill, they were told that clause seven would be adhered toe:He contended that the provisions of this clause with regard to representation were unfair to the ratepayers. The Bill was most unjust and a Bill which trade Unionists ought to condemn on every possible occasion. To his mind, as this might be the only chance they would have of condemning the Bill in a large public meeting, they ought to consider very seriously whether to join in the demonstration, and not too flippantly throw the suggestion on one side. The CHAIRMAN was of opinion that the Council might give the meeting their "moral support," but he did not think they could do anything further. . Mr. YORKE said the Bill was practicelly passed and be did not think a demonstration would do any good. Several members expressed the opinion that the Coen. cil should not take part in the meeting because it had been arranged by the Liberal party. After some dis- cussion</p>
        <p>to The FECRETARY moved that the Council take no part in the demonstration. He thought the Liberals bad made a great mistake in not consulting the Council before arranging the meeting. Mr BUCKLEY seconded. He was of opinion that the suggested demonstration was a " political dodge." The Liberal party had one idea against the Bill and that was the defeat of the present Government, and the Free Church Council locked at it from a sectarian point of view. Neither of the two parties had considered the education of the children. The Trades Council looked at tbe question in a different light altogether. They had objected to the Bill upon the ground of representation, but be saw no reason why Hoag should join in the demonstration—if it was • demonstration, but he was greatly of the opinion that it was • political dodge. A great many people thought there was an election very close at hand, and they (the Council) knew perfectly well that the Liberals bad all their work cut out in Warrington to get support for the neat election. It was his opinion that they would use the meeting for that purpose, and be thought the Council would be wise in keeping away from it. The resolution was unanimously carried.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-10-17_WidnesExaminer_art0126" n="art0126">
        <head>Warrington Churchpeople Support the Bill.</head>
        <p>Warrington Churchpeople Support the Bill.</p>
        <p>Speeches by Mr John White and Mr. J. J. Cockshott.</p>
        <p>On Wednesday evening a meeting ot Warrington Churclipeople in support of the Government Education Bill was held in the Co-operative Hall, Sankey-street. Mr John White, J.P., presided and was supported on the platform by the Revs Canon Willis Morley Stevenson, and H A Lester, and Messrs J J dockshott, of Southport, C Sawkine Burgess, and H N Houghton. There was a fairly large attendance, which included a number of local clergy. . .</p>
        <p>The CHAIRM&amp;N thought they would agree that it was quite right that the Government, when the great ques- tion of the war was satisfactorily settled, should turn their attention to the most important matter of social legislation—and tbero could be no donbt about it that the most important matter in social legislation was the question of education. (Hear, hear.) When the Govern- ment introduced their Bill in the spring it was received in the House of Commons with very general approval from all parties. For a considerable length of time there was very little said against the bill. But as time went on and the political agitator emerged from that obscurity to which he had been relegated by the course of events he began to see that here again was a fine opportunity for the display of his courage and the attack on the bill in all its parts was commenced with the most exaggerated misrepresentation which any bill had experienced, he should think, since Parliament</p>
        <p>was a Parliament. He believed that the attack on the Education Bill Was nothing more nor less than the old attack on the Church of Eagland—(hear, hear)—and the outcome of a desire either to destroy or incapacitate voluntary schools. He thought all in that room wovild be convinced that whilst they were willing, as far as possible, for facilities to be given for minist -rs of every denomination to teach chiloten in their own particular faith, they were determined as Church people to maiu. tam n in their schools at whatever cost, religious educa- tion. (Hear, hear). They would not hand over those schools to anybody arid everybody. They were quite willing, if any practical scheme could be found,to allow children belonging to Nonconformist boiies to be educated in the faith of their people, on condition that the Church of England clergy had the power to enter Board schools and teach the children of Church of Eng- and parents their religious beliefs. (Hear, hear). There was one other point which was continually being thrust upon them and that was that Nonconformists were being made to par for the religious education of their people. This was an entirely false idea, and as Mr Balfour said, the people who made these statements must be either wilfully stating what was not true, or they could never have read the text of the Bill. It was quite true they would have to pay through the rates for the support of the voluntary schoole in the same way that they paid through the;taxes now,and there really was no difference to a man's censoicnce whether he paid by of way taxee or by way of rates. It was an infinitesimal amount that Nonconformists would have te pay in this way—a more drop in the ocean—when compared with the large SUMS. Church people had been subscribing all these years towards Board schools, the religious education in which they objected to entirely, or which was of such a dee- criotion as was not worth caLieg religious education at all. There could be no possib'e grievance of Nonco eform- ists in this respeca lie was very unwilling to make any remarks which might be considered as personal, but he did not think he would be worthy of the position he occupied if he allowed to pass unnoticed a statement which Mr Frederick Monks made. Speaking at a Uai- tarian; conference, Mr Monks gave utterance to a vary short passage wnich to his (the Chairman's) mind was not worthy of Mr Monks. He had known Mr Menke for over 40 years and he wee s man whom heeled always believed to be most liberal-minded and kind-hearted. Mr Monks did not belong, be was glad to think, to the political Nonconformist section but Co the conscientious Nonconformists and as such his remarks deserved con- sideration. If the remark had been made by a political Nonconformist he (the Chairman) should not have tholieht it worth while to notice it. The remark attributed to Mr Monks was "Priests of one denomina- tion or another had always been the foes of education.' He (the chairman) supposed that within the polity of the Nonconformist bodies such a thing as a priest did cot occur. Therefore Mr Monks must have been allud- ing to the Roman Catholics or the Church of Eagland. He (the chairman) held no brief for the Roman Catholics and te, did not suppose they would in any way like to have him as their champion. Anyone who lead seen the Roman Catholic schoces in Warrington where the Roman Catholics were educating the very poorest chit iren in the town would give them credit for looking after the education of the young. (Hear, hear.) When he knew the Church of England was at present educating more than half of the children of this country, when he knew that before Board Sohoole were established 30 years ago there was no other system of education exeept the Voluntary Schools the Roman Catholic and Church of England schools— and when he recollected that long before Non- conformity existed the Church was then educating the children Of this country, it was a most untrue, unjust and most uncharitable thing to repeat a statement which alas was only made too often but which was nevertheless a most unkind remark to make. (Hear, hear.) He hoped the Government would make no fu:teer concessions in this mater. (Hear, hoar.) The time for concession and compromise had gone by. Dr Clifford who WAS the leader of the opposition had himself said that no com- promise would satisfy them and that they would have nothing but the bill destroyed. With that statement in view it WAS impossible to make any concessions that would please them. As far as the Church of England was concerned he was tired of making concessiene. They gained nothing by it. The more that were made, the more were demanded. The time had arrived when as Ceurele people they must be up and doing and bring- ing to bear on the Govcrement such a measure of sup- port and pressure that would enable them to bring this matter to a satisfactory corclusion. (applause). Mr J J CCKillsriOTT moved the (owing resolution That in the opinion of this meeting the Government Education Bill should be passed into law without material alt,ration." He pointed out that last year nearly £17,500,000 of public money Alas speat on ele- mentary education in this country. Of that sum, two and a half millions went for the building and equipment of new Board schools, and as that money would go back again in the accounts by way of interest and sinking fund, he thought it was right that they should knock off that two and a half millions and say that in round figures fourteen and a half or fifteen millions were spent upon elementary education. The proolena which the Government had to f a c 3 was that of that fouttaen and a half millons, ton millions went to the children in the Board schools and four and a half millions to the children in the Voluntary schools. In the Board schools' there were 2,239,374 children whilst in the voluntary schools there were 2,492,536. Therefore there were a quarter of a million more children in the Voluntary schools than in the Board schools, and whilst the Board schools received ten millions of money the Voluntary schools received only four and a half millions. Was teat fair to the children? Was it not unfair to the teachers and to the country to which the education of the children was of such vast importance ? Tnerefore the Government said what any sensible man would say " We must do f something to equalise this expenditure." Did they think the Government could do anything else with an educational problem like that than to say " Our duty is to see that the two and a half minims of chil- dren in Voluntary Schools do not have their education stunted and rendered less useful to them than it ought to be for want of funds." People talked about endowing Church schools. Where did that fifteen millions of money come from ? Were not the supperters of voluntary schools, the Churchmen and the Rnman Catholics, at least one half of the people of the country. Did not they pay at least one half of the taxes ? Why should they have to pay one half of the ten millions given to schools of which they did not approve, and receive only four and a half millions for the Voluntary schools ? Although the Government were going by the Bill to improve the cenlition and the posi- tion of children in voluntary schools, they were not going to give the voluntary schools equality. When Mr Gladstone passed the Act of 1870 he said " Oae diatinc- tion there will be in these schools. It is a distinction not in favour of the church Mit rather bearing upon her, I will not say with hardness, because I trust and believe it will not be so, but with something like in- equality, something less than equality on this occasion she has been content to accept." Could anybody tell him that when Mr Gladstone uttered those words and said that churchmen had been content to accept som - thing lees than equality,he meant the difference b ,tween ten millions on one side and four and a half on the other ? That was not Mr Gladstone' meaning. That was not the meaning of the promoters of the Act of 1870 but the enormous increase in the cost of eoucation since 1870 had increased the inequality till it had created what they now called the intolerable strain,which the Bill was intended to remove. They would not get equality even when the Bill was passed, because in order that they might have the right to teach their own children their own faith in their own echoer's, they had to pro- vide the schools for nothing, and they load to keep them in repair for nothing. The speaker went on to speak of the expense waich would have to be incurred in building new schools and providing for repairs, and asked his hearers if they thought Non- conformists would submit to a . grievance like that. Toey (his hearers) knew they would not. and they who moved among the Nonconformists as their friends should plead with them that in this matter they should really look at what was in the interests of the nation and of the children, and give fair play to the taxpayer who was a Churchman or a Roman Catholie as well as to the taxpayer who was s Nonconformist. It was stated that the Bill would abolish Board schools, The Bill would abolish school Boards but it would not abolish Board schools. The children in the schools wouli not know when the school Board was abolished and the local education authority took its place. Everything would go on just the same and the education of the children would not be diminished one iota. Dealing with the objection that taxation and representation did not go together he said that taxes could not be levied in the House of Commons without their memter having a say in the matter; neither could a peony of rates be laid without every member of the local authority having a say in the =titer. The ides of taxation and representation not going together was fallacious and was used by people who had not thought the matter out. Alluding to the question of the control of the Voluntary Schools, the speaker Said that the local authority would have absolute and complete control over the secular educa- tion in the schools. They would not have absolute and corn pieta control over the religious education or the school building, because they were net going to pay for them. Another objection was that there were 8,000 parishes it which the only school was a church school. What did that prove?.—that the only people who had built schools in those parishes were church people. Referring to the statement that Nonconformist pupil teachers would not be rneployed in Church schools, he asked if it would not be a greater grievance that thou- sendelland hundreds of thousands of Church children in Church schools should be deprived of tbe opportunity of being taught the religion of the Church of England. than that every now and then a Nonconformist teacher I should not be appointed in tbe Church schools. If Non- conformists desired undenominationalism, by all means let them have it. but Church people preferred the faith of the Church of England, All they asked was that the fifteen millions of money—that great fund to which they contributed, and to which they were compelled to con- tribute—Should tot be administered in such a way with regard to secular education that they would be deprived of a privilege which they greatly valued, and which they ' and their predeeeSsors had Spent milllonS Of Money to</p>
        <p>maintain—the privilege of teaching their own faith to their own children in their ovn schools. (Applause). Mr J H SMETHURST seconded. He thought that the Bill, as tar as Courchmen were concerned, dirl little en?ugh. If there was any grumbling to be done Church- men had a right to grumble. He did not think their opposition Was AO much a fight against the Church of England or denominational—it was more little England- ism. There was the very same opposition to the Bill as there was to the recent war, and anything that was for the progress of the country would be objected to 1-5, these people. If the ceuntry was to continue to prosper education must be improved. The Church had done its best in the past,—better than any other denomination— and he contended thit the other party had not clone their duty locally. Daring the cast few years everyone knew the difficulty that the committee, with the Rector as its head, had had to provide the accommoda- tion wanted in Warrinet in for toe education of toe children. Mr Monks was very keen on a School Board, but what had the Unitarian body in Watrington done as a body to educate their children ? He maintained they had done nothing. They had schools big enough to put in as national schools and within a hundred yards from that hall they had these schools practically doing nothing during the week. The resolution was carried unanimously, and c)pies will be forwarded to tho Borough and County memitiers and to the Premier.</p>
        <p>In proposing a vote of thanks to the Chairman and to Mr Cockshott, the RECT)a said the clergy were not wholly enamoured of toe Bill. If they know the feelings of the clergy as well as he did, they would find that many wnre strongly opposed to it, but they thought it wa, the best Bill they could get, and intended to sup- port it. He Pranced to fay emphasis on three points,and the first was the advantage toe Bill was to all. fineer the Bill for the first tirne there would be one central authority and co-ordination, and no overlapping. Secondly there would he entire control in all things secular. These were two points of immense advantage to ail. Referring to the advantage to church people the Rector said there would be the relief frem the burden of payment of the s'aff. There would also no the power to Leach their own children their own faith and they would have the right t) appoint the teachers. There was a disadvantage and that Witi what made them view the Bill with anything hut strong favour. They would be saddled with • the expenses of the maintenance of the fabric: a-d that might be A wry serious expense indeed. When people were rated it would be much more dilfilult to get dab- scriptions than it was at pie emu% Subscription., how ever, would only be coded occasionally. The Rev MOBLEY STEVENSON seconded, &amp;rad the motion was heartily agreed to. In respending, the Cum RAIAN said he thought it was the duty of church people to support the Bill and he hoped the measure would pass into law.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-10-17_WidnesExaminer_art0127" n="art0127">
        <head>meeting then concluded. Mr. Balfour at Manchester.</head>
        <p>The meeting then concluded. Mr. Balfour at Manchester.</p>
        <p>Mr Balfour a irireosed a great me.eting, in the St. James's Hall, Mencooscer, so ruesday ingot. His speech was a defence of the Education Bill, and he said tat the Government are determined to precsod with the measure They had Cottle CO the determination because our present system of edueation is chaotic and has left us tar behind America and some of the Continental countries. The bill would pUG secondary education on a sound basis, and it would vastly improve our elemen- tary system. Mr Half )ur made light of much of the criticism of the bill, and said it was of a violently Political partisan character. The intention of the bill had been mistaken, for it would not divorce taxation from representation. Ho had great respect for the eon - scientious objections of Nonconformists, but the expressed intention of eons of them to refuse to pay rates if the bill became law was wrong in principle. The meeting agreed with a resolution which expressed un- abated confidence in the general policy of the Govern- met and more particularly an approval of the Educa- tion Bill.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-10-17_WidnesExaminer_art0128" n="art0128">
        <head>Another Tory M.P. Revolts.</head>
        <p>Another Tory M.P. Revolts.</p>
        <p>The Marquis of Hamilton, Conservative member for Londonderry, has announced to the Ministerial Whip that he feels unable further co support the Government in regard to the Education Bill unless some compromise can be arrived at which would largely increase the popu- lar control of Voluntary schools.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-10-17_WidnesExaminer_art0129" n="art0129">
        <head>Strong Protests by Gongregationalists.</head>
        <p>Strong Protests by Gongregationalists.</p>
        <p>Suggested Solution to the Problem.</p>
        <p>The Liverpool District of the Lancashire Congrega- tional Union held its autumnal meeting at Ga.rston on Tuesday, Mr W Crosfield, LP., presided over a repre- sentative attendance of delegates including the Rev John Yonge, of Warringtm (lion secretary). The prin- cipal business was the discussion of the education bill. The Rev Dr Karfoot, Leigh, said the crisis had a snecial relationship to Congregationalists,who tradition- ally stood for the enlightenment and freedom of the people. Having criticised the provisions ot the bill, he said compromise was spoken of, but they must beware Lew; they sell their birstkright for a mess of pottage. Little good could come of tinkering with the bill. The soon,?r it was killed and de.lently buried the better. They must have a new start, and secure a Scheme on the basis, not of clericalism, but education. A national system—the separation of the sesular from the religious —was the logical oae, and should be adopted. Toe Chairman, referring to a suggestion that the existing voluntary schools should be purchased, sail thqt some in the large towns were nct worth purchasing at any price. It was proposed to hand over the control of the question to the councils, and be ventured to say that the City Council was utterly incompetent to under- take the detail work necessary He was quite prepared to resist the bill if passed, and he hoped they would all have the courage of their fathers, and maintain the fight. (Applause). On the motion of Mr Karfoot, seconded by &amp;Tr Sykes (Wigan), a resolution was unanimously passed p °test- ing against the bill and callinc, upon the Gsvernment to withdraw it or t appeal to the constituencies. _ _ . . _ _ . .</p>
        <p>The Rev John Chafer (Southport) contended that they should resist the bill to the uttermost, and prop )sed " That this meeting, in view of the possibility of the education bill becoming law, expresses Its opinion that the refusal to pay the rate would be a justifiable torm if prot,st, and sympathises with those who feel compelled to adopt that course." _</p>
        <p>_ This was seconded by Mr Dawson, Skelmersdale. The Rev F W Clarke (Si Elclens) thought the position should be left open, and suggested that the wording should be that in the event of the bill b coming law it would compel Nonconformists to face the question whether they rould consistently with loyalty to con- science pay the education rate. This was put as an amendment, and seconded by the Rev J H Riddette (Raydock), but the original motion was carried by a large majority, only three voting against. the Rcv F W Clarke proposed, the Rv Adam Scott (Southport) seconded, and it was carried unanimously. " That so far as the solution of the education problem before the county may require the equitanle purchase or rent by the public from the denominations of their school buildings for the purpose of national eluoation, this meeting would approve of the acquisition of such as would meet present day requirements." A discussion also rook place on the question of con- current religious instruction, and the 4ev J H Riddette moved "That provided complete control of all schools is given to the representatives of the ratepa7ers every facility should be accorded to parents for the instruction of children in their own religious views by their own ministers," but the motion was defeated. This concluded the business, and an invitation to hold the next meeting at Formby was accepted.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-10-17_WidnesExaminer_art0130" n="art0130">
        <head>ST. HELENS.</head>
        <p>ST. HELENS.</p>
        <p>MARKET INSPECTOR FOR ST. HELENS.</p>
        <p>At the meeting of the St Helens Markets Committee, on Wednesday, Police-constable J Peters was appointed market inspector in succession to Police-constable Evans, who was successful in gaining an appointment at Old- ham.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-10-17_WidnesExaminer_art0131" n="art0131">
        <head>ST. HELENS SOT'S HEROISM.</head>
        <p>A ST. HELENS SOT'S HEROISM.</p>
        <p>On Tuesday Mr. S. Brighouse, county coroner, pre- sented Herbert Pierce, aged eleven, of 30, Lowe-street, with the testimonial of the Royal Humane Society for conspicuous bravery in saving the life of a boy named James Stanchion, who was in imminent danger of drowning in a clay pit. The Coroner said two boys, namrd Featherstone and James Stanchion, were playing on a raft in a pit of water when they fell in. Pierce, who was walking past at the time, walked into the water up to his armpits anti rescued Szanchion, but Featherstone was drowned. The little fellow seemed to be highly pleased to receive the testimonial.</p>
        <p>EVIDENCE, NOT 13EARSAY.—A careful investigation has been raid() by a trustworthy reporter into a curs in Manchester, ascribed to the use of Dr Williams' pink pills for pale people. The whole affair has been sifted out, and en interview with the person concerned ob- tained, with the result that the case is found an astonishing one. The evidence collected by the reporter isi submitted in another column. From what we can !earn, most people in Lancashire swear by Dr William's pink pills, and it i not to be wondered at, in view of the hundreds of cures they have effected in cures of paralysis, locomotor • ataxy, rheumatism, and sciatica ; also all diseases arising from Impoverishment of the hlood, scrofula, riallets, chronic erysipelas,consumption of the bowels and lungs, aosemia, pale and sallow complexion, general muscular weakness, loss of appetite, palpitations, pains in the back, nervous headache, and neuralgia, early decay, all forms of female weakness and hysteria. These pills are a tsnic, not a purgative. They are genuine only with the full name Dr Williams' pink pills for pale people. and are sold by chemists and by Dr Williams' Medicine Company, 46, Holborn-viaduct, London, E.C., at 2s 9d a box, or six boxes for 13s 9d. Pink pills sold loose or from glass jars are not Dr Williams' pink pills ; accept them only in the pink closed wrapper as above de- scribed.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-10-17_WidnesExaminer_art0132" n="art0132">
        <head>LOCAL CHURCHES.</head>
        <p>LOCAL CHURCHES.</p>
        <p>CONGREGATIONAL SCHOOL, NEWTON,</p>
        <p>What was termed a harvest social was b—eli in the Congregational schoolroom Nowtonon Wednesday even- ing. There were about 120 people present. Tables con- taining fruit &amp;c., were laid in different parts of the room the ladies' committee superintending the arrangements. Songs were rendered at intervals by Miss Jessie &amp;inward, Nii9S Grace Blthell, Messrs J White and C Pritchard. Refreshments consisting of fruit Sie. were handed round and a potato pie supper was after wards partaken of. At the close there was a sale of fruit in aid of the scho4l Nude.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-10-17_WidnesExaminer_art0133" n="art0133">
        <head>ST. PECER'S CHURCH, NEWTON.</head>
        <p>ST. PECER'S CHURCH, NEWTON.</p>
        <p>Harvest thanksgiving services were held in the above church on Sunday. file interior of the church was prettily, though not profuarly, decorated with choice flowers, fruit and cereals. At each of the services 'here were large congregations, the morning preacher being the Rev E Wolfe,RA.,Vicar of Si. Mark's, Bolton, wad in 1 the evening the pulpit was occupied by the Vicar, the Rev J a Ryder. In the afternoon an organ recital was given by the organist, Mr E iwin Leyland, with muaical selections by the choir. The following programme was submitted :—Variations on a theme in A flat Major ; vocal solo, " Resignation," Mr H F Houghton, Inter- zz )-organ ; anthem, " Praise the Lord," choir; violin solo with organ accompaniment, " La Serenata," Mr L Thorp P ; mPditation, organ; vocal solo, " Honour and Arms," Mr 9 Dring; Harvest Festival March,organ ; con- cluding voluntary, " Trumpet March." Tho collections were in aid of the church funds.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-10-17_WidnesExaminer_art0134" n="art0134">
        <head>MERSEY MISSION TO SEAMEN, RUNCORN,</head>
        <p>MERSEY MISSION TO SEAMEN, RUNCORN,</p>
        <p>The Harvest Thanks ;lying Services in connection with the above Mission were held on Thursday and Sunday. The church was m)st tastefully decorated by a willing band of workers, several designs of wheat and flowers eapecialle the anchors of fl 'were and the flower baskets suspended Nan the b" roe decorated with flowers looked very wed. 'rue itch. e..,d flowers were given by Mr J Elopes Rook Savage and Miss Hayes Frod- sham. A splendid lot of flo were w4icti decorated the tables at the laying of the foundation stones at the Man- , chaster Sanatorium was given to Mr Shaw by Mr and Mr 3 Crossby, Mrs Evans, Mrs Lawton, Mrs Mack. Mr - Matthews,Mrs Norton, Kinesley. Mr W Byfleld, Mr A Riley, and octiers. the service on Thursday considering , the wet night was well attended and an egcellent sermon was preached by the Rev. D W Hobson MA. On Sunday morning there Was a moderate attendance the preacher being the Rev T aiver, B.A. In the after- noon a Children's Service was held the preacher being Mr J W Gibson. The children numbering 300 behaved remarkably well and on leaving the Mission were pre- sented with fruit, kindly given by Mr J Stokes. At the evening service the church was croeeled to excess, scores of people being unable to gain admission. The choir were assisted by Mr C Tonk's string band and Mr Onthwaite assisted with his euphonium. Mr R Shaw presided at the organ. The anthem entitled " Sing to the Lord of ilarvest " was well rendered,the solo being , taken by Mr Shaw. The singing throughout Was very heartily rendered. Mr J Stair )s preached an excellent sermon from the text Gen. Ist 29 and 30, which was much appreciated. the collections were better than last year.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-10-17_WidnesExaminer_art0135" n="art0135">
        <head>HILL CLIFFE CHAPEL.</head>
        <p>HILL CLIFFE CHAPEL.</p>
        <p>Harvest thanksgiving servizres were held in Hill Cliffe Chapel on Sunday last when the chapel was beautifully and lavishly decorated for the occasion. The internal arrangements of the chapel lend thernselvos well for dis- play and full advantage was taken of this.Tbe pulpit was moat tastefully and richly decorated with corn andfiowers presenting a fine sight, aad was done by Miss Hailas and Mrs Reeves who devoted much time to the work. The Communion, choir stalls, windows and pews were also decorated, this being done by Messrs Joseph Moss, J Hallas, Gr Mosi, S Moss, J MOB 9, J J Woerall, together with Mrs Hughes, Mrs Moss, Mrs Lianas. Miss Hall, Miss Worrall, Miss Dodd, Mrs Jones, and Miss Jones, Miss , Berry, Miss Mitchell, Miss Pemberton, Miss England land oth-rs. The service on Sunday morning was fairly attended, the Pastor, Rev J S Hughes, preached on " The Doctrine of the harvest." In the afternoon there was a very good attendance, when the choir rendered a service of song entitled " Reaping Time." It 97A4 a musical sod sweet service of song,and well given by the choir. fhe reader was the Rev J S I Hughes. In the evening, the chapel was filled to over- ' flawing and whilst the congregation was gathering the choir sang several pieces. The pastor preached on the " Delights of Harvest " dwellag on the beauty, the fra- grance, the abundance, and the joys of harvest. The choir sang the anthems "He watereth the hills," " From the rising of the sun," and " Wnere are the reapers "in excellent style. Mr W Hallas manipulated the organ in his usually efficient manner,pltying during the day the folowing voluntaries :—Simper's opening voluntary in A, meditative in F, grand chorus " Thou crownest the year," offertnire in G, communion in A flat, Mozart's "Gloria." "Harvest March" and others. A fruit social was held on Monday night in the schoolreom when in spite of the pouring rain, about a hundred friends met to enjoy a few games &amp;e. given by Master Stanley Jones and sarnan-e-G J Worrall, whilst Mr George WhitfiAd contributed a humorous recitation. Selections were also given on the gramophone kindly lent by Mr James Strange Refreshments and fruit were served and the remaining fruit, vegetables and flowers were sold. The total pro- ceeds of harvest festival exceeded £lO. SANKEY BRIDGES WESLEYAN CHAPEL.</p>
        <p>'The chapel anniversary services in connection with this place of worship were held on Sunday. The preacher for tne occasion was the Rev Alfred Freeman and his dl3oourBes were greatly appreciated. in the afternoon a service of song entitled "Farmer Wharton's Vow" was given by the choir,under the conductorship of Mr Dunn. The proceedings were presided over by Mr J Within shaw, of Peuketh, and Mrs Witbinshaw read the con- nective readings. Solos were creditably sung by Mis- M Dunn, Nit E Walker and Mr J Fodea, and-a duet was effectively given by the Misses C ani B Jackson. Ms, Rlckers presided at the organ. Tne collection, were on behalf of the Trust Fund.</p>
        <p>Mr. Charles Collette's Visit to Warrington. An exceptional treat will be afforded the play- going public of Warrington on Thursday afternoon next, when Charles Collette the ancient comedian so long famous in "The Colonel " and " What -happened to Jones" pays a flying visit with his company to the Royal Court Theatre and will appear as Adonis Evergreen in " My Awful Dad." Of his performance of this character in Loudon a prominent critic says :—" The Gaiety Theatre was crowded yesterday with an audience eager to wel- come the return of Charles Collette—an old favour- ite in this house—as Adonis Evergreen in "My Awful Dad." To challenge comparision with Charles Idatthews in the same piece and on the same stage was a daring venture, and it is only fair to the younger actor to record that he 'came through the ordeal with complete success. The verdict was unanimous, enthusiastic and favourable,and Charles Collette may be congratulated on having added another to his long list of triumphs." Good lemplars Convention. The usual monthly session of No. 1 Convention of S.W. Lancashire Good Templars was held on Satur- day in the Wesleyan Schoolroom, Hiudley. Bro T Parr, CC T, of Leigh, presided over a good attend- ancejof officers and representatives from various lodges and temples in the area. Bro W Pilkington, V D, of Blackpool, was very heartily welcomed and gave an interesting address, expressing his strong eff,Ttion for the Convention, of which he was still a member, and concluded with an earnest appeal for more earnest and continuous activity in their Good Tomplary work ; and thorough consistency in all their aztions. The following officers tendered their reports of lodges under their care, and which they had visited during the quarter :—Bro T Parr. CC T, George Clarke, CO, Sister Mrs Woods, C S T T, and Mrs Hatton, CV T, Bro P Smith C S. Bro T Speakman, CT, ana Bro P Hulme, C M. The reports showed the lodges generally to be working well and adding to their numbers, parti- cularly, Warrington, Earlesto wn, Hindley, Ty ldesley and Leigh. One or two places needing attention were referred to the Executive for further con- sideration and action. The next monthly session is to be held at Tyldesley on No 7ember Bth. Thanks to visitors and to Hindley friends for their arrange- ments closed a very interesting session. Tea followed and a public meeting was afterwards held. presided over by Bro T E Yates, C.T. Earnest addresses were given by Bro G Clarke PD ES, of Earlestown and Bro John Parr CCT of Leigh. An excellent programme of songs, recitations &amp;c., was very kindly rendered by variotis members and friends.</p>
        <p>Songs were I 1414i.U.a., • Moss and J I :7- CH°</p>
