        <p>Mrs. Beamont's Wilt. Bequests to Charities and Churches. A Trust for the Needy. By her will dated 22nd December 1900, the late Mrs Letitia Beamont of Orford Hall, Warrington, after bequeathing various legacies to relatives, friends and servants also made the following be- quests :—To the Warrington Infirmary and Dis- pensary two thousand pounds; to the Royal National Life Boat Institution one thousand pounds • to the Warrington Blue Coat School one th'ousand pounds ; to the Solicitors' Benevolent Association one thousand pounds; to the Vicar of Padgate two hundred pounds to be applied for the benefit of the school built by the late Mr Beamont lat Orford; to the Rector of Warrington two hun- dred pounds for the benefit of the Parish Church 'schools; to the Vicar of Holy Trinity, Warrington two hundred pounds for the benefit of the Holy Trinity Schools ; to the Vicar of Holy Trinity, two Ihundred pounds for the benefit of St. Luke's Schools ; to the Vicar of St. Paul's, Warrington, two hundred pounds for the benefit of the St. Paul's Schools ; to the Vicar of St. Ann's, Warrington, two hundred pounds for the benefit of the St. Ann's 'Schools ; to the Vicar of St. Peter's. Warrington, two hundred pounds for the benefit of the St. IPeter's Schools. The residuary estate of the testa- I trix (the amount of which it is at present impos- sible to estimate) is bequeathed in the following terms " Upon trust to apply the income thereof in 'the payment of annual pensions of not less than twenty-six rounds or more than fifty-two pounds each, to well conducted and deserving persons of both sexes who may be in reduced or decayed cir- cumstances who are popularly described as "having known better days" ono have been born or resident in the Borough el Warrington in the County of Lancaster or within three miles therefrom during their lives, or for such less periods as may be thought desirable by the persons to whom the power of selection of the recipients of such pensions is hereinafter given, the number of persons receiving such pensions to be as nearly as is practicable an equal number of both sexes. But I declare that it is not my intention by the trust hereby created to give mere pauper relief or to assist persons in humble life who can be much better relieved under the prevailing poor laws for the time being, and I further declare that in ease in any year the selec- tors shall not find a sufficient number of such per- sons as aforesaid as recipients of the said pensions, to exhaust the income of the said trust moneys the surplus of such income may be applied so far as the same will extend for the benefit of any person or persons wherever born or resident who would have been eligible to receive pensions under the trusts aforesaid if such person or persons had been born or resident in the Borough of Warrington aforesaid or within three miles therefrom." The Executors and Trustees of the Will are Messrs Vere Beamont Davies and John Burgess of Warrington, Sc licitorsi</p>
